\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Various scientific problems in fluid dynamics, structural mechanics, and electromagnetics are governed by partial differential equations (PDEs), which result in a sparse matrix when discretized with finite difference and finite element methods. The boundary element method (BEM) has an advantage over such volume discretization methods, since it only discretizes the boundary and the number of elements can be drastically reduced.
However, BEM requires the solution of a dense linear system, which has cubic complexity if solved directly.
For very large dense matrices, the Cholesky factorization can be computed using distributed memory implementations from SCALAPACK, DPLASMA~\cite{bosilca2011}, SLATE~\cite{gatesSLATEDesignModern2019} or Elemental~\cite{poulson2013}.
Although modern parallel computer architectures offer significant speedups due to the use of multiple threads, the cubic complexity of dense factorization remains prohibitive for large matrices.


