A few remarks are in order. 
\begin{itemize}
	\item The parity relations, which do not appear in the definition of axes-aligned number walls, simply guarantees that our pattern (at least as long as its shape is connected) agrees with a pattern of the form $P^{R,v_0}$ for some pattern $P$ and translation vector $v_0\in\bbz^2$.
	\item The ex relations guarantee that in diagonally-aligned number walls the zeros appear in diagonally-aligned rectangles.
	\item The inner frame relations make sure that the patterns of zeros are diagonally-aligned squares, 
