\noindent\emph{Ex relations} \eqref{item: FR ex}. 
The ex patterns whose underlying shapes are not contained in $\bbn^2$ are clearly satisfied. The rest of them are contained in the image under $\ka_1$ of a $2\times 2$ matrix over $\Sig$. As noted in the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:consistent}, all the square patterns of size $2$ that appear 
in $\sig^\infty(\splus{0})$, must already appear in $\sig^5(\splus{0})$. Checking that $\ka_1(\sig^5(\splus{0}))$ satisfies the ex relation is a finite check that is verified with a computer program. In fact, by the first paragraph of the current proof, this holds since $\frakg_{m,n}=\frakf_{m,n}(\alpha)$ whenever $0\leq m\leq129$ and $1\leq n \leq 130$, and $\frakf(\alpha)$ does satisfy the ex relations. 

