\section{Case Studies}
\label{sec:CaseStudies}

To validate the accuracy and robustness of the proposed quadrature rules, we present a set of representative case studies for solving the Poisson equation using high-order finite elements on both standard and curved geometries. We compare the performance of our optimised quadrature rules against classical Gaussian quadrature in terms of $L^2$ error convergence. In each case, we employ a quadrature rule to integrate exactly the mass matrix, and thus, its gradient when the mappings are affine. Our focus is on serendipity (trunk) spaces with varying polynomial degrees in both 2D and 3D settings.

\subsection{Test Case 1: Regular Geometries}

We begin by considering the classical Poisson problem:
\begin{equation}
    -\Delta u = f \quad \text{in } \Omega,
\end{equation}
where the domain $\Omega$ is the unit square $[0,1]^2$ in two dimensions, and the unit cube $[0,1]^3$ in three dimensions. Homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions are imposed throughout.

