\section{Introduction}

Brain-computer interfaces (BCIs) have emerged as transformative tools, bridging the gap between human cognition and computational systems. By translating neural activity into actionable commands, BCIs hold immense potential for applications ranging from assistive technologies to entertainment and beyond. However, achieving seamless, complex human-computer interaction remains a significant challenge for existing BCI approaches. Current practical BCIs primarily fall into two categories: non-invasive systems, such as those utilizing electroencephalography (EEG) within the visual evoked potential (VEP) paradigm, and invasive systems, which rely on implanted electrodes to capture neural signals directly.

\subsection{Invasive Devices}

Invasive brain-computer interfaces (BCIs) involve surgically implanting electrodes into brain tissue to record high-resolution neural signals, enabling precise control of external devices. This section explores their historical development, operational principles, key technologies, target users, market trends, and challenges.

\textbf{Background}: The concept of invasive BCIs emerged in the late 1990s with experiments demonstrating neural control of robotic devices in primates \cite{chapin1999real}. Advances in electrode technology and neural decoding algorithms in the 2000s enabled human applications, particularly for individuals with severe motor impairments \cite{hochberg2006neuronal}. Companies like Neuralink and Synchron have since advanced the field, moving from research to clinical trials \cite{musk2019neuralink, oxley2016minimally}.

\textbf{Operational Principles}: Invasive BCIs use microelectrode arrays implanted in the cortex to capture action potentials or local field potentials \cite{lebedev2017brain}. Signal processing employs machine learning, such as neural networks, to decode motor intentions with low latency \cite{anumanchipalli2019speech}. Implantation typically requires craniotomy or endovascular delivery, using biocompatible materials like platinum \cite{polikov2005response}. Challenges include tissue scarring and signal degradation over time, which can reduce long-term efficacy \cite{polikov2005response}.

\textbf{Key Technologies}: \textbf{Neuralink} develops the Telepathy N1 implant, with thousands of electrodes to record neural activity at high frequency \cite{musk2019neuralink}. A robotic system inserts ultra-thin electrodes to minimize tissue damage, and early human trials in 2024 enabled cursor control for quadriplegic patients \cite{neuralink2024first}. \textbf{Synchron}’s Stentrode, delivered endovascularly, uses a stent with electrodes to record motor cortex signals, supporting digital device control for ALS patients \cite{oxley2016minimally, oxley2021endovascular}. \textbf{Neuroxess} focuses on high-density microelectrode arrays, achieving single-neuron resolution in preclinical trials \cite{neuroxess2023highdensity}.

\textbf{Target Users}: Invasive BCIs primarily serve individuals with severe motor impairments, such as quadriplegia, ALS, and locked-in syndrome. These systems enable tasks like robotic arm control, cursor navigation, and communication, restoring independence for patients with intact cognitive function \cite{hochberg2012reach, oxley2021endovascular}.

\textbf{Market Trends}: The BCI market, including invasive systems, is growing due to increasing prevalence of neurological disorders and advancements in neurotechnology \cite{grandview2023bci}. North America leads in adoption, driven by robust healthcare infrastructure \cite{grandview2023bci}. High costs and regulatory hurdles, however, limit broader access \cite{synchron2021fda}.

\textbf{Challenges and Future Trends}: Surgical risks, biocompatibility issues, and signal degradation pose challenges \cite{polikov2005response}. Ethical concerns, including neural privacy, also arise \cite{musk2019neuralink}. Future developments include biodegradable electrodes, wireless power systems, and AI-enhanced decoding to improve reliability \cite{lebedev2017brain}. Hybrid systems combining invasive and non-invasive approaches are also emerging \cite{grandview2023bci}.

\textbf{Summary}: Invasive BCIs offer high-fidelity neural control for motor-impaired individuals but face surgical and ethical challenges. Ongoing innovations in materials and algorithms will enhance their clinical impact.

\subsection{Non-Invasive Devices}

Non-invasive BCIs use scalp-based sensors to measure brain activity, offering a safer alternative to invasive systems. This section focuses on Visual Evoked Potential (VEP)-based BCIs, covering their background, principles, key products, users, market trends, and limitations.

\textbf{Background}: Non-invasive BCIs originated in the 1970s with electroencephalography (EEG) for brain signal detection \cite{vidal1973toward}. Steady-state VEP (SSVEP) systems, leveraging visual stimuli to elicit neural responses, became prominent in the 1990s due to their robustness \cite{vialatte2010steady}. These systems have evolved into commercial products for assistive and consumer applications \cite{allison2010toward}.

\textbf{Operational Principles}: SSVEP-BCIs use flickering visual stimuli (5-20 Hz) to generate frequency-locked responses in the occipital cortex, detected via EEG electrodes \cite{vialatte2010steady}. Signal processing, such as Canonical Correlation Analysis, identifies the target frequency with high accuracy \cite{zhu2010design}. Noise from artifacts requires filtering, and multi-channel setups improve resolution but increase complexity \cite{vialatte2010steady}. Dry electrodes simplify use but reduce signal quality \cite{zhu2010design}.

\textbf{Key Products}: \textbf{g.tec}’s Intendix system supports SSVEP-based spelling and navigation for locked-in patients \cite{guger2012intendix}. \textbf{NeuroSky}’s MindWave, an affordable single-channel headset, enables attention-based gaming \cite{neurosky2023mindwave}. \textbf{Emotiv}’s EPOC+ uses multiple channels for SSVEP applications in virtual reality and neurofeedback \cite{emotiv2023epoc}. These products balance cost and functionality for different markets \cite{allison2010toward}.

\textbf{Target Users}: VEP-BCIs benefit patients with ALS, quadriplegia, and locked-in syndrome, enabling communication and device control \cite{wolpaw2002brain}. Stroke survivors also use these systems for assistive tasks, though efficacy varies \cite{zhu2010design}. They are less suitable for visually impaired or photosensitive individuals \cite{vialatte2010steady}.

\textbf{Market Trends}: Non-invasive BCIs dominate the market due to their accessibility, with growth driven by neurological disorder prevalence and consumer demand for gaming and neurofeedback \cite{grandview2023bci}. North America and Europe lead in adoption \cite{grandview2023bci}.

\textbf{Challenges and Future Trends}: Low signal-to-noise ratios and user fatigue limit performance \cite{wolpaw2002brain}. Environmental factors also affect accuracy \cite{vialatte2010steady}. Future trends include hybrid BCIs, improved dry electrodes, and AI-based signal processing to enhance usability \cite{allison2010toward}. Integration with virtual reality could expand applications \cite{grandview2023bci}.

\textbf{Summary}: VEP-BCIs provide accessible solutions for motor-impaired users but are constrained by signal quality and fatigue. Advances in hardware and AI will broaden their impact.

% \subsection{Invasive Devices}

% Invasive brain-computer interfaces (BCIs) involve the surgical implantation of electrodes into brain tissue to capture high-fidelity neural signals, enabling precise control of external devices. This section provides a detailed analysis of invasive BCIs, focusing on their historical background, operational principles, leading companies and products, applicable user groups, market scale, limitations, and future trends.

% \textbf{Background}: The concept of invasive BCIs emerged in the 1990s with early experiments demonstrating direct neural control of robotic devices in primates \cite{chapin1999real}. Advances in microelectrode technology and neural decoding algorithms in the 2000s paved the way for human applications, particularly for individuals with severe motor impairments \cite{hochberg2006neuronal}. Companies like Neuralink, Synchron, and Neuroxess have since commercialized or trialed invasive BCIs, transitioning the field from research to clinical and potential consumer applications \cite{musk2019neuralink}. These systems are distinguished by their ability to record single-neuron activity, offering superior resolution compared to non-invasive alternatives \cite{lebedev2017brain}.

% \textbf{Operational Principles}: Invasive BCIs typically use microelectrode arrays implanted in the cortex to record action potentials (spikes) or local field potentials (LFPs) with amplitudes of 50-500 $\mu$V and bandwidths of 0.1-10 kHz \cite{lebedev2017brain}. Signal processing involves spike sorting to isolate individual neuron activity and machine learning models (e.g., Kalman filters, neural networks) to decode motor intentions, achieving latencies below 10 ms \cite{anumanchipalli2019speech}. Implantation requires craniotomy or endovascular delivery, with electrode materials like platinum or silicon ensuring biocompatibility \cite{polikov2005response}. High-density arrays (100-10,000 channels/cm²) enhance spatial resolution but increase power consumption (50-100 mW) and data processing demands, requiring onboard chips or wireless transmission \cite{musk2019neuralink}. Challenges include tissue damage, glial scarring, and signal degradation over time, reducing electrode efficacy by 30-40\% within 6-12 months \cite{polikov2005response}.

% \textbf{Common Companies and Products}: Several companies lead the invasive BCI market, each with distinct technologies and applications. \textbf{Neuralink}, founded by Elon Musk, develops the \textbf{Telepathy N1} implant, featuring 1,024 electrodes per shank across multiple arrays, capable of recording from thousands of neurons at 20 kHz per channel \cite{musk2019neuralink}. A robotic implantation system inserts ultra-thin electrodes (5-10 $\mu$m diameter) with sub-micron precision, minimizing tissue trauma \cite{musk2019neuralink}. Early human trials in 2024 enabled quadriplegic patients to control cursors with error rates of 1.5 bits/sec and type at 8 words/min \cite{neuralink2024first}. The device, priced at an estimated \$100,000, targets both medical and cognitive enhancement applications \cite{neuralink2024first}. \textbf{Synchron} offers the \textbf{Stentrode}, a minimally invasive BCI delivered endovascularly through the jugular vein, using a nitinol stent with 16 platinum electrodes \cite{oxley2016minimally}. It records motor cortex signals with a 0.1-200 Hz bandwidth and 5 $\mu$V RMS noise floor, achieving 92\% accuracy in digital device control for ALS patients after three months \cite{oxley2021endovascular}. Synchron’s device costs approximately \$50,000 and avoids craniotomy, reducing infection risk to below 1\% \cite{oxley2021endovascular}. \textbf{Neuroxess}, an emerging player, develops high-density microelectrode arrays (10,000 channels/cm²) for cortical recordings, offering single-neuron resolution (SNR >10) \cite{neuroxess2023highdensity}. Preclinical trials in primates demonstrated 85\% accuracy in decoding hand movements, though human trials are pending and costs exceed \$50,000 per array \cite{neuroxess2023highdensity}.

% \textbf{Applicable User Groups}: Invasive BCIs primarily serve individuals with severe motor and communication impairments. \textbf{High-level quadriplegia}, caused by C1-C4 spinal cord injuries (over 50,000 new cases annually in the US), benefits from Neuralink’s cursor control and robotic arm operation, achieving 75\% grasp success \cite{nscisc2023spinal, hochberg2012reach}. \textbf{Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)}, affecting 1 in 50,000, relies on Synchron’s Stentrode for click-based communication, with 90\% accuracy in advanced stages \cite{rowland2001amyotrophic, oxley2021endovascular}. \textbf{Severe post-stroke motor impairments}, impacting 15 million globally each year, use invasive BCIs with functional electrical stimulation (FES) to restore hand dexterity by 15-20\% after six months \cite{who2023stroke, biasiucci2018brain}. \textbf{Locked-in syndrome} patients leverage BCIs for basic interaction, with training completed in 2-4 weeks \cite{hochberg2012reach}. These systems are less suitable for patients with compromised cortical function (e.g., severe traumatic brain injury), as signal quality depends on intact neural pathways \cite{lebedev2017brain}.

% \textbf{Market Scale}: The global BCI market, including invasive systems, was valued at \$1.5 billion in 2022 and is projected to reach \$3.7 billion by 2030, with a compound annual growth rate (CAGR) of 12.3\% \cite{grandview2023bci}. Invasive BCIs account for approximately 40\% of the market, driven by their high precision and clinical applications \cite{grandview2023bci}. The medical segment, targeting quadriplegia, ALS, and stroke, constitutes 70\% of invasive BCI revenue, with North America holding a 45\% market share due to advanced healthcare infrastructure \cite{grandview2023bci}. Key growth drivers include rising neurological disorder prevalence (e.g., 5.5 million ALS patients globally) and increasing investment in neurotechnology, with \$1.2 billion in venture funding for invasive BCI startups in 2022 \cite{rowland2001amyotrophic, grandview2023bci}. However, high costs and regulatory hurdles limit market penetration \cite{grandview2023bci}.

% \textbf{Limitations and Future Trends}: Invasive BCIs face significant challenges, including surgical risks (e.g., 0.5-2\% hemorrhage incidence), biocompatibility issues, and signal degradation due to glial scarring \cite{polikov2005response, oxley2016minimally}. Devices like Neuralink’s require frequent recharging, and Synchron’s Stentrode has limited spatial resolution (1-2 mm) for fine motor tasks \cite{oxley2021endovascular}. Ethical concerns include neural privacy, equitable access (e.g., \$100,000+ costs exclude low-income patients), and potential misuse for cognitive enhancement \cite{musk2019neuralink}. Regulatory delays, such as FDA approval processes, extend commercialization timelines by 2-5 years \cite{synchron2021fda}. Future trends include biodegradable electrode coatings to reduce scarring, wireless power systems to eliminate battery constraints, and AI-driven decoding to improve accuracy to 98\% \cite{lebedev2017brain}. Hybrid systems combining invasive and non-invasive modalities and integration with neuroprosthetics are also emerging \cite{grandview2023bci}.

% \textbf{Summary}: Invasive BCIs offer unmatched signal fidelity, transforming lives for motor-impaired populations. However, surgical risks, high costs, and ethical concerns pose challenges. Innovations in biomaterials, AI, and regulatory pathways will drive their clinical and commercial success.

