In practical applications, machine learning models are typically trained and inferred using programs written in Python. Since Python is an interpreted language, its source code is interpreted line by line. This poses challenges to the efficiency of model inference, especially for SpMV, which has a millisecond-level running time.
% Figure \ref{fig:spmv-nnz} shows the variation of SpMV runtime with the number of non-zero elements in the matrix. For a sparse matrix with 1,000,000 non-zero elements, the time to perform one SpMV operation is approximately 0.01ms~0.1ms. The fastest inference time among the five models trained in this paper is about 1.5ms, which is 15~150 times the SpMV execution time. Even in iterative computation scenarios where the SpMV kernel is repeatedly called multiple times, it can cause a certain degree of performance loss. 
In the method proposed in this paper, excessive model inference overhead may lead to the use of suboptimal SpMV configurations for a long time in iterative algorithms, missing a number of iterations. Given that the models selected in this paper are highly interpretable, they can be converted into compiled language programs with faster execution speeds, thereby enhancing the inference speed. Model-To-Code Generator (m2cgen) \cite{m2cgen} is a lightweight open-source tool used to convert trained ML models into code in various programming languages, including C, Java, Go, and C\#. 
% The SpMV kernel implementations considered in this paper are all written in C/C++ and the CUDA programming model. Therefore, 
Similar to \cite{jpdc/GaoJLWS24,10.1145/3664924}, this paper uses the tool to convert ML models into C language codes, allowing for seamless integration into iterative algorithms. Table \ref{tab:m2cgen-vs-python} shows the inference overhead of using the original Python ML model and the C language code generated by m2cgen in five classification tasks. It can be observed that the C language code achieves a significant speedup on six classifiers, with a maximum of 1,235x and an average of 549x. 
% In short, there are three main reasons for the efficiency improvement brought by using m2cgen: (1) the model is converted into program code for direct execution, eliminating the step of loading model files from the disk; (2) C language, as a compiled language, has a natural advantage in execution efficiency compared with Python; (3) C language programs can seamlessly interface with SpMV kernels written in CUDA, avoiding the additional overhead caused by cross-language calls.

% Additionally, the use of the m2cgen tool offers advantages in model deployment. Models trained automatically with AutoGluon have limitations on Python versions and involve a multitude of external dependencies during use, which may also have version compatibility issues. Deploying models on target computers requires special attention to the coupling relationships of various dependent libraries. However, converting models into C language programs using m2cgen eliminates the dependence on Python and third-party libraries, compiles the model into a dynamic link library for ease of use, and improves the scalability and portability of the optimization methods in this paper.

% Based on the previous model evaluation, the models with the best performance on different prediction tasks are all tree-based models. These models are translated into if-else branch statements in the target language by m2cgen, and then recursively process each branch node of the tree. The code in \ref{code-lgbm2c} shows the C language function code generated by m2cgen for the FORMAT model. The input array stores the feature values of the sparse matrix. The score function determines which branch to enter based on the size of the feature values, assigns different values to intermediate variables, and after completing the calculation of all intermediate variables, inputs them into the softmax function according to a specific calculation rule. The softmax function calculates the probability of achieving optimal SpMV operation performance for the input matrix in five sparse storage formats and stores it in the array var0. Finally, the probability array var0 is copied to the output array output. After calling the score function, the array index corresponding to the maximum value in the returned array can be used as the result of model classification.

