\section{Conclusions}
\label{sec:conclusions}
In this paper, we proposed a method to improve the recognition of unknown words and proper nouns in existing CTC-based models without requiring retraining. 
During inference, wildcard CTC is applied to an intermediate layer of the acoustic encoder to efficiently search CTC paths corresponding to target keywords. 
These paths are then used to bias subsequent layers. 
By treating non-keyword segments as wildcards, the proposed approach is robust to ambiguous alignments, enabling keyword detection even at lower encoder layers, where predictions are generally less reliable.
In Japanese ASR experiments, the method achieved a 29\% improvement in the F1 score for unknown words.
Since no additional training or text-to-speech modules are required, the method can be easily integrated into large-scale CTC-based ASR models, reducing operational costs while improving the recognition of target keywords.

