\section{Strongly continuous and bi-continuous semigroups}
\label{sec:koopmansemigroup}

Let $X$ be a Banach space over the complex numbers and $\mathcal{L}(X)$ be the set of bounded linear operators from $X$ into $X$. A family $\{T(t), t\geq 0\} \subseteq \mathcal{L}(X)$ is called  a \textit{strongly continuous semigroup} if $T(0)=I$, if $T(t+r)=T(t)  T(r)$ for all $t,r \geq 0$, and if for each $x\in X$ the map $t \to T(t)x$ is continuous on $[0,\infty)$. If $\{T(t), t\geq 0 \} \subset \mathcal{L}(X)$ is a semigroup of linear operators, then the linear operator $\mathcal{A}$ defined by $$ D(\mathcal{A}) = \{x\in X: \lim_{t\to 0^+} \frac{T(t)x-x}{t} :=y \hbox{ exists in } X\},\; \mathcal{A} x:=y \; \text{for}\;  x\in D(\mathcal{A}) $$
is called the generator of the semigroup $\{T(t),t\geq0\}$. 

If the semigroup is strongly continuous, then 
the domain $D(\mathcal{A})$ is dense in $X$, $\mathcal{A}$ is a closed linear operator, and there exists $w\in\mathbb{R}$ such that $\lambda I - \mathcal{A}$ is invertible for all $\lambda \in \mathbb{C}$ with $Re(\lambda) > w$ (see \cite{nagel2}). Also, it is well known that all strongly continuous semigroups are \textit{exponentially bounded}; i.e., if the three defining properties above hold, then there exist $M\geq 1$ and $\omega \in \mathbb{R}$ such that $\Vert T(t)\Vert \leq Me^{{\omega}t}$ for all $t\geq 0$, (see, e.g \cite{nagel2}, page 39).

\begin{example}\label{nowhere}
Let \(\Omega = [0,\infty)\) and let \(\sigma(t,x) = t + x\) denote the unique solution of \(x'(t) = 1\) with initial condition \(x(0) = x \in \Omega\). The induced Koopman-Lie semigroup is given by
\begin{equation}\label{e4}
t\rightarrow T(t)g(x)=g(\sigma(t,x))=g(t+x).
\end{equation}

This defines a semigroup on $\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{F}(\Omega,\mathbb{C})$ and on all $T(t)$-invariant function spaces $\mathcal{M} \subset \mathcal{F}$. However, while the algebraic semigroup properties $T(0)=I$, and $T(t+r)=T(t)T(r)$ for all $t,r \geq 0$ hold on all $T(t)$-invariant $\mathcal{M} \subset \mathcal{F}$, the regularity property (3) depends heavily on $\mathcal{M}$. For example:
\begin{itemize}
\item If $\mathcal{M} :=C_{0}([0,\infty),\mathbb{C})=\{g\in C_b([0,\infty),\mathbb{C}): \lim\limits_{x \to \infty}g(x)=0\}$, then it can be easily seen that the semigroup (\ref{e4}) is strongly continuous since $g$ vanishes at infinity and is uniformly continuous on compact intervals.
\item If $\mathcal{M} := C_{b}([0,\infty),\mathbb{C})$, then the shift semigroup (\ref{e4}) is not strongly continuous. This can be seen by taking $g(x)=e^{ix^{2}}$. Then, $\Vert T(t+r)g- T(t)g\Vert =2$ for all $t,r \geq 0$. So, $t \to T(t)g$ is nowhere continuous and, therefore, not measurable on $[0,\infty)$ since it is not almost separably valued (see, for example Pettis' Theorem in \cite{abhn}).
\end{itemize}
\end{example} 

The shift semigroup (\ref{e4}) on $\mathcal{M} := C_{b}([0,\infty),\mathbb{C})$ is an example of a semigroup that is not strongly continuous but ``\textit{bi-continuous"}. The bi-continuous semigroup framework, introduced by F. K\"uhnemund in her dissertation \cite{JD3} at the University of T\"ubingen, provides an efficient approach to the work of Dorroh, Lovelady, Neuberger, and Sentilles, see \cite{JD3}, \cite{JD8}, and \cite{JD9}. One of the key features of the bi-continuous semigroup framework is that many important results from the theory of strongly continuous semigroups can be lifted to bi-continuous semigroups.

 The shift semigroup (\ref{e4}) on $\mathcal{M} := C_{b}([0,\infty),\mathbb{C})$ is bi-continuous in the following sense: for each $t\ge 0$, $T(t)$ is a continuous (bounded) linear operator from the Banach space $\M$ into itself  with respect to norm topology on $\M$, while for each $f\in \M$ the map $t \rightarrow T(t)f$ from $[0,\infty)\rightarrow C_{b}([0,\infty),\mathbb{C})$ is continuous with respect to the compact-open topology, or equivalently, the topology of uniform convergence on compact sets. This leads to the concept of bi-admissible Banach spaces $(\M, ||\cdot||, \tau)$.
\begin{definition}
\label{Def:re-admissable}
Let $(X, \| \cdot \|)$ be a Banach space, and let $\tau$ be the topology generated by a family of seminorms $\{p_\alpha\}_{\alpha \in I}$ on $X$. Then $X = (X, \| \cdot \|, \tau)$ is a bi-admissible Banach space if $p_\alpha$ satisfies the following conditions:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\|x\| = \sup\limits_{\alpha \in I} p_\alpha(x)$ for all $x \in X$.
\item Every norm-bounded, $p$-Cauchy sequence is $p$-convergent in $X$.
\item $x = 0$ if and only if $p_\alpha(x) = 0$ for all $\alpha \in I$ (separating).
\item The space $(X,\tau)^*$ is norming for $(X,||\cdot||)$; i.e., $||x|| = \sup\{|\phi(x)|\}$, where the supremum is taken over all $\phi\in (X,\tau)^{*}\subset (X,\|\cdot\|)^{*}$ with $\|\phi\|\le 1$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}\label{defBi}
Let $(X,\|\cdot\|,\tau)$ be a bi-admissible Banach space. An operator family $\{T(t) : t\geq 0\} \subseteq \mathcal{L}(X)$ is a bi-continuous semigroup with respect to $\tau$ and of type $\omega$ if the following conditions hold:

\begin{itemize}[before=\setlength{\baselineskip}{27pt},itemsep=-10pt]
\item $T(0)=I$ and $T(t+s)=T(t)T(s)$ for all $s,t \geq 0$.    
\item The operators are exponentially bounded, i.e.; $\|T(t)\| \leq M e^{\omega t}$ for all $t\geq 0$ and some constants $M\geq 1$ and $\omega \in \mathbb{R}$.    
\item The map $t \rightarrow T(t)f$ $(t\geq 0)$ is strongly $\tau$-continuous for each $f \in X$.    
\item $T(t)$ is locally bi-equicontinuous, where for every $\tau$-convergent null sequence $f_n\subset X$, $\tau$-$\lim\limits_{n \rightarrow \infty}T(t)f_n=0$ uniformly for $t$ in compact intervals of $\mathbb{R_+}$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

As observed by K\"uhnemund in \cite{JD3}, it is one of the great advantages of the framework of bi-continuous semigroups, that many (most) of the central results of the theory of strongly continuous semigroups that are based on Laplace transform methods (see \cite{JD50}) can be lifted to the bi-continuous case. To state the main properties of bi-continuous semigroups and their generators, the following definition is needed. 

\begin{definition} Let $(X,\|\cdot\|,\tau)$ be a bi-admissible Banach space. 
\begin{itemize}
\item A subset $D\subset X$ is called bi-dense if for every $g\in X$ there
exists a $\|\cdot\|$-bounded sequence $g_n\in D$  which is $\tau$–convergent to $g$. 
\item An operator $(\K,D(\K))$ is called bi-closed, if for all sequences $g_n \in D(\K)$ that satisfy $\sup\limits_{n\in\N}\{\|g_n\|, \|\K g_n\|\} < \infty$, $\tau-\lim g_n = g$, and $\tau-\lim \K g_n = f$, we have $g\in D(\K)$ and $\K g = f$.
\item The generator $(\K, D(\K))$ of a bi-continuous semigroup $T(t)$ on $X$ is given by \[\K g := \tau - \lim\limits_{t\to 0^+} \frac{T(t)g-g}{t}\]
for all $g \in D(\mathcal{K})$, where $D(\mathcal{K})$ denotes the set of all $g\in X$ such that $\tau\text{-}\lim\limits_{t\to 0^+} \frac{T(t)g - g}{t}$ exists and $\sup\limits_{0<t\le 1} \{\frac{\|T(t)g-g\|}{t}\}$ is finite.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\section{Flows and Koopman semigroups}\label{sec:flow}

In this section, we review key definitions and properties of flows, introduce the Lie–Koopman generator, and present the Dorroh–Neuberger Theorem, which characterizes Koopman generators of continuous flows on Polish spaces \cite{JD2}. 
\begin{definition}
\label{def:flow}
Let $\Omega$ be a set (called the \emph{state space}). A function 
\[
\sigma : [0,\infty) \times \Omega \rightarrow \Omega, \quad (t, x) \mapsto \sigma(t, x),
\]
is called an \emph{autonomous, $\Omega$-invariant flow map} if the following conditions hold:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\sigma(0, x) = x$ for all $x \in \Omega$;
\item $\sigma(t, \sigma(r, x)) = \sigma(t + r, x)$ for all $x \in \Omega$ and all $t, r \geq 0$ with $t + r < \infty$;
\item For every $x \in \Omega$, the trajectory $\sigma(t, x)$ remains in $\Omega$ for all $t \geq 0$.
\end{itemize}
Then, the collection $\Sigma = \{ \sigma(\cdot, x) : x \in \Omega \}$ is called an \emph{autonomous flow in} $\Omega$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
\label{def:cts at x}
Let $\Omega\subseteq \mathbb{R}^{N}$ be locally compact. A flow $ \sigma:[0,\infty)\times \Omega\to \Omega$ is said to be jointly continuous if    
\begin{itemize}
\item $t \to \sigma(t,x)$ is continuous for $t\in[0,\infty)$ for all $x \in \Omega$, and
\item $x \to \sigma(t,x)$ is continuous on $\Omega$ for all $t\geq 0$. That is, if $x\in \Omega$, and $x_{n}\to x$, then $\sigma(t,x_n) \to \sigma(t,x)$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}
