\begin{definition}
Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ be a space state, assume $F: \mathbb{R}^n \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^n$ where $x\in\Omega$, and let $t\rightarrow x(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n$ be a differentiable function for $t\in [0,\infty)$ that satisfies 
\begin{equation}\label{difeq}
x'(t)=F(x(t)),\quad x(0)=x_0=x 
\end{equation}
for all $t \in [0,\infty)$. Then $x(\cdot)$ is called a classical solution. 
%\footnote {a set $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ is positively invariant if and only if no solution starting inside $\Omega$ can leave in the future, i.e. $\forall \hspace{2mm} x \in \Omega,\hspace{2mm} \forall \hspace{2mm} t \geq 0,\hspace{2mm} x(t)\in \Omega$}. 
\end{definition}	


\begin{proposition}\label{flow, autoumus}
Assume that for all $x\in \Omega$, (\ref{difeq}) has a unique classical solution $t \rightarrow x(t)$ for $0\leq t<\infty$. If $\Omega$ is $[0,\infty)$-invariant, then
\begin{equation*}
t \rightarrow \sigma(t,x):=x(t) \quad (t\in[0,\infty)
\end{equation*}
defines an autonomous flow in $\Omega$.
\end{proposition}	

\begin{proof}
Let $t \rightarrow x(t)=\sigma(t,x)$ be the unique solution of (\ref{difeq}) for $0\leq t<\infty$. Assume that $0\leq t,r,t+r<\infty$ and define
\begin{equation*}
w(t):=x(t+r)=\sigma(t+r,x) \quad \text{for} \quad   0\leq t+r < \infty.
\end{equation*}
Then $w(0)=x(r)=\sigma(r,x)$ and $w'(t)=x'(t+r)=F(x(t+r))=F(w(t))$. By uniqueness,
\begin{equation*}
\sigma(t+r,x)=w(t)=\sigma(t,\sigma(r,x)).
\end{equation*}
\end{proof}

We now introduce the Lie–Koopman semigroup and explore its connection to the dynamics of the underlying flow. Let $\Omega$ be a set and $\mathcal{F}(\Omega, \mathbb{C})$  the vector space of all functions from $\Omega$ into $\mathbb{C}$. The goal of ``Koopmanism'' is to show that for every autonomous, $\Omega$-invariant flow $\Sigma$ and appropriately chosen observation $g:\Omega \rightarrow \mathbb{C}$, there exists a linear Koopman operator $\mathcal{K}$ that ``generates'' the observations $t \rightarrow g(\sigma(t,x))$. The infinitesimal generator
\begin{equation}
\label{koos}
\mathcal{K}g: x\to \lim_{t \to 0^{
+}}\frac{T(t)g(x)-g(x)}{t}
=\lim_{t \to 0^{+}}\frac{g(\sigma(t,x))-g(x)}{t}  
\end{equation}
is always a well-defined linear operator with domain $$D(\mathcal{K}):=\{g\in \mathcal{F}(\Omega, \mathbb{C}): \hbox{limit in (\ref{koos}) exists for all } x\in \Omega \}$$ and range in $\mathcal{F}(\Omega, \mathbb{C})$. 
If $\Omega$ is a topological vector space, then $\mathcal{K}$ is given by 
\begin{equation}
\label{lie}
\mathcal{K}g(x)=T^{\prime}(0)g(x) =  g^{\prime}(x)\cdot \sigma^{\prime}(0,x),
\end{equation} 
and the Koopman semigroup associated to $\mathcal{K}$ is $$T(t)g(x)=e^{t\K}g(x) = g(\sigma(t,x))$$
for all $x\in\Omega$ and $0\le t<  \infty$.
As seen above in Example \ref{nowhere}, the Koopman semigroup $T(t)g(x) := g(\sigma(t,x))$ induced by a jointly continuous and global flow $\sigma : \R_+ \times \Omega$ in a Polish Space $\Omega$ may not always be strongly continuous for $g \in \mathbb{C}_b(\Omega)$ with respect to the sup-norm. However, in joint work done by J.R. Dorroh and J.W. Neuberger
 between 1990 and 1996, Koopman generators $(\K, D(\K))$ of jointly continuous and global flows on $\Omega$ were fully characterized, where the graph $(g,f)$ consists of $g,f \in \mathbb{C}_b(\Omega)$ for which
 \begin{equation*}
 \K g = f(x)= \lim_{t\to 0^+} \frac{g(\sigma(t,x))-g(x)}{t} \hbox{  for all  } x\in \Omega.
\end{equation*}
 In the language of bi-continuous semigroups, the main result of the joint work of Dorroh and Neuberger \cite{JD1, 2} concerning jointly continuous, global flows can be restated as follows. 

