\begin{example}[Van der Pol Oscillator]\\
The Van der Pol oscillator models nonlinear systems with amplitude-dependent damping, producing self-sustained oscillations that converge to a stable limit cycle. Originally used for electrical circuits, it remains a key example for studying nonlinear behavior such as relaxation oscillations and bifurcations. The Van der Pol oscillator is described by the second-order nonlinear differential equation:
 % Another well-known example of a nonlinear dynamical system is the Van der Pol oscillator, which was originally introduced to describe electrical circuits containing nonlinear resistive elements. Unlike simple harmonic oscillators, this system incorporates a nonlinear damping term that varies with the amplitude of oscillation—injecting energy when the amplitude is low and removing energy when it is high. This nonlinear damping leads to self-sustained oscillations that settle into a stable limit cycle, regardless of initial conditions. When the damping parameter is set to 1, the system demonstrates moderate nonlinearity, which makes it especially useful for exploring dynamic behaviors such as relaxation oscillations, bifurcations, and the long-term stability of nonlinear systems. The Van der Pol Equation 
$$x''(t) + (x(t)^2-1)x'(t) +x(t)=0, \quad x(0)=x_0=x,\quad x'(0)=y$$
has a unique, global solution $t\to x(t)$ for all $x,y \in\R^2$. 
 Generalized Van der Pol $\rightarrow$ Li\'enard system:\begin{equation*} x''(t) - a(x(t))x'(t) -b(x(t))=0, \quad x(0)=x_0=x,\quad x'(0)=y.
    \end{equation*}    
Define $y(t)=x'(t)$, $F_1(x,y):=y$ and $F_2(x,y)=a(x)y+b(x)$. Then the equivalent first-order autonomous system is given by 
    $$x'(t)=F_1(x(t),y(t))=y(t), \quad x(0)=x_0=x$$ $$y'(t)=F_2(x(t),y(t))=a(x(t))y(t)+b(x(t)), \quad y(0)=y_0=y$$
\textbf{Step 1:} Solve the first-order system via separation of variables.
 \begin{itemize}
   \item[a)] Freeze $y$:\hspace{1.8em} $x'(t)=y, \quad x(0)=x$
   $$\Rightarrow x(t)=\sigma_y(t,x)=yt +x$$

\item[b)] Freeze $x$:\hspace{1.8em}
$y'(t)=a(x)y(t)+b(x), \quad y_0=y$
$$\Rightarrow y(t)=\gamma_x(t,y)=
b(x)\frac{(e^{t a(x)}-1)}{a(x)}+ye^{t a(x)}$$
        \end{itemize}
\textbf{Step 2:} Finally, to approximate the solutions, we can apply the Lie-Trotter $V_1(t)$ method (Part (a), Remark \ref{remApp}).

