\begin{example}[Lorenz System]\\ 
% The Lorenz system is a well-known model that illustrates how complex and chaotic behavior can emerge from simple deterministic equations. Originally developed to approximate atmospheric convection, it consists of three nonlinear differential equations that govern the evolution of fluid-related quantities over time. What makes the Lorenz system remarkable is its extreme sensitivity to initial conditions, leading to drastically different outcomes from small changes—an essential feature of chaotic dynamics. Its solutions tend to spiral around a distinctive, butterfly-like structure in three-dimensional space, famously known as the Lorenz attractor. For given parameters $\alpha$, $\beta$, and $\rho$, consider the Lorenz equations
The Lorenz system is a classic example of chaos arising from simple deterministic rules. Originally developed to model atmospheric convection, it is governed by three nonlinear differential equations describing fluid flow. Known for its sensitivity to initial conditions, small variations can lead to drastically different outcomes, a hallmark of chaos. The system’s solutions exhibit the characteristic butterfly-shaped Lorenz attractor in three-dimensional space. For parameters $\alpha$, $\rho$, and $\beta$, the system is defined by:
\begin{align*}
    &x'(t)=\alpha(y(t)-x(t)), & x_0&=x,& \\
    &y'(t)=x(t)(\rho -z(t)) -y(t),& y_0&=y,&\\
    &z'(t)=x(t)y(t) -\beta z(t), & z_0&=z.& 
\end{align*}
\textbf{Step 1:} Solve the first order Lorenz system via separation of variables
\begin{itemize}
\item[a)] Freeze $y$, $z$:\hspace{1.8em} $x'(t)=F_1(x(t),y,z)=\alpha(y-x(t)), \quad x_0=x$
$$\Rightarrow x(t)=\sigma_{y,z}(t,x)=y(1-e^{-\alpha t})+xe^{-\alpha t}$$
\item[b)]  Freeze  $x$, $z$:\hspace{1.8em}
$y'(t)=F_2(x,y(t),z)=x(\rho - z)-y(t), \quad y_0=y $
$$\Rightarrow y(t)=\gamma_{x,z}(t,y)=x(\rho-z)(1- e^{-t})+ye^{-t}$$
\item[c)] Freeze  $x$, $y$:\hspace{1.8em}
$ z'(t)=F_3(x,y,z(t))=xy-\beta z(t), \quad z_0=z$
$$\Rightarrow z(t)=\tau_{x,y}(t,z)=\frac{xy}{\beta}(1-e^{-\beta t})+z e^{-\beta t} $$
\end{itemize}
\textbf{Step 2:} Finally, to approximate the solutions, we can apply the Lie-Trotter $V_1(t)$ method (see Remark \ref{rem324}).
