\title{Prefix-bounded matrices}

\author{N\'ora A. Borsik \thanks{Department of Operations Research, E\"otv\"os Lor\'and University Budapest, P\'azm\'any P. s. 1/c, Budapest H-1117.  E-mail:  {\tt nborsik\char'100 gmail.com} }
\ \ \ \ \ {Andr\'as Frank \thanks{HUN-REN–ELTE Egerv\'ary Research Group on Combinatorial Optimization, Department of Operations Research, E\"otv\"os Lor\'and University Budapest, P\'azm\'any P. s. 1/c, Budapest H-1117.  E-mail:  {\tt andras.frank\char'100ttk.elte.hu } }
\ \ \ \ \ {P\'eter Madarasi \thanks{HUN-REN Alfréd Rényi Institute of Mathematics, and Department of Operations Research, E\"otv\"os Lor\'and University Budapest, P\'azm\'any P. s. 1/c, Budapest H-1117.  E-mail: {\tt madarasip\char'100 staff.elte.hu }}
\ \ \ \ \ {Tam\'as Tak\'acs \thanks{Department of Operations Research, E\"otv\"os Lor\'and University Budapest, P\'azm\'any P. s. 1/c, Budapest H-1117.  E-mail: {\tt takacst2003\char'100 gmail.com }}} } }}



\maketitle




\begin{abstract}
  By unifying various earlier extensions of alternating sign matrices (ASMs), we introduce the notion of prefix-bounded matrices (PBMs).
  It is shown that the convex hull of these matrices forms the intersection of two special (called laminar) g-polymatroids.
  This implies (in a more general form) that the linear inequality system given by Behrend and Knight~\cite{behrend2007higher} and by Striker~\cite{striker2007alternating, striker2009alternating} for describing the polytope of alternating sign matrices is TDI, confirming a recent conjecture of Edmonds.
  By relying on the polymatroidal approach, we derive a characterization for the existence of prefix-bounded matrices meeting upper and lower bound requirements on their entries.

  Furthermore, we point out that the constraint matrix of the linear system describing the convex hull of PBMs (in particular, ASMs) is a network matrix.
  This implies that (a) standard network flow techniques can be used to manage algorithmically optimization and structural results on PBMs obtained via g-polymatroids, (b) the linear system is actually box-TDI, and (c) the convex hull of PBMs has (a sharpened form of) the integer Carath\'eodory property, in particular, the integer decomposition property.
  This latter feature makes it possible to confirm (in an extended form) an elegant conjecture of Brualdi and Dahl~\cite{brualdi2023multi} on the decomposability of a so-called $k$-regular alternating sign matrix as the sum of $k$ pattern-disjoint ASMs.
\end{abstract}



\noindent {\bf Keywords}:  \ alternating sign matrices, generalized polymatroids, network flows, polynomial algorithms, totally unimodular matrices, min-max and feasibility theorems.





\section{Introduction}\label{bpmintro}





A $(0,\pm 1)$-valued square matrix is called an {\bf alternating sign matrix} (ASM) if (a) the total sum of the entries in each row and in each column is $+1$, and (b) the $+1$ and $-1$ entries in each row and in each column alternately follow each other (implying that both the first and the last non-zero entries in each row and column are $+1$).
An immediate observation shows that a $(0,\pm 1)$-valued square matrix is an ASM if and only if (a) holds, and (b') the sum of the entries in every starting segment (which is called a prefix) of each row and each column is at least $0$ and at most $+1$.

The first appearance of this concept seems to be the paper of Mills et al.~\cite{mills1982proof} from 1982.
The study of AMSs is motivated by their connection with models in statistical mechanics, most notably the square ice model (see, e.g., the book of Bressoud~\cite{bressoud1999proofs}).


Since the first appearance of ASMs, a huge literature of the topic has been developed.
At the beginning, researchers concentrated mainly on enumerative problems concerning ASMs.
In 1999, Bressoud and Propp published a survey~\cite{bressoud1999alternating} detailing early results in this direction.
They write:

\medskip

{\it ``The ASM Conjecture has served to cross-fertilize the various modern offspring of classical invariant theory, drawing attention to connections no one had recognized.
  The study of alternating sign matrices should continue to bear fruit for many years to come and to tantalize us with fruit that is just beyond our reach.''}


\medskip In 2007, Behrend and Knight~\cite{behrend2007higher} and Striker~\cite{striker2007alternating, striker2009alternating} opened a new door to investigations of ASMs by providing a polyhedral description of the polytope (convex hull) of ASMs.
Since then, various extensions of the notion of ASMs have been introduced and investigated, concerning, among others, polyhedral descriptions and existence (or feasibility) problems in which the goal is to explore situations where there exists (extended) ASMs with specified restrictions and prescriptions on their entries.
In this direction, we should emphasize the rich and fertilizing contribution of R.\ Brualdi and his co-authors.


For example, in a recent paper, Brualdi and Dahl~\cite{brualdi2024frobenius} solved a special case of the problem of characterizing $(0,\pm 1)$-valued square matrices which can be made an ASM by replacing some of their $\pm 1$ entries by $0$.
They pose the general problem of characterizing such $(0,\pm 1)$-valued square matrices, and write:

\medskip {\it ``Basic Question:  Given an $n\times n$ $(0,\pm 1)$-matrix, when does it contain an $n\times n$ ASM?''}

\medskip

\noindent Here a matrix $X$ ``contains'' $A$, or in their terms, $A$ is a subordinate of $X$ if $A$ can be obtained from $X$ by replacing some non-zero entries of $X$ by $0$.
Brualdi and Dahl solved the problem for a special class of matrices $X$ (see Theorem~4.1 in their paper), but remarked for the general case --- when $X$ is an arbitrary $(0,\pm 1)$-matrix --- that:

\medskip
{\it ``In this generality, there is probably no simple answer\dots''}
\medskip

In the present paper, we shall provide such an answer (see Theorem~\ref{alarendelt}), in fact, within a broader framework that extends beyond alternating sign matrices (see Theorem~\ref{general1}).


What is the novelty that gave rise to this new result?
In 2024 and also in 2025, Jack Edmonds gave two conference talks entitled ``Ray and me''~\cite{Edmonds2024_talk2, Edmonds2024_talk1} on the occasion of the 100th birthday of D.R.\ Fulkerson.
In his slides, as well as in a circular email sent to several experts, he drew attention of operations researchers by indicating that classical concepts and results of combinatorial optimization might be applicable to the study of alternating sign matrices.
For example, he formulated the following:

\medskip {\it ``Ray Fulkerson's Birthday Conjecture (RFB):  The Behrend-Knight-Striker linear inequality system is TDI.''  }

\medskip


\noindent Here TDI is the standard abbreviation of total dual integrality which is a fundamental concept in combinatorial optimization (see, e.g.,~\cite{schrijver2003combinatorial}).
Edmonds also remarked that the natural problem of finding a minimum cost ASM had not previously been investigated, and indicated that he found such an algorithm.
With this conjecture and remark, Edmonds opened a new door from combinatorial optimization to the hall of ASMs.
It was his initiative that triggered our present work, which is an attempt to enter this door.

With the help of a rather easy observation, we shall show that the truth of Edmonds' RFB conjecture follows from an early result of Edmonds~\cite{edmonds1970submodular} stating that a linear inequality system described by the incidence matrix $A$ of the union of two laminar families is totally unimodular (TU) and therefore such a system is totally dual integral (TDI).
In fact, we shall prove the conjecture in a stronger form concerning prefix-bounded matrices, see Corollary~\ref{box-TDI}.
Based on the total unimodularity of $A$ and on a simple new observation (formulated in Proposition~\ref{strong-Cara}), we shall prove a recent conjecture of Brualdi and Dahl~\cite{brualdi2023multi}, see Corollary~\ref{B+D.conj}.

Actually, the polytope of ASMs (and various extensions of ASMs) is the intersection of two (rather special) generalized polymatroids.
This area stemmed from the paper of Edmonds~\cite{edmonds1970submodular}, and it makes possible to develop general results on ASMs and extensions.

We also show that these polytopes can be obtained as the projection of a network circulation polyhedron, implying that several optimization problems concerning ASMs (and extensions) can be managed algorithmically via standard network flow techniques.




\subsection{Preliminaries}\label{preli}



For an integer $j\geq 1$, $[j]$ denotes the set $\{1,\dots ,j\}$.
For integers $i\leq j$, $[i,j]:= \{i,\dots ,j\}$.
For integers $m\geq 1, n\geq 1$, let $S_{m,n}:=[m] \times [n]$.
$S_{m,n}$ may also be viewed as a two-dimensional array.
A matrix $A$ of size $m \times n$ may be viewed as a two-variable function on the set $S_{m,n}$.
Therefore, the entry of $A$ in row $i$ and column $j$ is denoted by $A(i,j)$.


For a function $f$ on ground-set $S$ and for $X\subseteq S$, we use the notation
\[
  \widetilde f(X):=\sum\ [f(s) : s\in X],
\]
where $\widetilde f(\emptyset ):=0$.
In particular, for a set-function $p$ and set-system $\cal I$, $ \widetilde p({\mathcal I}) : = \sum [p(X) : X\in {\cal I}]$.
For set-functions we assume throughout that they are $0$ on the empty set.


The union $X\cup \{v\}$ of a set $X$ and a singleton $\{v\}$ is denoted by $X+v$.
The difference $\{v :  v\in X, v\not \in Y\}$ of sets $X$ and $Y$ is denoted by $X-Y$.
The symmetric difference of sets $X$ and $Y$ is denoted by $X \ominus Y:= (X-Y)\cup (Y-X)$.
The complement $S-Z$ of a subset $Z\subseteq S$ is denoted by $\ol Z$.



Two subsets $X$ and $Y$ of a ground-set $S$ is called {\bf intersecting} if $X\cap Y \not= \emptyset $ and {\bf properly intersecting} if $X-Y$, $Y-X$, and $X\cap Y$ are non-empty.
If, in addition, $S-(X\cup Y)$ is non-empty, then $X$ and $Y$ are {\bf crossing}.
A family of subsets is {\bf laminar} (resp., {\bf cross-free}) if it contains no two properly intersecting (crossing) members.
A {\bf sub-partition} $\{X_1,\dots ,X_k\}$ of ground-set $S$ is a family of disjoint subsets of $S$.



Although the concept of a network matrix is well-known (see, e.g., the books~\cite{frank2011connections, schrijver2003combinatorial}), we include its definition here for completeness.

Let $T$ be the arc-set of a (directed) spanning tree of a (weakly) connected digraph $D= (V,A)$, and let $N:=A-T$ be the set of non-tree arcs.
A {\bf network matrix} $M_D$ belonging to $T$ is a $(0,\pm 1)$-valued $\vert T\vert \times \vert N\vert$ matrix in which the rows (columns) correspond to the elements of $T$ \ ($N$).
The entry $M_D(e,f)$ belonging to row $e\in T$ and column $f\in N$ is non-zero if and only if $e$ lies in the unique circuit $C$ of $T + f$ (the fundamental circuit of $f$); in that case, $M_D(e,f) = +1$ if $e$ and $f$ are oriented in the same direction in $C$, and $-1$ otherwise.
In particular, if every fundamental circuit happens to be a one-way circuit, then the network matrix is $(0,+1)$-valued.


It is known that if a linear cost-function $cx$ on a polyhedron $Q$ (which is the intersection of a finite number of (closed) half-spaces, or in other words, the solution set of a system of linear inequalities) is bounded from below, then $\min \{cx :  x\in Q\}$ exists.
When $cx$ is not bounded from below over $Q$, we write $\min \{cx :  x\in Q\}=-\infty $, and similarly if $cx$ is not bounded from above, then $\max \{cx :  x\in Q\}=+\infty $.

A {\bf box} $T(f,g) := \{x\in {\bf R}\sp S : f\leq x\leq g\}$ is a polyhedron defined by functions $f\leq g$ where $f$ may have $-\infty $ values, $g$ may have $+\infty $ values.
A {\bf plank} $K(\alpha,\beta ):= \{x\in {\bf R}\sp S : \alpha \leq \widetilde x(S) \leq \beta \}$ is a polyhedron defined by numbers $-\infty \leq \alpha \leq \beta \leq +\infty $.

For a polyhedron $Q:=\{x : Ax\leq b\}$ and a positive integer $k$, the polyhedron $\{x : Ax\leq kb\}$ is called the {\bf $\boldsymbol{k}$-elongation} (or just an elongation) of $Q$.
An integral polyhedron $Q$ is said to admit the {\bf integer decomposition property} if, for every positive integer $k$, every integral vector of the $k$-elongation of $Q$ can be expressed as the sum of $k$ integral vectors in $Q$.
Baum and Trotter~\cite{baum1978integer} proved that a polyhedron described by a TU-matrix and an integral bounding vector has the integer decomposition property.

As a significant sharpening of this result, Gijswijt and Regts~\cite{gijswijt2012polyhedra} (see Theorem~6 in their paper) proved that the projection (along coordinate axes) of a polyhedron $Q \subseteq {\bf R}\sp n$ defined by a TU-matrix and an integral bounding vector admits the {\bf integer Carath\'eodory property}.
That is, for every positive integer $k$, any integral vector $z^*$ in the $k$-elongation of $Q$ can be expressed as a positive integer linear combination of affinely independent integral vectors in $Q$.
More precisely, there exist affinely independent integral vectors $z_1, \dots, z_t \in Q$ and positive integers $\mu_1, \dots, \mu_t$ such that $\mu_1 + \dots + \mu_t = k$ and $z^* = \mu_1 z_1 + \dots + \mu_t z_t$.

We say that in the expression $z\sp * = \mu_1z_1 + \cdots + \mu_tz_t$, the vectors $z_1, \dots ,z_t$ are {\bf sign-consistent with} $z\sp *$ if, for each $j \in [n]$, $z\sp *(j) \geq 0$ implies $z_i(j) \geq 0$ for each $i \in [t]$, and $z\sp *(j) \leq 0$ implies $z_i(j) \leq 0$ for each $i \in [t]$.
We close this section by showing that the theorem of Gijswijt and Regts has a self-refining nature, as captured in the following proposition.

\Proposition\label{strong-Cara}
Let $A$ be a TU-matrix and $b$ an integer vector such that the polyhedron $Q := \{x: Ax\leq b\}$ is non-empty.
For a positive integer $k$, let $Q_k$ denote the $k$-elongation of $Q$, that is, $Q_k := \{x:  Ax\leq kb \}$, and let $z\sp *$ be an integral vector in $Q_k$.
Then there exist affinely independent integral vectors $z_1,\dots ,z_t \in Q$ that are sign-consistent with $z^*$, and there are positive integer coefficients $\mu _1, \dots, \mu_t$ with $\mu_1 + \dots + \mu_t = k$ such that $z\sp *=\mu_1z_1 + \cdots + \mu_tz_t$.
\eP
\Proof
We show that the proposition follows directly from the theorem of Gijswijt and Regts.
To see this, extend the original linear system $Ax\leq b$ by the following new inequalities:
$$ \hbox{ $x(i)\geq 0$ \ if \ $z\sp *(i)\geq 0$, \quad \quad \quad $x(i)\leq 0$ \ if \ $z\sp *(i)\leq 0$.  }\ $$

The resulting system still has a totally unimodular constraint matrix, and it defines a new polyhedron $Q' \subseteq Q$.
Let $Q'_k$ denote the $k$-elongation of $Q'$.
It immediately follows that $z\sp *\in Q'_k$, thus, by applying the original theorem of Gijswijt and Regts, we obtain affinely independent integral vectors $z_1, \dots ,z_t \in Q'$, and positive integer coefficients $\mu _1, \dots, \mu_t$ with $\mu_1 + \dots + \mu_t = k$ such that $z\sp * = \mu _1z_1 + \cdots + \mu _tz_t$.
The definition of $Q'$ ensures that the vectors $z_1, \dots, z_t$ are sign-consistent with $z\sp *$, as claimed.
\FBOX

\medskip

Proposition~\ref{strong-Cara} will be referred to as the sign-consistent sharpening of the theorem of Gijswijt and Regts.


\section{Generalized polymatroids}

As already mentioned in the Introduction, the main driving force behind the present work is the simple yet fundamental observation that the convex hull of alternating sign matrices is the intersection of two (rather special) generalized polymatroids.
This is why we outline some important properties of g-polymatroids in the following two sections.
As a natural extension of the notion of polymatroids, g-polymatroids were introduced and studied by Frank~\cite{frank1984generalized}.
The books of Frank~\cite{frank2011connections} and Schrijver~\cite{schrijver2003combinatorial} are rich sources for further information.
This polymatroidal background helps formulating the right theorems, which is useful even in cases when it is possible to work out proofs not relying on g-polymatroids.
We also hope that the polymatroidal view shall open new doors toward further investigations.

In what follows, we are given two integer-valued set-functions $p$ and $b$, defined on a ground-set $S$, as well as two integer-valued functions $f$ and $g$ on $S$.
Here, $f$ and $p$ serve as lower bounds, and their values are permitted to be $-\infty$.
Analogously, the upper bounds $g$ and $b$ are allowed to take the value $+\infty$.



A set-function $b$ on ground-set $S$ is {\bf fully submodular} or just {\bf submodular} if the submodular inequality
\[
  b(X) + b(Y) \geq b(X\cap Y) + b(X\cup Y)
\]
holds for every $X,Y \subseteq S$.
When the submodular inequality is required only for intersecting pairs $X,Y$, we speak of an {\bf intersecting submodular} function.
A set-function is {\bf fully} ({\bf intersecting}) {\bf supermodular} if $-p$ is fully (intersecting) submodular.


A pair $(p, b)$ of set-functions on a ground-set $S$ is called {\bf strongly paramodular} (or, simply, a {\bf strong pair}) if $p$ is fully supermodular, $b$ is fully submodular, and the {\bf cross-inequality}
\[
  b(X) - p(Y) \geq b(X - Y) - p(Y - X)
\]
holds for all $X, Y \subseteq S$.

For a strong pair $(p,b)$, the polyhedron $Q(p,b):= \{x : p(Z) \leq \widetilde x(Z) \leq b(Z)$ for every $Z \subseteq S \}$ (or, more concisely, $\{x : p\leq \widetilde x\leq b\}$) is called a {\bf g-polymatroid}, and we say that $Q(p,b)$ is {\bf bordered} by $(p,b)$.
The empty set is also considered to be a g-polymatroid.
It is known that both a face and a projection of a g-polymatroid are also g-polymatroids.
A deeper theorem asserts that the (Minkowski-) sum of g-polymatroids $Q(p_i,b_i)$ \ ($i=1,\dots ,k)$ bordered by integer-valued strong pairs is also a g-polymatroid whose unique bordering strong pair is $(\sum p_i, \sum b_i)$ (see Theorem~14.2.15~in~\cite{frank2011connections}).



Let $b$ be a fully submodular function for which $b(S)$ is finite.
The polyhedron $B(b):= \{x : \widetilde x(Z)\leq b(Z)$ for each $Z\subset S$ and $\widetilde x(S)=b(S)\}$ is called a {\bf base-polyhedron}.
When $b(S)=0$, we speak of a {\bf 0-base-polyhedron}.
The empty set is also considered to be a base-polyhedron.
When $p$ is a fully supermodular function for which $p(S)$ is finite, the polyhedron $B'(p):= \{x : \widetilde x(Z)\geq p(Z)$ for every $Z\subset S$ and $\widetilde x(S)=p(S)\}$ is a base-polyhedron, namely, $B'(p)=B(b)$ where $b(X):= p(S)-p(S-X)$ for every $X\subseteq S$.


\Claim\label{pSbS} Let $(p\sp *,b\sp *)$ be a strong pair for which $p\sp *(S)=b\sp *(S)$.
Then $p\sp *(X) = b\sp *(S)-b\sp *(S-X)$ for every subset $X\subseteq S$, and the g-polymatroid $Q(p\sp *,b\sp *)$ is the base-polyhedron $B(b\sp *) = B'(p\sp *)$.
\eCl

\Proof By applying the cross-inequality first to sets $S-X, S$ and then to sets $S,X$, we get that $p\sp *(S) - b\sp *(S-X) \leq p\sp *(X)$ and $b\sp *(S) - p\sp *(X) \geq b\sp *(S-X)$, and hence $p\sp *(S) - b\sp *(S-X) \leq p\sp *(X) \leq b\sp *(S) - b\sp *(S-X)$.
But here we must have equality because $p\sp *(S)=b\sp *(S)$.
Therefore, $p\sp *(X)=b\sp *(S) - b\sp *(S-X)$, and we get similarly that $b\sp *(X)=p\sp *(S) - p\sp *(S-X)$, from which $Q(p\sp *,b\sp *) = B(b\sp *) = B'(p\sp *)$ follows.
\FBOX

\medskip



The following result is also known from the literature (see, e.g., Proposition~II.2.2 in~\cite{frank1988generalized}).


\Proposition The g-polymatroid $Q(p\sp *,b\sp *)$ bordered by an integer-valued strong pair $(p\sp *,b\sp *)$ is an integral polyhedron which is never empty.
\FBOX \eP

A pair $(p,b)$ of set-functions is called {\bf weakly paramodular} (or, simply, a {\bf weak pair}) if $p$ is intersecting supermodular, $b$ is intersecting submodular, and the cross-inequality holds for properly intersecting subsets $X,Y \subseteq S$.
By Propositions~II.2.5 and~II.2.6 in~\cite{frank1988generalized}, we have

\Proposition\label{J17-2.6} {\bf (A)} \ The polyhedron $Q(p,b)$ bordered by a weak pair $(p,b)$ is a (possibly empty) g-polymatroid, which is a base-polyhedron when $p(S)=b(S)$.

\medskip

\noindent {\bf (B)} \ The polyhedron $Q(p,b)$ is non-empty if and only if $p(\cup Z_i) \leq \sum b(Z_i)$ and $b(\cup Z_i) \geq \sum p(Z_i)$ hold for every sub-partition $\{Z_1,\dots ,Z_q\}$ of the ground-set $S$.
\FBOX \eP

Part (B) of Proposition~\ref{J17-2.6} is, in fact, equivalent to a theorem of Fujishige~\cite{fujishige1984structures}, which characterizes the non-emptiness of a base-polyhedron bordered by a crossing submodular function.

The following four propositions on g-polymatroids are useful results from the literature (see, e.g., the paper~\cite{frank1988generalized} or the book~\cite{frank2011connections}).

\Proposition\label{hatarpar} Let $Q$ be a non-empty g-polymatroid.
There exists a unique strong pair $(p\sp *,b\sp *)$ for which $Q=Q(p\sp *,b\sp *)$, namely,
\[
  p\sp *(Z) = \min \{ \widetilde x(Z) : x\in Q\} \ \ \ \hbox{and}\ \ \ b\sp *(Z)= \max \{ \widetilde x(Z) : x\in Q\}.
\]
\FBOX \eP


\Proposition\label{felig} Suppose that a g-polymatroid $Q:=Q(p,b)$ bordered by a weak pair $(p,b)$ is non-empty and that $p(S)=b(S)$.
Let $(p\sp *,b\sp *)$ denote the unique strong pair bordering $Q$.
Then $Q=B(b\sp *)$.
\FBOX \eP


For a weak pair $(p,b)$, it is easily seen that if we increase the $p$-values on some singletons and/or decrease the $b$-values on some singletons, then the modified pair $(p\sp +,b\sp -)$ is also weakly paramodular.
It is also true that we get a weak pair when $p(S)$ is increased and/or $b(S)$ is decreased.
These imply the following.


\Proposition The intersection of a g-polymatroid $Q$ with a box $T(f,g)=\{x : f\leq x\leq g\}$ is a g-polymatroid, and so is the intersection of $Q$ with a plank $K(\alpha,\beta )=\{x : \alpha \leq \widetilde x(S) \leq \beta \}$.
\FBOX \eP


Proposition~II.2.11 in~\cite{frank1988generalized} is as follows.

\Proposition\label{J17-2.11} Suppose that the intersection of a g-polymatroid $Q:=Q(p\sp *,b\sp *)$ bordered by a strong pair $(p\sp *,b\sp *)$ with a box $T:=T(f,g)=\{x : f\leq x\leq g\}$ is non-empty.

\medskip

\noindent {\bf (A)} \ The unique strong pair $(p\sp T,b\sp T)$ bordering the g-polymatroid $Q\cap T$ is
\[
  \begin{cases}
    & p\sp T (Z) = \max \{p\sp *(X) + \widetilde f(Z-X) - \widetilde g(X-Z) : X\subseteq S \},\\
    & b\sp T (Z) = \min \{b\sp *(X) + \widetilde g(Z-X) - \widetilde f(X-Z) : X\subseteq S \}.
  \end{cases}
\]

\noindent {\bf (B)} \ In the special case when $g\equiv +\infty $, the formula in (A) reduces to
\[
  \begin{cases}
    & p\sp T (Z) = \max \{p\sp *(X) + \widetilde f(Z-X) : X\subseteq Z \},\\
    & b\sp T (Z) = \min \{b\sp *(X) - \widetilde f(X-Z) : X \supseteq Z \}.
  \end{cases}
\]

\noindent {\bf (C)} \ In the special case when $f\equiv -\infty $, the formula in (A) reduces to
\[
  \begin{cases}
    & p\sp T (Z) = \max \{p\sp *(X) - \widetilde g(X-Z) : X \supseteq Z \},\\
    & b\sp T (Z) = \min \{b\sp *(X) + \widetilde g(Z-X) : X \subseteq Z \}.
  \end{cases}
\]
\FBOX \eP






\section{Special g-polymatroids}


\subsection{Laminar g-polymatroids }\label{lam.g-poli}

A special case of g-polymatroids bounded by a weak pair is the following.
Let $\cal L$ be a laminar family of non-empty subsets of ground-set $S$, and suppose that the bordering set-functions $p$ and $b$ may be finite only on the members of $\cal L$.
In this case, the intersecting sub- and supermodularity as well as the cross-inequality requires nothing since a laminar family has no two properly intersecting members.
We shall refer to such a g-polymatroid $Q(p,b)$ as a {\bf laminar g-polymatroid}.
Since adding singletons and/or the ground-set $S$ to $\cal L$ preserves laminarity, the intersection of a laminar g-polymatroid with a box or a plank is again a laminar g-polymatroid.
Similarly, both faces and projections of a laminar g-polymatroid remain laminar g-polymatroids.


The following basic observation was formulated by Edmonds~\cite{edmonds1970submodular} (see Theorem~(39) in his paper, and also Theorem~41.11 in the book of Schrijver~\cite{schrijver2003combinatorial}).

\Lemma [Edmonds]\label{Edmonds-lemma} Let ${\cal L}'$ be the union of two laminar families on a ground-set $S$.
Then the incidence matrix of ${\cal L}'$ is totally unimodular.
\FBOX \eL



\Remark\label{2-lamin} {\em It is easy to show that the incidence matrix of ${\cal L}'$, in which the columns correspond to the elements of $S$, is a network matrix (see, for example, Theorem~4.2.8 in~\cite{frank2011connections}).
  A classic theorem of Tutte~\cite{tutte1965lectures} states that every network matrix is TU, implying Edmonds' lemma (see also Theorem~4.2.5 in~\cite{frank2011connections}).
  It is also known that a polyhedron described by an arbitrary network matrix is the projection of a feasible circulation polyhedron (see, for example, Theorem~4.3.2 in~\cite{frank2011connections}).
  This implies that a linear program described by a network matrix (or its transpose) can be formulated as a cheapest circulation problem (or its dual), and hence it can be solved via standard flow algorithms in strongly polynomial time.
  $\bullet $ }\eRe

It is known that a linear inequality system described by a TU-matrix and an integral bounding vector is box-TDI.
Since the intersection of a laminar g-polymatroid with a box and a plank is also a laminar g-polymatroid, the following theorem follows from Lemma~\ref{Edmonds-lemma} and Remark~\ref{2-lamin}.

\THEOREM\label{box-TDI1} Let ${\cal L}_1$ and ${\cal L}_2$ be two laminar families on ground-set $S$.
For $i=1,2$, let $p_i \leq b_i$ be integer-valued functions on ${\cal L}_i$.
Moreover, let $f\leq g$ be lower and upper bound functions on $S$, and let $\alpha \leq \beta $ be two integers.


\medskip

\noindent {\bf (A)} \ The linear inequality system
\begin{equation}
  \begin{cases}
    & p_1(Z) \leq \widetilde x(Z) \leq b_1(Z) \hbox{ for every } Z \in {\mathcal{L}}_1\\
    & p_2(Z) \leq \widetilde x(Z) \leq b_2(Z) \hbox{ for every } Z \in {\mathcal{L}}_2\\
    & f\leq x\leq g\\
    & \alpha \leq \widetilde x(S) \leq \beta
  \end{cases}\label{(2-lamin-leir)}
\end{equation}
is box-TDI.
\medskip

\noindent {\bf (B)} \ For a cost-function $c$, a cheapest (integral) element of the polyhedron described by~\eqref{(2-lamin-leir)} can be computed with the help of an algorithm for computing a cheapest feasible circulation.
\FBOX \eT




\subsection{Prefix-bounded g-polymatroids }\label{PB-g-poli}

Let $U:=\{u_1,\dots ,u_t\}$ be a totally ordered ground-set, and let $u_0$ be an additional element not contained by $U$.
A subset $U_{[h,k]}:=\{u_h,\dots ,u_k\}$ \ $(1\leq h\leq k\leq t)$ of $U$ consisting of subsequent elements of $U$ is called a {\bf segment}, while the special segment $U_{[k]}:=U_{[1,k]}$ is a {\bf prefix} of $U$.
An arbitrary subset $Z\subseteq U$ can uniquely be decomposed into disjoint maximal segments $Z_1,\dots ,Z_q$, which are {\bf separated} in the sense that they do not ``touch'' each other, that is, they are separated by some elements of $U-Z$.
We refer to these subsets $Z_i$ as the {\bf segments} in $Z$.

Let $\varphi \leq \gamma $ be two integer-valued functions on $U+u_0$, for which $\varphi (u_0)=\gamma (u_0)=0$.
Here $\varphi $ may take $-\infty $ value and $\gamma $ may take $+\infty $ value.
Define two set-functions $p_\varphi $ and $b_\gamma $ on $U$ as follows.
For a prefix $Z:=U_{[k]}$, let $p_\varphi (Z):= \varphi (u_k)$ and $b_\gamma (Z):= \gamma (u_k)$ \ ($k=1,\dots ,t$), and, for all other non-empty subsets $Z$ of $U$, let $p_\varphi (Z):=-\infty $ and $b_\gamma (Z):=+\infty $.
Then $(p_\varphi,b_\gamma)$ is a weak pair, where $p_\varphi$ is fully supermodular, $b_\gamma$ is fully submodular, and $p_\varphi \leq b_\gamma$.
By the discrete separation theorem~\cite{frank1982algorithm}, there always exists a modular function between $p_\varphi$ and $b_\gamma$, thus the g-polymatroid $Q(p_\varphi,b_\gamma)$ is non-empty.
We call such a g-polymatroid an {\bf elementary prefix-bounded g-polymatroid}, while the direct sum of such g-polymatroids is a {\bf prefix-bounded g-polymatroid}.
It follows from the definition that a prefix-bounded g-polymatroid is laminar; consequently, its intersection with a box and a plank is also laminar.
Our objective is to identify the unique strong pair that borders it.
To do so, it suffices to consider only elementary prefix-bounded g-polymatroids.


\medskip

Define two set-functions $p\sp * $ and $b\sp * $ on $U$ as follows.
For a segment $Z:=U_{[h,k]}$ \ ($1\leq h\leq k\leq t$), let
\begin{equation}
  \begin{cases}
    & p\sp *(Z) := \varphi (u_k) - \gamma (u_{h-1}),\\
    & b\sp *(Z) := \gamma (u_k) - \varphi (u_{h-1}),
  \end{cases}\label{(p'b'def)}
\end{equation}
and for a non-empty subset $Z\subseteq U$ with segments $Z_1,\dots ,Z_q$, let
\begin{equation}
  \begin{cases}
    & p\sp *(Z) := \sum\ [p\sp *(Z_r) : r=1,\dots, q],\\
    & b\sp *(Z) := \sum\ [b\sp *(Z_r) : r=1,\dots, q].
  \end{cases}\label{(addit)}
\end{equation}



\Lemma\label{p*b*} The pair $(p\sp *,b\sp *)$ of set-functions defined in~\eqref{(addit)} is the unique strong pair bordering the elementary prefix-bounded g-polymatroid $Q(p_\varphi, b_\gamma) \subseteq {\bf R} \sp U$.
If $\varphi (u_t)=\gamma (u_t)$, then $Q(p_\varphi, b_\gamma)$ is the base-polyhedron for which $p\sp *(U) = \varphi (u_t)=\gamma (u_t) =b\sp *(U)$ and $p\sp *(Z) = b\sp *(U) - b\sp *(U-Z)$.
\eL



\Proof We present the details of the proof only for the special case when both $\varphi$ and $\gamma$ are finite-valued.
The general case follows by similar arguments, though it involves additional technical considerations, see Remark~\ref{infdet}.


By Proposition~\ref{hatarpar}, what we have to prove for every subset $Z\subseteq U$ is that
\[
  \begin{cases}
    & p\sp *(Z) = \min \{\widetilde x(Z) : x\in Q(p_\varphi, b_\gamma)\},\\
    & b\sp *(Z) = \max \{\widetilde x(Z) : x\in Q(p_\varphi, b_\gamma)\}.
  \end{cases}
\]


We prove only the first equality, as the second one follows analogously.
Let us prove first that $\min \{\widetilde x(Z) : x\in Q(p_\varphi, b_\gamma)\} \geq p\sp *(Z)$, that is, $\widetilde x(Z) \geq p\sp *(Z)$ holds for an arbitrary element $x\in Q(p_\varphi, b_\gamma)$.

Consider first the case when $Z$ itself is a segment $U_{[h,k]}$ \ ($1\leq h\leq k \leq t$).
If $h=1$, then for the prefix $U_{[k]}=U_{[1,k]}$, we have
\[
  \widetilde x(Z)= \widetilde x(U_{[k]})\geq p_\varphi (U_{[k]}) = \varphi (u_k) = p\sp *(U_{[k]}) = p\sp *(Z).
\]

If $h\geq 2$, then for a non-prefix segment $U_{[h,k]}$, we have
\begin{align}\label{(x(Z))}
  \begin{split}
    \widetilde x(Z) &= \widetilde x(U_{[h,k]}) = \widetilde x(U_{[k]}) - \widetilde x(U_{[h-1]}) \geq  p_\varphi (U_{[k]}) - b_\gamma (U_{[h-1]})
    \\ &= \varphi (u_k) - \gamma (u_{h-1}) = p\sp *(U_{[h,k]}) = p\sp *(Z).
  \end{split}
\end{align}


In the general case, let $Z_1,\dots ,Z_q$ be the maximal segments in $Z$.
By applying~\eqref{(x(Z))} to $Z_r$ \ ($r=1,\dots ,q$) in place of $Z$, we obtain the following from the definition of $p\sp *$ given in~\eqref{(addit)}:
\[
  \widetilde x(Z) = \sum\ [\widetilde x(Z_r) : r=1,\dots ,q] \geq \sum\ [p\sp *(Z_r) : r=1,\dots ,q] = p\sp *(Z).
\]


To see the reverse inequality $\min \{\widetilde x(Z) : x\in Q(p_\varphi, b_\gamma)\} \leq p\sp *(Z)$, we have to find an element $x_Z\in Q(p_\varphi, b_\gamma)$ for which $\widetilde x_Z(Z)= p\sp *(Z)$.
To this end, define function $\mu _Z : U+u_0\rightarrow {\bf Z} $ as follows.
\[
  \mu _Z(u) :=
  \begin{cases}
    \varphi (u) & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ u\in Z,
    \\ \gamma (u) & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ u\in U-Z,
    \\ 0 & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ u=u_0.
  \end{cases}
\]


Obviously, $\varphi (u) \leq \mu _Z(u) \leq \gamma (u)$ holds for every element $u\in U$.
Define $x_Z : U\rightarrow {\bf Z} $ as
\[
  x_Z(u_j):= \mu _Z(u_j)- \mu _Z(u_{j-1}) \ \ (j=1,\dots ,t).
\]
For every segment $U_{[h,k]}$ \ ($1\leq h\leq k\leq t$), we have the following.
\[
  \widetilde x_Z(U_{[h,k]}) = \mu _Z(u_k) - \mu _Z(u_{h-1}),
\]
and in particular
\[
  \widetilde x_Z(U_{[k]}) = \mu _Z(u_k)
\]
holds for every prefix $U_{[k]} = U_{[1,k]}$.
Therefore,
\[
  p_\varphi (U_{[k]}) = \varphi (u_k) \leq \widetilde x_Z(U_{[k]}) \leq \gamma (u_k) = p_\gamma (U_{[k]}),
\]
that is, $x_Z\in Q(p_\varphi, b_\gamma)$.


We claim that $\widetilde x_Z(Z) = p\sp *(Z)$.
By~\eqref{(addit)}, it suffices to see this only for the segments in $Z$.
Let $U_{[h,k]}$ be a segment of $Z$.
Then
\[
  \widetilde x_Z(U_{[h,k]}) = \mu _Z(u_k) - \mu _Z(u_{h-1}) = \varphi (u_k) - \gamma (u_{h-1}) = p\sp *(U_{[h,k]}),
\]
and thus the proof of the first part of the lemma is complete.

The second half of the lemma follows directly from Claim~\ref{pSbS}.
\FBOX \medskip




\Remark\label{infdet} {\em The proof of Lemma~\ref{p*b*} in the general case, when $\varphi $ may take $-\infty $ and $\gamma $ may take $+\infty $ values, can roughly be done along the following line.
  Let $K$ be a sufficiently large positive integer.
  For every element $u\in U$ with $\varphi (u)= -\infty $, let $\varphi (u):=-K$, and for every element $u$ with $\gamma (u)=+\infty $, let $\gamma (u):=K$.
  The general case of the lemma follows by applying its finite-valued case to these modified (finite-valued) $\varphi $ and $\gamma $.
  $\bullet $ }\eRe

\Remark {\em A special class of elementary prefix-bounded g-polymatroids is the following.
  Let $\gamma (u_i):=1$ for $i\in [t]$, and let $\varphi (u_i) := 0$ for $i\in [t-1]$ and $\varphi (u_t):=1$.
  Then the prefix-bounded g-polymatroid defined by these functions is a base-polyhedron whose integral elements are $(0,\pm 1)$-valued for which the first and last non-zero component is $+1$, and which are sign-alternating in the sense that the non-zero components follow each other with alternating sign.
  $\bullet $}\eRe


\Remark\label{extended.pbm} {\em The notion of elementary prefix-bounded g-polymatroids can be extended in a natural way to the case when $\varphi (u_0)$ and $\gamma (u_0)$ are not necessarily zero.
  This model includes the polytope of such sign-alternating $(0,\pm 1)$-valued vectors whose first and last non-zero components are arbitrarily prescribed (and not necessarily $+1$ and $+1$), see Section~\ref{WASM}.) $\bullet $ }\eRe

\Remark\label{suffix-is} {\em The question naturally arises whether one gets a g-polymatroid when there are bounds not only on the prefixes but on the suffixes (i.e., on the complements of the prefixes), as well.
  The answer in general is negative.
  Let $U:=\{u_1,u_2,u_3\}$, and let $+1$ be the upper bound on prefix $\{u_1,u_2\}$ and suffix $\{u_2,u_3\}$.
  The polyhedron belonging to these is not a g-polymatroid.

  On the other hand, if the lower and the upper bounds for the ground-set coincide, then the lower and upper bounds on the prefixes and suffixes define a base-polyhedron.
  $\bullet $ }\eRe
\medskip








\section{The intersection of two g-polymatroids with a box and a plank}



The intersection theorem concerning two g-polymatroids is as follows (see, e.g., Proposition~V.3.4 and Theorem~V.1.4 in~\cite{frank1988generalized}).


\Proposition\label{metszet} {\bf (A)} \ Let $Q_1:=Q(p\sp *_1,b\sp *_1)$ and $Q_2:=Q(p\sp *_2,b\sp *_2)$ be two g-polymatroids bordered by integer-valued strong pairs $(p\sp *_1,b\sp *_1)$ and $(p\sp *_2,b\sp *_2)$.
Their intersection $Q:=Q_1\cap Q_2$ is an integral polyhedron which is non-empty if and only if
\[
  p\sp *_1\leq b\sp *_2 \ \ \ \hbox{and}\ \ \ p\sp *_2\leq b\sp *_1.
\]

\noindent {\bf (B)} \ Let $(p_1,b_1)$ and $(p_2,b_2)$ be integer-valued weak pairs.
Then the linear inequality system
\begin{equation}
  \bigl\{\max \{p_1(X), p_2(X) \} \leq \widetilde x(X) \leq \min \{b_1(X), b_2(X)\} \hbox{ for every } X\subseteq S \bigr\}\label{(box-TDI)}
\end{equation}
is box-TDI.
\FBOX \eP
It is easy to see that Part (A) of Proposition~\ref{metszet} is equivalent to the discrete separation theorem.
Since lower and upper bounds $f\leq g$ for components and $\alpha \leq \beta $ for the total sum of components can be incorporated in a weak pair, it follows that the linear inequality system obtained from~\eqref{(box-TDI)} by adding $f\leq x\leq g$ and $\alpha \leq \widetilde x(S)\leq \beta $ is also box-TDI.
It should be noted that Part (B) was formulated earlier for polymatroids by Edmonds~\cite{edmonds1970submodular}.

We note that the set of integral elements of an integral g-polymatroid (or of the intersection of two integral g-polymatroids) is also known as an {\bf M$\nat$-convex} set (respectively, an {\bf M$\nat_2$-convex} set), as introduced by Murota~\cite{Murota03}, which play a fast increasing role in discrete convex optimization.




\Proposition\label{p0b0f0g0} Let $p_0$ be a fully supermodular function on a ground-set $S_0$ and let $b_0$ be a fully submodular function, and suppose that $p_0(S_0) = b_0(S_0)=0$.
Furthermore, let $f_0$ and $g_0$ be lower and upper bound functions on $S_0$ for which $f_0\leq g_0$.
The intersection $B_0:=B(b_0)\cap B'(p_0)\cap T(f_0,g_0)$ is non-empty if and only if
\begin{equation}
  p_0(Y_1) + \widetilde f_0(Y_2-Y_1) \leq b_0(Y_2) + \widetilde g_0(Y_1-Y_2)\label{(Y1Y2)}
\end{equation}
holds for every $Y_1,Y_2\subseteq S_0$.
\eP

\Proof For an element $z\in B(b_0)\cap B'(p_0)\cap T(f_0,g_0)$, we have
\[
  p_0(Y_1) + \widetilde f_0(Y_2-Y_1) \leq \widetilde z(Y_1) + \widetilde z(Y_2-Y_1) = \widetilde z(Y_1\cup Y_2)
\]
and
\[
  b_0(Y_2) + \widetilde g_0(Y_1-Y_2) \geq \widetilde z(Y_2) + \widetilde z(Y_1-Y_2) = \widetilde z(Y_1\cup Y_2),
\]
from which~\eqref{(Y1Y2)} follows.


Now we prove the reverse direction.
First, apply Proposition~\ref{J17-2.6} for the following weak pair defining the intersection $B'(p_0) \cap T(f_0,+\infty )$:
\[
  p(X) :=
  \begin{cases}
    \max\{\widetilde f_0(X),p_0(X)\} & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ |X| = 1,\\
    p_0(X) & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ |X| \neq 1,
  \end{cases}
  \qquad
  b(X) :=
  \begin{cases}
    0& \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ X = S_0,\\
    +\infty & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ X \neq S_0,
  \end{cases}
\]
where $X\subseteq S_0$.
Observe that the condition of non-emptyness is $p_0(Y_1) + \widetilde f_0(S_0-Y_1) \leq 0$ for every $Y_1\subseteq S_0$, which holds by~\eqref{(Y1Y2)}.
Thus, the 0-base-polyhedron $B'(p_0) \cap T(f_0,+\infty)$ is non-empty.



It follows from Proposition~\ref{J17-2.11} that the unique fully supermodular function $p_0\sp f$ bordering this $0$-base-polyhedron from below is
\[
  p\sp f_0(U)= \max\{p_0(Y) + \widetilde f_0(U-Y) : Y\subseteq U\}.
\]

We obtain analogously that the 0-base-polyhedron $B(b_0)\cap T(-\infty ,g)$ is also non-empty, and the unique fully submodular function $b_0\sp g$ bordering this $0$-base-polyhedron from above is
\[
  b\sp g_0(U)= \min\{b_0(Y) + \widetilde g_0(U-Y) : Y\subseteq U\}.
\]

It follows from the discrete separation theorem that $B_0$ is non-empty if and only if $p_0 \sp f\leq b \sp g_0$, which is equivalent to requiring that
\[
  p_0(Y_1) + \widetilde f_0(U-Y_1) \leq b_0(Y_2) + \widetilde g_0(U-Y_2)
\]
holds whenever $U\subseteq S_0$ and $Y_1, Y_2\subseteq U$.
But if this is violated for some $U,Y_1,Y_2$, then $f_0\leq g_0$ implies that it is violated when $U$ is replaced by $U':=Y_1\cup Y_2$, that is, $p_0(Y_1) + \widetilde f_0(Y_2-Y_1) > b_0(Y_2) + \widetilde g_0(Y_1-Y_2)$, contradicting~\eqref{(Y1Y2)}.
\FBOX

\medskip In the literature, there is a characterization for the non-emptiness of the intersection of two g-polymatroids described by a weak pair (see, for example,~\cite{frank1984generalized}) but the condition is a bit cumbersome.
Fortunately, we shall need only a special case when we want to characterize the non-emptiness of the intersection of two g-polymatroids, given by their strong pairs, with a box and a plank.
The following result is not fundamentally new, but we are not aware of any previous formulation.




\THEOREM\label{Q1Q2T} We are given two g-polymatroids $Q_1:=Q(p\sp *_1,b\sp *_1)$ and $Q_2:=Q(p\sp *_2,b\sp *_2)$ on a ground-set $S$ where $(p\sp *_1,b\sp *_1)$ and $(p\sp *_2,b\sp *_2)$ are strong pairs.
Furthermore, let $f$ and $g$ be lower and upper bounds on $S$ for which $f\leq g$, where $f$ may take $-\infty $ and $g$ may take $+\infty $ values.
Let $T:=T(f,g)=\{x : f\leq x\leq g\}$ denote the box defined by $f$ and $g$.
Finally, let $\alpha $ and $\beta $ be numbers serving as lower and upper bounds for which $-\infty \leq \alpha \leq \beta \leq +\infty $, and let $K=K(\alpha,\beta )$ denote the plank defined by $\alpha $ and $\beta $.

Then the intersection $Q_1\cap Q_2\cap T\cap K$ is non-empty if and only if each of
\begin{gather}
  p\sp *_1(X_1) + \widetilde f(X_2-X_1) \leq b\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde g(X_1-X_2),\label{(Q1Q2Ta)}\\
  p\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde f(X_1-X_2) \leq b\sp *_1(X_1) + \widetilde g(X_2-X_1),\label{(Q1Q2Tb)}\\
  b\sp *_1(X_1) + b\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde g(\ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2}) - \widetilde f(X_1\cap X_2) \geq \alpha ,\label{(Q1Q2Talfa)}\\
  p\sp *_1(X_1) + p\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde f(\ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2}) - \widetilde g(X_1\cap X_2) \leq \beta\label{(Q1Q2Tbeta)}
\end{gather}
holds whenever $X_1,X_2\subseteq S$.
\eT

\Proof To prove necessity, let $z$ be an element of the intersection $Q_1\cap Q_2\cap T\cap K$.
Then
\[
  p\sp *_1(X_1) +\widetilde f(X_2-X_1) \leq \widetilde z_1(X_1) +\widetilde z(X_2-X_1) = \widetilde z(X_2) +\widetilde z(X_1-X_2) \leq b\sp *_2(X_2) +\widetilde g(X_1-X_2),
\]
from which~\eqref{(Q1Q2Ta)} follows.
The necessity of~\eqref{(Q1Q2Tb)} follows similarly.
Moreover, it is true for $z$ that
\begin{align*}
  \alpha &\leq \widetilde z(S) = \widetilde z(X_1) + \widetilde z(X_2) -\widetilde z(X_1\cap X_2) + \widetilde z(\ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2})\\
  &\leq b\sp *_1(X_1) + b\sp *_2(X_2) - \widetilde f(X_1\cap X_2) + \widetilde g(\ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2}),
\end{align*}
from which~\eqref{(Q1Q2Talfa)} follows.
The necessity of~\eqref{(Q1Q2Tbeta)} can be seen analogously.

\medskip

To prove sufficiency, let $s_0$ be a new element (not occurring in $S$) and let $S_0:=S+s_0$.
Define the bounding functions $f_0$ and $g_0$ on $S_0$ as
\[
  f_0(s) :=
  \begin{cases}
    f(s) & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s\in S,\\
    -\beta & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s=s_0,
  \end{cases}
  \qquad
  g_0(s) :=
  \begin{cases}
    g(s) & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s\in S,\\
    -\alpha & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s=s_0,
  \end{cases}
\]
and let $T_0:=T(f_0,g_0).$
Define two set-functions $p\sp *_0$ and $b\sp *_0$ on $S_0$ as
\[
  p\sp *_0(Y) :=
  \begin{cases}
    p\sp *_1(Y) & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s_0 \notin Y,\\
    -b\sp *_1(S_0-Y) & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s_0\in Y,
  \end{cases}
  \qquad
  b\sp *_0(Y) :=
  \begin{cases}
    b\sp *_2(Y) & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s_0 \notin Y,\\
    -p\sp *_2(S_0-Y) & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s_0\in Y.
  \end{cases}
\]
Now $p\sp *_0$ is fully supermodular for which $p\sp *_0(S_0) = 0$, and $b\sp *_0$ is fully submodular for which $b\sp *_0(S_0)=0$.
It follows readily that for any element $z \in Q_1 \cap Q_2 \cap T \cap K$, the vector $z_0$ defined as
\[
  z_0(s) :=
  \begin{cases}
    z(s) & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s\in S,\\
    -\widetilde z(S) & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s= s_0
  \end{cases}
\]
lies in $B'(p_0\sp *)\cap B(b_0\sp *) \cap T_0$.
Conversely, if $z_0 \in B'(p_0\sp *)\cap B(b_0\sp *)\cap T_0$, then the restriction of $z_0$ to $S$ lies in $Q_1\cap Q_2\cap T\cap K$.
That is, the original intersection $Q_1\cap Q_2\cap T\cap K$ is non-empty if and only if the intersection $B'(p\sp *_0) \cap B(b\sp *_0) \cap T_0$ is non-empty.

Apply Proposition~\ref{p0b0f0g0} to $p\sp *_0$ and $b\sp *_0$ in place of $p_0$ and $b_0$, and assume indirectly that~\eqref{(Y1Y2)} is violated by $Y_1$ and $Y_2$, that is,
\begin{equation}
  p\sp *_0(Y_1) + \widetilde f_0(Y_2-Y_1)> b\sp *_0(Y_2) + \widetilde g_0(Y_1-Y_2).\label{(kontra)}
\end{equation}

If $Y_1,Y_2\subseteq S$, then, for $X_1:=Y_1$ and $X_2:=Y_2$,~\eqref{(kontra)} means

\[
  p\sp *_1(X_1) + \widetilde f(X_2-X_1) > b\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde g(X_1-X_2),
\]
contradicting~\eqref{(Q1Q2Ta)}.

If $s_0\in Y_1\cap Y_2$, then, for $X_1:=S_0-Y_1$ and $X_2:=S_0-Y_2$,~\eqref{(kontra)} means
\[
  p\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde f(X_1-X_2) > b\sp *_1(X_1) + \widetilde g(X_2-X_1),
\]
contradicting~\eqref{(Q1Q2Tb)}.


If $s_0\in Y_1$ and $Y_2\subseteq S$, then~\eqref{(kontra)} implies
\[
  -b\sp *_1(S_0-Y_1) + \widetilde f_0(Y_2-Y_1) > b\sp *_2(Y_2) + \widetilde g_0(Y_1-Y_2).
\]
In this case, for $X_1:=S_0-Y_1$ and $X_2:=Y_2$, we have $Y_2-Y_1=X_1\cap X_2$, \ $Y_1-Y_2=S-(X_1\cup X_2) + s_0 = \ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2} + s_0$, moreover, $g(s_0)=-\alpha $, and hence
\[
  -b\sp *_1(X_1) + \widetilde f(X_1\cap X_2) > b\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde g(\ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2}) - \alpha,
\]
contradicting~\eqref{(Q1Q2Talfa)}.

Finally, if $Y_1\subseteq S$ and $s_0\in Y_2$, then it follows from~\eqref{(kontra)} that
\[
  p\sp *_1(Y_1) + \widetilde f_0(Y_2-Y_1) > - p\sp *_2(S_0-Y_2) + \widetilde g_0(Y_1-Y_2).
\]
In this case, for $X_1:=Y_1$ and $X_2:=S_0- Y_2$, we have $Y_1-Y_2= X_1\cap X_2$, \ $Y_2-Y_1= S- (X_1\cup X_2) + s_0 = \ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2} +s_0 $, moreover, $f(s_0)=-\beta $, and hence
\[
  p\sp *_1(X_1) + \widetilde f(\ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2}) - \beta > -p\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde g(X_1\cap X_2),
\]
contradicting~\eqref{(Q1Q2Tbeta)}.
\FBOX




\Corollary\label{Bresz} If both g-polymatroids in Theorem~\ref{Q1Q2T} are base-polyhedra, that is, $Q_1:=B(b\sp *_1)$ and $Q_2:=B(b\sp *_2)$ for which $H:= b\sp *_1(S)=b\sp *_2(S)$, and $\alpha =-\infty,\ \beta =+\infty $, then~\eqref{(Q1Q2Ta)} and~\eqref{(Q1Q2Tb)} are equivalent, and hence, the intersection $Q_1\cap Q_2\cap T\cap K$ is non-empty if and only if
\begin{equation}
  H \leq b\sp *_1(X') + b\sp *_2(X'') + \widetilde g(\ol{X'} \cap \ol{X''}) - \widetilde f(X' \cap X'')\label{(ekvi)}
\end{equation}
holds for every $X',X''\subseteq S$.
\eCo

\Proof Apply Theorem~\ref{Q1Q2T} to the case when $\alpha =-\infty,\ \beta =+\infty $.
In this case, only~\eqref{(Q1Q2Ta)} and~\eqref{(Q1Q2Tb)} matter.
Observe that for $X' := \ol{X_1}$ and $X'':=X_2$, we have $ X_2-X_1 = X'\cap X''$, $X_1-X_2 = \ol{X'} \cap \ol{X''}$, $b\sp *_2(X_2) = b\sp *_2(X'')$, and Claim~\ref{pSbS} implies that $p\sp *_1(X_1) = H- b\sp *_1(X')$.
Therefore, the inequalities in~\eqref{(Q1Q2Ta)} and~\eqref{(ekvi)} are equivalent.
We get in an analogous way that, for $X':=X_1$ and $X'' := \ol{X_2}$, the inequalities in~\eqref{(Q1Q2Tb)} and~\eqref{(ekvi)} are equivalent.
\FBOX


\medskip The following corollary can immediately be obtained from Theorem~\ref{Q1Q2T}.

\Corollary\label{maxx(S)} Let $Q_1:=Q(p\sp *_1,b\sp *_1), Q_2:= Q(p\sp *_2,b\sp *_2)$, and $T(f,g)$ be as in Theorem~\ref{Q1Q2T}, and suppose that $Q := Q_1 \cap Q_2\cap T(f,g)$ is non-empty.
Then
\begin{gather*}
  \max \{\widetilde x(S) : x\in Q\} = \min \{b\sp *_1(X_1) + b\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde g(\ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2}) - \widetilde f(X_1\cap X_2) : X_1, X_2\subseteq S\},\\
  \min \{\widetilde x(S) : x\in Q\} = \max \{ p\sp *_1(X_1) + p\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde f(\ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2}) - \widetilde g(X_1\cap X_2) : X_1, X_2\subseteq S\}.
\end{gather*}
\FBOX \eCo

To close this section, we investigate the special case when a specified finite-valued prescription vector $m_P$ is defined on a subset $S_P$ of $S$, and we are interested in the existence of an element in the intersection of two g-polymatroids whose components in $S_P$ are the prescribed values.


\Corollary\label{fixed} Let $Q_1:=Q(p\sp *_1,b\sp *_1)$ and $Q_2:=Q(p\sp *_2,b\sp *_2)$ be g-polymatroids in ${\bf R} \sp S$ with strong bordering pairs $(p\sp *_1,b\sp *_1)$ and $(p\sp *_2,b\sp *_2)$.
Furthermore, let $m_P$ be a finite-valued vector on a subset $S_P$ of $S$.

{\bf (A)} \ The intersection $Q_1\cap Q_2$ has an integral element $z$ for which $z\vert S_P= m_P$ (that is, the restriction of $z$ to $S_P$ equals $m_P$) if and only if the following two inequalities hold for all subsets $X_1, X_2 \subseteq S$ with $X_1 \ominus X_2 \subseteq S_P$:
\begin{gather}
  p\sp *_1(X_1) + \widetilde m_P(X_2-X_1) \leq b\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde m_P(X_1-X_2),\label{(Q1Qq-mF-1)}\\
  p\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde m_P(X_1-X_2) \leq b\sp *_1(X_1) + \widetilde m_P(X_2-X_1).\label{(Q1Qq-mF-2)}
\end{gather}



{\bf (B)} \ In the special case when $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ are base-polyhedra for which $H:= b\sp *_1(S)= b\sp *_2(S)$, there exists an integral element $z\in Q_1 \cap Q_2$ for which $z\vert S_P= m_P$ if and only if
\begin{equation}
  H \leq b\sp *_1(X') + b\sp *_2(X'') + \widetilde m_P(\ol{X'} \cap \ol{X''}) - \widetilde m_P(X' \cap X'')\label{(ekvib)}
\end{equation}
holds for every $X',X'' \subseteq S$ for which $\ol{S_P} \subseteq X'\ominus X''$.
\eCo


\Proof Let $\alpha :=-\infty,\ \beta :=+\infty$, and let
\[
  f(s) :=
  \begin{cases}
    m_P(s) & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s\in S_P,\\
    -\infty & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s\in \ol{S_P},
