\medskip

It is known that while a single g-polymatroid admits the integer decomposition property, the same does not generally hold for the intersection of two g-polymatroids.
However, in the special case of laminar g-polymatroids, the situation is more favorable.
Recall that the intersection of a laminar g-polymatroid with a box and a plank yields another laminar g-polymatroid, and that the intersection of two laminar g-polymatroids can be described by a linear inequality system with a totally unimodular constraint matrix.
Therefore, Proposition~\ref{strong-Cara} directly yields the following result.

\Corollary\label{integer-Cara}
The intersection of two laminar g-polymatroids (in particular, two prefix-bounded g-polymatroids) with a box and a plank admits the sign-consistent integer Carath\'eodory property, and hence also the sign-consistent integer decomposition property.
\FBOX
\eCo

Note that a sign-consistent integer decomposition can be found in strongly polynomial time via standard network flow algorithms.





\section{Prefix-bounded matrices}

Heuer and Striker~\cite{heuer2022partial} introduced the notion of a {\bf partial alternating sign matrix} (PASM), defined as a $(0, \pm1)$-valued $m \times n$ matrix in which the sum of entries in every horizontal and vertical prefix (i.e., prefix of a row and column, respectively) is either $0$ or $+1$.
(Throughout, we refer to these simply as prefixes, occasionally specifying them as horizontal or vertical prefixes when clarity demands.)
It is straightforward to verify that any upper-left rectangular submatrix of an ASM is a PASM, and conversely, every PASM can be obtained in this way.
Heuer and Striker~\cite{heuer2022partial} showed that the convex hull of PASMs is the polytope $\{ x \in {\bf R}\sp {m \times n} : 0 \leq \widetilde{x}(Z) \leq 1 \text{ for every prefix } Z\}$, where $\widetilde{x}(Z)$ denotes the sum of entries in prefix $Z$, see Theorem~4.6 of their paper.

Another extension of ASMs was investigated by Behrend and Knight~\cite{behrend2007higher}.
They call an integer-valued square matrix a {\bf higher spin alternating sign matrix} if, for a positive integer $r$, the sum of entries of all prefixes is non-negative and at most $r$, and this sum is exactly $r$ for all full rows and columns.
For $r=1$, we get back the class of ASMs.
For larger values of $r$, however, unlike classical ASMs, the signs of non-zero entries in a row or column of a higher spin ASM do not necessarily alternate.

The notion of higher spin alternating matrices was further generalized by Brualdi and Dahl~\cite{brualdi2017alternatingExtensions}.
Given an $n \times n$ matrix $B := (b_{i,j})$ of non-negative integers, an integer-valued $n \times n$ matrix is said to be {\bf sum-majorized} by $B$ if, for each $(i,j)$, both the sum of entries in the horizontal prefix ending at $(i,j)$ and the sum of entries in the vertical prefix ending at $(i,j)$ are non-negative and at most $b_{i,j}$.
Additionally, the sum of entries in row $i$ must be exactly $b_{i,n}$ for all $i = 1, \dots, n$, and the sum of entries in column $j$ must be exactly $b_{n,j}$ for all $j = 1, \dots, n$.

A related development appears in a more recent paper by Brualdi and Dahl~\cite{brualdi2023multi}, where they study $(0,\pm 1)$-valued $m \times n$ matrices such that, for given non-negative integers $r_1, \dots, r_m$ and $s_1, \dots, s_n$, the sum of entries in row $i$ is required to be exactly $r_i$, and for every proper prefix of that row, the sum must lie between $0$ and $r_i$, and analogously, the sum of entries in column $j$ is exactly $s_j$, and for every proper prefix of that column, the sum is constrained to lie between $0$ and $s_j$.
Among other results, the authors provide a polyhedral description of the convex hull of these matrices.

Yet another extension of ASMs was introduced by Aval~\cite{aval2008keys}, who defined an $(0, \pm1)$-valued $m \times n$ matrix to be a {\bf sign matrix} if the sum of entries in every vertical prefix is either $0$ or $+1$, and the sum of entries in every horizontal prefix is non-negative.
For further studies of related classes of sign-alternating matrices, see the work of Solhjem and Striker~\cite{solhjem2019sign} as well as Heuer and Striker~\cite{heuer2022partial}.

All of the matrix classes discussed above can be viewed as special cases of the following general framework.
We call an integer-valued $m \times n$ matrix a {\bf prefix-bounded matrix} (PBM) if the sum of entries in each horizontal and vertical prefix lies between specified lower and upper bounds, which may vary from prefix to prefix.
Our goal is to establish a necessary and sufficient condition for the existence of such a matrix, subject also to given lower and upper bounds on the individual entries, and on the total sum of the entries.

Recall that $S_{m,n} := [m] \times [n]$ denotes the set of positions of the entries in an $m \times n$ matrix.
We are given two integer-valued functions $f\leq g$ on $S_{m,n}$ serving as lower and upper bounds for the entries.
Moreover, we are given a lower bound $\alpha$ and an upper bound $\beta$ for the total sum of the entries, we assume that $\alpha \leq \beta$.
Here $f$ and $\alpha$ may take $-\infty$, $g$ and $\beta$ may take $+\infty$ values.
We are also given four integer-valued $m \times n$ matrices $\Phi\sp 1$, $\Phi\sp 2$, $\Gamma\sp 1$, and $\Gamma\sp 2$, where $\Phi\sp 1$ and $\Phi\sp 2$ may have entries of value $-\infty$ and $\Gamma\sp 1$ and $\Gamma\sp 2$ may have entries of value $+\infty$.
These matrices specify lower and upper bounds on the entry-sums of horizontal and vertical prefixes, respectively.
We assume that $\Phi\sp 1 \leq \Gamma\sp 1$ and $\Phi\sp 2 \leq \Gamma\sp 2$.

Recall from Section~\ref{PB-g-poli} that given a totally ordered set $U = \{u_1, \dots, u_t\}$ and functions $\varphi $ and $\gamma $ on $U$, we defined the set-functions $p\sp *$ and $b\sp *$ as in equations~\eqref{(p'b'def)} and~\eqref{(addit)}.
We now apply this separately to each row and each column of $S_{m,n}$ with the natural left-to-right or top-to-bottom ordering as the total order, respectively.

First, consider the rows.
For the $i$-th row ($i=1,\dots ,m$), let $Q\sp 1_i$ denote the prefix-bounded (and thus laminar) g-polymatroid defined by the $i$-th row of the matrices $\Phi\sp 1$ and $\Gamma\sp 1$.
In this context, the underlying ground-set $U=\{u_1,\dots ,u_t\}$ corresponds to the positions of the $i$-th row, hence $t=n$, with $\varphi(u_k)=\Phi\sp 1 ({i,k})$ and $\gamma(u_k)=\Gamma\sp 1 ({i,k})$ for $k = 1, \dots, n$.
Let $Q\sp 1$ be the direct sum of these $m$ g-polymatroids.
Let $P\sp 1_{i,j}$ denote the horizontal prefix of $S_{m,n}$ consisting of the first $j$ positions of the $i$-th row.
Then
\begin{equation}
  Q\sp 1 = \{x\in {\bf R}\sp {m \times n} : \Phi\sp 1 (i,j) \leq \widetilde x(P\sp 1_{i,j}) \leq \Gamma\sp 1 (i,j) \hbox{ for every } (i,j) \in S_{m,n} \}.\label{(Q1def)}
\end{equation}

Second, we define the prefix-bounded g-polymatroid $Q\sp 2$ analogously as the direct sum of the $n$ elementary prefix-bounded g-polymatroids on the columns of $S_{m,n}$.
Let $P\sp 2_{i,j}$ denote the vertical prefix of $S_{m,n}$ consisting of the first $i$ positions of the $j$-th column.
Then
\begin{equation}
  Q\sp 2 = \{x\in {\bf R}\sp {m \times n} : \Phi\sp 2 (i,j) \leq \widetilde x(P\sp 2_{i,j}) \leq \Gamma\sp 2 (i,j) \hbox{ for every } (i,j) \in S_{m,n} \}.\label{(Q2def)}
\end{equation}

The unique strong pairs $(p\sp *_1,b\sp *_1)$ and $(p\sp *_2, b\sp *_2)$ bordering $Q\sp 1$ and $Q \sp 2$ can be obtained by applying Lemma~\ref{p*b*} separately to each row and each column, respectively, which we will derive later in this section.

With these constructions in place, and invoking Proposition~\ref{metszet}, we arrive at the following result.
\Proposition\label{gp-metszet} The convex hull of prefix-bounded matrices defined by the horizontal-prefix bounds $(\Phi\sp 1, \Gamma\sp 1)$ and vertical-prefix bounds $(\Phi\sp 2, \Gamma\sp 2)$ is the intersection of the prefix-bounded g-polymatroids $Q\sp 1$ and $Q\sp 2$ given in~\eqref{(Q1def)} and~\eqref{(Q2def)}.
In particular, the set of these prefix-bounded matrices is an M$\nat _2$-convex set.
\FBOX \eP



From this and from Theorem~\ref{box-TDI1}, we obtain the following two corollaries.

\Corollary\label{box-TDI} The linear inequality system
\[
  \begin{cases}
    & \Phi\sp 1 (i,j) \leq \widetilde x( P\sp 1_{i,j}) \leq \Gamma\sp 1 (i,j) \hbox{ for every } (i,j) \in S_{m,n}\\
    & \Phi\sp 2 (i,j) \leq \widetilde x(P\sp 2_{i,j}) \leq \Gamma\sp 2 (i,j) \hbox{ for every } (i,j) \in S_{m,n}
  \end{cases}
\]
describing the intersection of the (prefix-bounded) g-polymatroids $Q\sp 1$ and $Q\sp 2$ given in~\eqref{(Q1def)} and~\eqref{(Q2def)} is box-TDI.
\FBOX \eCo

\Corollary\label{cor:boundedPBMPolytope}
For given prefix-bounds $\Phi\sp 1 \leq \Gamma\sp 1$ and $\Phi\sp 2 \leq \Gamma\sp 2$, the convex hull of the integer-valued prefix-bounded matrices of size $m \times n$ whose entries lie between $f$ and $g$, and whose total sum lies between $\alpha$ and $\beta$ is described by the linear inequality system
\[
  \begin{cases}
    & \Phi\sp 1 (i,j) \leq \widetilde x( P\sp 1_{i,j}) \leq \Gamma\sp 1 (i,j) \hbox{ for every } (i,j) \in S_{m,n}\\
    & \Phi\sp 2 (i,j) \leq \widetilde x(P\sp 2_{i,j}) \leq \Gamma\sp 2 (i,j) \hbox{ for every } (i,j) \in S_{m,n}\\
    & f \leq x \leq g\\
    & \alpha \leq \widetilde x(S_{m,n}) \leq \beta.
  \end{cases}
\]
\FBOX \eCo


This polyhedron is the intersection of two laminar g-polymatroids, as it is the intersection of the (prefix-bounded) g-polymatroids $Q\sp 1$ and $Q\sp 2$ given in~\eqref{(Q1def)} and~\eqref{(Q2def)} with the box $T(f,g)$ and the plank $K(\alpha, \beta)$. 
As we noted in Section~\ref{lam.g-poli}, the linear optimization problem concerning the intersection of two laminar g-polymatroids can be formulated as an optimal feasible circulation problem.
In the special case of prefix-bounded matrices, the details of this approach shall be worked out in Section~\ref{aramokkal}.

As a special case of Corollary~\ref{integer-Cara}, we get the following.

\Corollary\label{int-decomp-spec}
The convex hull of entry-bounded and total-sum bounded prefix-bounded matrices admits the sign-consistent integer Carath\'eodory property --- and therefore also the sign-consistent integer decomposition property.
In particular, for given prefix-bounds $\Phi\sp 1 \leq \Gamma\sp 1$ and $\Phi\sp 2 \leq \Gamma\sp 2$, let $A$ be an $m \times n$ prefix-bounded matrix whose entries are between $f$ and $g$, and for which the total sum of entries lies between $\alpha$ and $\beta$.
For every positive integer $k$, there exist $m \times n$ prefix-bounded matrices $A_1, \dots, A_k$ defined by $\left\lfloor\frac{\Phi^1}{k}\right\rfloor$, $\Bigl\lceil\frac{\Gamma^1}{k}\Bigr\rceil$, $\left\lfloor\frac{\Phi^2}{k}\right\rfloor$, and $\Bigl\lceil\frac{\Gamma^2}{k}\Bigr\rceil$ whose entries are between $\left\lfloor\frac{f}{k}\right\rfloor$ and $\Bigl\lceil\frac{g}{k}\Bigr\rceil$, and for which the total sum of entries lies between $\Bigl\lfloor\frac{\alpha}{k}\Bigr\rfloor$ and $\left\lceil\frac{\beta}{k}\right\rceil$, such that $A = A_1 + \dots + A_k$ and $A_1, \dots, A_k$ are sign-consistent with $A$.
\FBOX \eCo

This immediately yields a result conjectured by Brualdi and Dahl~\cite{brualdi2023multi} (see Conjecture 5.12 in their paper), which we now describe.
For a positive integer $k$, they call a $(0,\pm1)$-valued square matrix a {\bf $\boldsymbol{k}$-regular ASM} if the entry-sum of each row and each column is $k$, and the entry-sum of each horizontal and vertical prefix lies between $0$ and $k$.

\Corollary\label{B+D.conj}
Every $k$-regular alternating sign matrix can be expressed as the sum of $k$ pairwise pattern-disjoint alternating sign matrices, where the pattern of a matrix is the set of positions of its non-zero entries.
\eCo
\Proof
The polytope of $k$-regular ASMs is the polyhedron of entry-bounded PBMs in the special case when the lower bound $\Phi\sp 1$ is identically $k$ in the last column and $0$ elsewhere; $\Phi\sp 2$ is identically $k$ in the last row and $0$ elsewhere; the upper bounds $\Gamma\sp 1$ and $\Gamma\sp 2$ are identically $k$; and the entry bounds $f$ and $g$ are identically $-1$ and $+1$, respectively.
Apply Corollary~\ref{int-decomp-spec} to this setting, and observe that sign-consistency ensures that the obtained ASMs are pairwise pattern-disjoint, completing the proof.
\FBOX

\bigskip

Now, we turn to the problem of characterizing the existence of PBMs with entry-bounds and total-sum bounds.
We first derive the unique strong pairs $(p\sp *_1, b\sp *_1)$ and $(p\sp *_2, b\sp *_2)$ bordering $Q\sp 1$ and $Q\sp 2$, respectively.
Recall the definition of separated segments within a totally ordered set $U$ from Section~\ref{PB-g-poli}, which we now apply to the rows and columns of the ground-set $S_{m,n}$.
There is a natural one-to-one correspondence between subsets of $S_{m,n}$ and systems of separated horizontal segments~---~where no restrictions are imposed between segments of different rows.
For a given subset $X \subseteq S_{m,n}$, let $\mathcal{I}\sp {\mathrm{ho}}(X)$ and $\mathcal{I}\sp {\mathrm{ve}}(X)$ denote the system of the maximal horizontal and vertical segments in $X$, respectively.
Both systems are, by definition, separated.

By applying Lemma~\ref{p*b*} separately for each row and column of $S_{m,n}$, we get that the strong pairs $(p\sp *_1, b\sp *_1)$ and $(p\sp *_2, b\sp *_2)$ bordering $Q\sp 1$ and $Q\sp 2$ are 
\begin{equation}\label{(bi*pi*)}
  \begin{aligned}
    &\begin{cases}
      & p\sp *_1 (X) %= \sum\ [ p\sp *_1(I) :  I\in {\cal I}\sp 1]
        = \sum\ [ \Phi\sp 1 (i, j_2) - \Gamma\sp 1 (i, j_1-1) :  \{(i,j_1),\dots,(i,j_2)\} \in \mathcal{I}\sp {\mathrm{ho}}(X)],\\
      & b\sp *_1 (X) %= \sum\ [ b\sp *_1(I) : I\in {\cal I}\sp 1]
        = \sum\ [ \Gamma\sp 1 (i, j_2) - \Phi\sp 1 (i, j_1-1) :  \{(i,j_1),\dots,(i,j_2)\} \in \mathcal{I}\sp {\mathrm{ho}}(X)],
      \end{cases}\\
      &\begin{cases}
      & p\sp *_2 (X) %= \sum\ [ p\sp *_2(I) : I\in {\cal I}\sp 2]
        = \sum\ [ \Phi\sp 2 (i_2, j) - \Gamma\sp 2 (i_1-1, j) :  \{(i_1,j),\dots,(i_2,j)\} \in \mathcal{I}\sp {\mathrm{ve}}(X)],\\
      & b\sp *_2 (X) %= \sum\ [ b\sp *_2(I) : I\in {\cal I}\sp 2]
        = \sum\ [ \Gamma\sp 2 (i_2, j) - \Phi\sp 2 (i_1-1, j) :  \{(i_1,j),\dots,(i_2,j)\} \in \mathcal{I}\sp {\mathrm{ve}}(X)].
    \end{cases}
  \end{aligned}
\end{equation}

Note that, for any subset $X \subseteq S_{m,n}$, the functions $p \sp *_1(X)$ and $b\sp*_1(X)$ are the strictest lower and upper bounds, respectively, implied by the horizontal-prefix bounds $\Phi^1$ and $\Gamma^1$; analogously, the functions $p \sp *_2(X)$ and $b\sp*_2(X)$ are the strictest lower and upper bounds, respectively, implied by the vertical-prefix bounds $\Phi \sp 2$ and $\Gamma \sp 2$. 
In what follows, we investigate the existence of prefix-bounded matrices (PBMs), even under additional restrictions. 
The functions $p\sp*_1$, $b\sp*_1$, $p\sp*_2$, and $b\sp*_2$ will be useful for stating the necessary and sufficient conditions.

Theorem~\ref{Q1Q2T} yields the following characterization for the existence of PBMs with entry-bounds and total-sum bounds.
\THEOREM\label{general1}
For given prefix-bounds $\Phi\sp 1 \leq \Gamma\sp 1$ and $\Phi\sp 2 \leq \Gamma\sp 2$, there exists an integer-valued prefix-bounded matrix of size $m \times n$ whose entries lie between $f$ and $g$, and for which the total sum of the entries lies between $\alpha$ and $\beta$, if and only if the following inequalities hold for every $X_1, X_2 \subseteq S_{m,n}$:
\begin{gather}
  p\sp *_1(X_1) + \widetilde f (X_2-X_1) \leq b\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde g(X_1-X_2),\label{(gen1a)}
  \\ p\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde f (X_1-X_2) \leq b\sp *_1(X_1) + \widetilde g(X_2-X_1),\label{(gen1b)}
  \\ b\sp *_1(X_1) + b\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde g(\ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2}) - \widetilde f(X_1\cap X_2) \geq \alpha,\label{(gen1alfa)}
  \\ p\sp *_1(X_1) + p\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde f(\ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2}) - \widetilde g(X_1\cap X_2) \leq \beta,\label{(gen1beta)}
\end{gather}
where $p\sp*_1$, $b\sp*_1$, $p\sp*_2$, and $b\sp*_2$ are the functions defined in \eqref{(bi*pi*)}.
\FBOX \eT

\Corollary\label{maxpkm}
Suppose that, for given prefix-bounds $\Phi\sp 1 \leq \Gamma\sp 1$ and $\Phi\sp 2 \leq \Gamma\sp 2$, there exists a prefix-bounded matrix whose entries are between $f$ and $g$.
Then the maximum of the total sum of entries over such matrices is equal to the value
\[
  \min \{b\sp *_1(X_1) + b\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde g(\ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2}) - \widetilde f(X_1\cap X_2) : X_1, X_2\subseteq S_{m,n}\},
\]
while the minimum of the total sum of entries is equal to
\[
  \max \{ p\sp *_1(X_1) + p\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde f(\ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2}) - \widetilde g(X_1\cap X_2) : X_1, X_2\subseteq S_{m,n}\},
\]
where $p\sp*_1$, $b\sp*_1$, $p\sp*_2$, and $b\sp*_2$ are the functions defined in \eqref{(bi*pi*)}.
\FBOX \eCo


In the special case $f\equiv -\infty $, $g\equiv +\infty $, $\alpha =-\infty $, $\beta =+\infty $, Theorem~\ref{general1} reduces to the following form.
\Corollary\label{nofg} There exists an integer-valued prefix-bounded matrix defined by $\Phi\sp 1$, $\Gamma\sp 1$, $\Phi\sp 2$, and $\Gamma\sp 2$ if and only if
\[
  p\sp *_1(X) \leq b\sp *_2(X) \ \ \ \hbox{and}\ \ \ p\sp *_2(X) \leq b\sp *_1(X)
\]
hold for every subset $X \subseteq S_{m,n}$, where $p\sp*_1$, $b\sp*_1$, $p\sp*_2$, and $b\sp*_2$ are the functions defined in \eqref{(bi*pi*)}.
For such matrices, the maximum possible total entry-sum is $\min \{b\sp *_1(S_{m,n}), b\sp *_2(S_{m,n})\}$, and the minimum total entry-sum is $\max \{p\sp *_1(S_{m,n}), p\sp *_2(S_{m,n})\}$.
\FBOX \eCo




\medskip


In the special case where $\alpha = -\infty$ and $\beta = +\infty$, Theorem~\ref{general1} states that there exists an $(f, g)$-entry-bounded PBM if and only if~\eqref{(gen1a)} and~\eqref{(gen1b)} hold for every $X_1, X_2 \subseteq S_{m,n}$.

Our next goal is to show that this condition can be further simplified under the additional assumption that the last columns of $\Phi\sp 1$ and $\Gamma\sp 1$ are equal, and likewise, the last rows of $\Phi\sp 2$ and $\Gamma\sp 2$ are equal.
We refer to such a matrix as a {\bf strict} PBM.
Note that ASMs, by definition, fall into this stricter class.




\THEOREM\label{strictPBM}
Assume that the last column of $\Phi\sp 1$ is equal to that of $\Gamma\sp 1$, and the last row of $\Phi\sp 2$ is equal to that of $\Gamma\sp 2$.
Furthermore, assume that the total sum of the entries in the last column of $\Phi\sp 1$ (and thus that of $\Gamma\sp 1$) equals the total sum of the entries in the last row of $\Phi\sp 2$ (and thus that of $\Gamma\sp 2$); and let $H$ denote this common value.
Then, for these prefix-bounds $\Phi\sp 1 \leq \Gamma\sp 1$ and $\Phi\sp 2 \leq \Gamma\sp 2$, there exists a (strict) prefix-bounded matrix whose entries are bounded by $f$ and $g$ if and only if
\begin{equation}
  H\leq b\sp *_1(X') + b\sp *_2(X'') + \widetilde g(\ol{X'} \cap \ol{X''}) - \widetilde f (X' \cap X'')\label{(pbmstrict)}
\end{equation}
holds for every $X',X'' \subseteq S_{m,n}$, where $b\sp *_1$ and $b\sp *_2$ are the functions defined in~\eqref{(bi*pi*)}.
\eT
\Proof
The polyhedron $Q^1$ is the direct sum of $m$ base polyhedra defined on the rows of $S_{m,n}$, while the polyhedron $Q^2$ is the direct sum of $n$ base polyhedra defined on the columns of $S_{m,n}$, thus both $Q^1$ and $Q^2$ are base polyhedra, as well.
By applying Corollary~\ref{Bresz} to $Q_1$ and $Q_2$, we obtain the desired characterization.
\FBOX \medskip


By applying Corollary~\ref{fixed} to the special case concerning PBMs, we obtain the following.

\Corollary\label{fixedPB}
Let $\Phi\sp 1$, $\Gamma\sp 1$, $\Phi\sp 2$, and $\Gamma\sp 2$ be as in Theorem~\ref{general1}.
Let $(p\sp *_1, b\sp *_1)$ and $(p\sp *_2, b\sp *_2)$ be the corresponding strong bordering pairs as defined in~\eqref{(bi*pi*)}.
Let $S_P \subseteq S_{m,n}$ and suppose a partial prescription $m_P : S_P \rightarrow {\bf Z}$ is given for the entries.
Then, for the prefix-bounds $\Phi\sp 1 \leq \Gamma\sp 1$ and $\Phi\sp 2 \leq \Gamma\sp 2$, there exists a prefix-bounded matrix extending $m_P$ if and only if~\eqref{(Q1Qq-mF-1)} and~\eqref{(Q1Qq-mF-2)} hold for every $X_1, X_2 \subseteq S_{m,n}$ for which $X_1 \ominus X_2 \subseteq S_P$.
Moreover, if the last columns of $\Phi\sp 1$ and $\Gamma\sp 1$ coincide and have entry-sum $H$, and similarly, the last rows of $\Phi\sp 2$ and $\Gamma\sp 2$ also coincide and have entry-sum $H$, then a (strict) prefix-bounded matrix extending $m_P$ exists if and only if~\eqref{(ekvib)} holds for every $X', X'' \subseteq S_{m,n}$ for which $\ol{S_P} \subseteq X' \ominus X''$.
\FBOX \eCo




\Remark {\em The concept of prefix-bounded matrices naturally extends to even more general settings.
  In addition to imposing bounds on horizontal- and vertical-prefix sums, one may also impose lower and upper bounds on the total sum of entries across the first $i$ rows (for each $i \in [m]$) and the first $j$ columns (for each $j \in [n]$).
  It is apparent that these additional requirements can be incorporated into the framework of laminar g-polymatroids.
  Indeed, the collection of all horizontal prefixes, together with the unions of the first $i$ complete rows (for $i \in [m]$), forms a laminar family.
  A similar statement holds for the vertical prefixes and the columns.
  Consequently, the results established above extend naturally to this broader setting.
  In fact, the previously discussed constraint on the total sum of entries can be seen as a special case of this generalization.
  $\bullet $ }\eRe




\Remark\label{bipartite} {\em The results of this section on prefix-bounded matrices naturally extend to bipartite graphs.
  Suppose we are given a bipartite graph $G = (V_1, V_2; E)$, where the edges incident to each node $v \in V_1 \cup V_2$ are endowed with a total order.
  Let $\Phi\sp 1 \leq \Gamma\sp 1$ and $\Phi\sp 2 \leq \Gamma\sp 2$ be functions defined on $E$, serving as lower and upper bounds.
  We are interested in the polyhedron of vectors $x \in {\bf R}\sp E$ satisfying the following constraints: for every edge $e = v_1v_2 \in E$, the sum of the $x$-values over the prefix of edges incident to $v_1$ ending with $e$ is between $\Phi\sp 1(e)$ and $\Gamma\sp 1(e)$; similarly, the sum of $x$-values over the prefix of edges incident to $v_2$ ending with $e$ lies between $\Phi\sp 2(e)$ and $\Gamma\sp 2(e)$.
  This polyhedron is the intersection of two prefix-bounded g-polymatroids, one for each node class, and as such, all previous results extend to this setting with minimal modification.
  $\bullet $ }\eRe









\section{Special classes of prefix-bounded matrices}

In this section, we explore how the previously established results on prefix-bounded matrices apply to certain well-known matrix classes.

\subsection{Alternating sign matrices}\label{AS-matrixok}

Observe that ASMs are exactly the $n \times n$ strict PBMs in the special case when the lower bound $\Phi\sp 1$ is identically $+1$ in the last column and $0$ elsewhere; the lower bound $\Phi\sp 2$ is identically $+1$ in the last row and $0$ elsewhere; and the upper bounds $\Gamma\sp 1$ and $\Gamma\sp 2$ are identically $+1$.

Recall the definitions of separated systems of horizontal and vertical segments from Section~\ref{PB-g-poli}.
For a given subset $X \subseteq S_{m,n}$, let $\mathcal{I}\sp {\mathrm{ho}}(X)$ and $\mathcal{I}\sp {\mathrm{ve}}(X)$ denote the system of the maximal horizontal and vertical segments in $X$, respectively.
We introduce the notation
\[
  \begin{cases}
    & \sigma_1(X):= \vert {\cal I}\sp {\rm ho}(X)\vert,\\
    & \sigma_2(X):= \vert {\cal I}\sp {\rm ve}(X)\vert.
  \end{cases}
\]
Clearly, for any subset $X \subseteq S_{m,n}$ formed as the union of a system $\mathcal{I}$ of separated horizontal or vertical segments, we have $\mathcal{I} = \mathcal{I}\sp {\mathrm{ho}}(X)$ or $\mathcal{I} = \mathcal{I}\sp {\mathrm{ve}}(X)$, respectively.

\THEOREM\label{AS-feas} Let $f\leq g$ be lower and upper bounds on $S_{n,n}$.
The following three conditions are pairwise equivalent.
\medskip

\noindent {\bf (A)} There exists an alternating sign matrix whose entries lie between $f$ and $g$.

\medskip

\noindent {\bf (B1)}
\begin{equation}
  \vert {\cal I}\sp 1\vert + \vert {\cal I}\sp 2\vert \geq n + \widetilde f (I_2)- \widetilde g(I_0)\label{(nkgf1b)}
\end{equation}
holds for every system ${\cal I}\sp 1$ of separated horizontal segments and for every system ${\cal I}\sp 2$ of separated vertical segments, where $I_2$ denotes the set of those positions of $S_{n,n}$ which are covered by both ${\cal I}\sp 1$ and ${\cal I}\sp 2$, while $I_0$ denotes the set of those positions which are covered by neither of them.


\medskip

\noindent {\bf (B2)}
\begin{equation}
  \sigma_1(X') + \sigma_2(X'') \geq n + \widetilde f (X' \cap X'')- \widetilde g(\ol{X'} \cap \ol{X''})\label{(nkgf1)}
\end{equation}
holds for every $X',X'' \subseteq S_{n,n}$.
\eT
\medskip


\Proof
The equivalence of~(B1) and~(B2) is immediate by the definitions of $\sigma_1$, $\sigma_2$, and by the relationships between systems of segment and subsets of $S_{n,n}$.
To prove the necessity of~\eqref{(nkgf1)}, assume that there exists an ASM $z$ satisfying the bounds $f \leq z \leq g$, and let $z(s)$ denote the entry of this matrix at position $s$.
Then, by the definition of ASMs, $\widetilde z(I)\leq 1$ holds for each segment $I$.
Therefore, for every $X', X'' \subseteq S_{n,n}$, we obtain
\[
  \begin{cases}
    & \widetilde z(X') = \sum\ [\widetilde z(I) : I \in {\cal I}\sp {\rm ho}(X')] \leq \sigma_1(X'),\\
    & \widetilde z(X'') = \sum\ [\widetilde z(I) : I \in {\cal I}\sp {\rm ve}(X'')] \leq \sigma_2(X''),
  \end{cases}
\]
from which
\begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
    n &= \widetilde z(S_{n,n}) = \widetilde z(X') + \widetilde z(X'') - \widetilde z(X' \cap X'') + \widetilde z(\ol{X'} \cap \ol{X''})\\
    &\leq \sigma_1(X') + \sigma_2(X'') - \widetilde f(X' \cap X'' ) + \widetilde g(\ol{X'} \cap \ol{X''})
  \end{split}
\end{align*}
follows, which proves the necessity of~\eqref{(nkgf1)}.


The sufficiency of the condition will be derived from Theorem~\ref{strictPBM}.
Consider the case when $m = n$, the lower bound $\Phi\sp 1$ is identically $+1$ in the last column and $0$ elsewhere; $\Phi\sp 2$ is identically $+1$ in the last row and $0$ elsewhere; and the upper bounds $\Gamma\sp 1$ and $\Gamma\sp 2$ are identically $+1$.
Recall that the class of strict PBMs defined by these functions coincides precisely with the class of ASMs.

Now the values of $b\sp *_1$ on the horizontal segments and the values of $b\sp *_2$ on the vertical segments are uniformly $+1$, and $H=n$.
Thus, the condition in~\eqref{(pbmstrict)} from Theorem~\ref{strictPBM} reduces exactly to~\eqref{(nkgf1)}, completing the proof.
\FBOX


\medskip


For ease of application, we reformulate Theorem~\ref{AS-feas} in an equivalent but slightly more tangible form.
Let ${\cal S} :=\{S_0, S_{+1}, S_{-1}, S_+, S_-, S_F\}$ be a partition of the ground-set $S_{n,n}$, where each part may be empty.
We say that a matrix is {\bf $\boldsymbol{{\cal S}}$-compatible} if each position in $S_0$, $S_{+1}$, and $S_{-1}$ is assigned the fixed value $0$, $+1$, and $-1$, respectively; each position in $S_+$ is assigned a non-negative value, and each position in $S_-$ is assigned a non-positive value.
No restrictions are imposed on the values of positions in $S_F$.
We refer to the positions in the set $S_P := S_0 \cup S_{+1} \cup S_{-1}$ as the {\bf prescribed} positions, and those in $S_F$ as the {\bf free} positions.
Our goal is to characterize those partitions $\cal S$ for which there exists an $\cal S$-compatible ASM.

Note that a system ${\cal I}\sp 1$ of separated horizontal segments is not necessarily disjoint from a system ${\cal I}\sp 2$ of separated vertical segments, since it may happen that a one-element segment $\{s\}$ appears in both systems.
To accommodate this, we adopt the convention that the union of the two systems is denoted by ${\cal I} := {\cal I}\sp 1 \uplus {\cal I}\sp 2$, where one-element segments occurring in both systems are included with multiplicity two.
That is, ${\cal I}$ is formally treated as a family of segments, rather than a system of segments.
Accordingly, when we refer to a separated family of (horizontal and vertical) segments, we allow a one-element segment to occur in two copies.

We say that the family ${\cal I} := {\cal I}\sp 1 \uplus {\cal I}\sp 2$ of segments is {\bf $\boldsymbol{{\cal S}}$-feasible} if
\begin{equation}
  I_0 \ \subseteq \ S_0 \cup S_{-1} \cup S_{-} \ \ \ \hbox{and}\ \ \ I_2 \ \subseteq \ S_0 \cup S_{+1} \cup S_{+},\label{(megenga)}
\end{equation}
where $I_0$ denotes the set of positions in $S_{n,n}$ which are not covered by any of these two systems, while $I_2$ is the set of positions which are covered by both ${\cal I}\sp 1$ and ${\cal I}\sp 2$.
Note that ${\cal S}$-feasibility is equivalent to requiring that $\cal I$ covers the positions in $S_F$ exactly once, the positions in $S_- \cup S_{-1}$ at most once, and the positions in $S_+ \cup S_{+1}$ at least once.


\THEOREM\label{AS-feas2} There exists an $\cal S$-compatible alternating sign matrix if and only if
\begin{equation}
  \vert {\cal I}\vert \geq n + \vert S_{-1} \cap I_0\vert + \vert
  S_{+1}\cap I_2\vert\label{(nkgf2)}
\end{equation}
holds for every $\cal S$-feasible family ${\cal I}:={\cal I}\sp 1 \uplus {\cal I}\sp 2$ of segments, where $I_0$ and $I_2$ denote the set of positions not covered by $\cal I$ and covered twice by $\cal I$, respectively.
\eT


\Proof The $\cal S$-compatibility of an ASM can be encoded with the help of lower and upper bound functions $f$ and $g$ defined on the ground-set $S_{n,n}$ as follows:
\[
  f(s) :=
  \begin{cases}
    0 & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s\in S_0 \cup S_+,\\
    +1 & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s\in S_{+1},\\
    -\infty & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s \in S_{-1} \cup S_-\cup S_F,\\
  \end{cases}
  \qquad
  g(s) :=
  \begin{cases}
    0 & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s\in S_0 \cup S_-,\\
    -1 & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s\in S_{-1},\\
    +\infty & \ \ \hbox{if}\ \ \ s \in S_{+1} \cup S_+\cup S_F.\\
  \end{cases}
\]

Note that $f(s)$ is finite if and only if $s\in S_0 \cup S_{+1} \cup S_+$, so the second condition in~\eqref{(megenga)} is equivalent to requiring that $\widetilde f(I_2)$ is finite; in this case, $\widetilde f(I_2) = \vert S_{+1} \cap I_2 \vert $.
Similarly, $g(s)$ is finite if and only if $s\in S_0 \cup S_{-1} \cup S_-$, and hence the first condition in~\eqref{(megenga)} is equivalent to requiring that $\widetilde g(I_0)$ is finite; in this case, $\widetilde g(I_0) = - \vert S_{-1} \cap I_0 \vert $.

Now we apply Theorem~\ref{AS-feas}.
Clearly,~\eqref{(nkgf1b)} holds automatically when $\widetilde f(I_2)=-\infty $ or $\widetilde g(I_0)=+\infty $, thus, we only need to require the inequality in cases where both quantities are finite.
In such cases, substituting the expressions for $\widetilde f(I_2)$ and $\widetilde g(I_0)$ into~\eqref{(nkgf1b)} yields the condition in~\eqref{(nkgf2)}.
\FBOX


\medskip

Let us now consider some notable special cases of Theorem~\ref{AS-feas2}.
If $S_{-} \cup S_{+} = \emptyset$, meaning that only free and prescribed positions are present and no sign constraints are imposed, then the equivalent definition of ${\cal S}$-feasibility yields the following simplified form of Theorem~\ref{AS-feas2}.


\Corollary\label{ASMF}
Let $\{S_0, S_{-1}, S_{+1}, S_F\}$ be a partition of $S_{n,n}$.
Then there exists an alternating sign matrix in which each entry with position in $S_i$ is equal to $i$ for $i \in \{0, -1, +1\}$ if and only if~\eqref{(nkgf2)} holds for every separated family ${\cal I}$ of segments that covers each position in $S_F$ exactly once, each position in $S_{-1}$ at most once, and each position in $S_{+1}$ at least once.
\FBOX \eCo


If $S_{-1} \cup S_{+1} = \emptyset$, meaning that only sign constraints may be imposed and neither $+1$ nor $-1$ values are prescribed, then Theorem~\ref{AS-feas2} reduces to the following simpler form.

\Corollary\label{subord}
Let $\{S_0,S_-, S_+, S_F \}$ be a partition of the set $S_{n,n}$.
There exists an alternating sign matrix which is $0$ on the positions in $S_0$, non-negative on the positions in $S_+$, and non-positive on the positions in $S_-$ if and only if $\vert {\cal I}\vert \geq n$ holds for every separated family ${\cal I}$ of segments which covers the positions in $S_F$ exactly once, the positions in $S_+$ at least once, end the positions in $S_-$ at most once.
\FBOX \eCo




\medskip


Brualdi and Dahl~\cite{brualdi2024frobenius} introduced the following relationship between matrices.
An $n \times n$ matrix $A$ is said to be a {\bf subordinate} of an $n \times n$ matrix $X$ \iffalse{($A\trianglelefteq X$), ($A\triangleleft X$) }\fi if $A$ can be obtained from $X$ by replacing some non-zero entries with zeros in $X$.
The problem considered in~\cite{brualdi2024frobenius} is to characterize those matrices $X$ which have an ASM subordinate.
Theorem~4.1 and~4.3 in their paper provide necessary and sufficient conditions for the existence of such a subordinate in two special cases.
Our next result extends their findings by providing a characterization that applies to the general case.

\THEOREM\label{alarendelt}
A $(0,\pm 1)$-valued $n \times n$ matrix $X$ has an alternating sign matrix subordinate if and only if it is not possible to cover the $+1$ entries of $X$ using fewer than $n$ separated segments in such a way that each $-1$ entry is covered by at most one segment.
\eT

\Proof
Finding a subordinate ASM of $X$ is the same as finding an ${\cal S}$-compatible ASM for the partition where $S_0$ is the set of positions where $X$ is $0$, $S_+$ is the set of positions where $X$ is $+1$, and $S_-$ is the set of positions where $X$ is $-1$, while $S_F, S_{-1}$, and $S_{+1}$ are empty.
The theorem then follows directly by applying Corollary~\ref{subord} to this setting.
\FBOX


\Remark {\em In their recent paper~\cite{brualdi2024frobenius}, Brualdi and Dahl posed the problem (Question 4.5) of maximizing the number of $-1$ entries over the subordinate ASMs of a given matrix $X$.
  This is clearly equivalent to maximizing the number of $+1$ entries.
  As we have seen earlier, the convex hull of $\cal S$-feasible ASMs is the intersection of two laminar base-polyhedra, and therefore, there exists a strongly polynomial algorithm for computing a maximum $w$-weight $\cal S$-feasible ASM for any weight function $w : S_{n,n}\rightarrow {\bf R}$.
  In particular, if we define $w$ to be $+1$ on the positions where $X$ is $+1$ and $0$ elsewhere, then a maximum $w$-weight subordinate ASM of $X$ has the maximum number of $+1$ entries.
  Moreover, since the underlying base-polyhedra are laminar, we shall show in Section~\ref{aramokkal} that the relevant algorithmic problems can be addressed efficiently using standard network flow algorithms.
  $\bullet $ }\eRe


\Remark {\em
  Brualdi and Kim~\cite{brualdi2015completions} established, by a self-contained inductive proof, that any so-called ``bordered-permutation'' $(0,-1)$-valued $n \times n$ matrix can be extended to an ASM by turning certain $0$ entries into $+1$.
  Our framework is well-suited to handle such extension problems; in particular, let $S_{-1}$ denote the set of positions where $A$ is $-1$, and let $S_+$ denote the set of positions where $A$ is $0$.
  Then Theorem~\ref{AS-feas2} provides a necessary and sufficient condition for the existence of such an extension for any $(0,-1)$-valued starting matrix.
  One can verify that this condition is satisfied in the special case of bordered-permutation matrices, and thus, the result of Brualdi and Kim can be derived as a corollary.
  However, this derivation is not more straightforward than the original inductive proof by Brualdi and Kim.

  The melody of this relationship between a general result and its special case is similar to the situation when we want to prove the following ``theorem''.
  {\it Every 2-regular bipartite graph has a perfect matching}.
  Although this fact can be deduced from Hall’s theorem, verifying the Hall-condition for such a graph is not at all simpler than proving the existence of a perfect matching directly.
  $\bullet $ }\eRe


\subsection{Partial alternating sign matrices}

A {\bf partial alternating sign matrix} (PASM), first introduced and studied by Heuer and Striker~\cite{heuer2022partial}, is an $m \times n$ prefix-bounded matrix defined by the identically~$0$ bounding functions $\Phi\sp 1$ and $\Phi\sp 2$ and the identically $+1$ functions $\Gamma\sp 1$ and $\Gamma\sp 2$.
For this special class of PBMs, the strong bordering pair can be derived directly from~\eqref{(bi*pi*)}, allowing us to apply Theorem~\ref{general1} and obtain the following result.

Let $\sigma^{\text{se}}_1(X)$ and $\sigma^{\text{se}}_2(X)$ denote the number of maximal proper horizontal and vertical segments in $X$, respectively, where the superscript ``\text{se}'' stands for segment.
Similarly, let $\sigma^{\text{su}}_1(X)$ and $\sigma^{\text{su}}_2(X)$ denote the number of maximal proper horizontal and vertical suffixes in $X$, respectively, where the superscript ``\text{su}'' stands for suffix.

\THEOREM\label{PASM}
There exists a partial alternating sign matrix of size $m \times n$ whose entries lie between $f$ and $g$, and whose total sum lies between $\alpha$ and $\beta$ if and only if the following inequalities hold for every $X_1, X_2 \subseteq S_{m,n}$:
\begin{gather}
  p\sp *_1(X_1) + \widetilde f(X_2-X_1) \leq b\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde g(X_1-X_2),\label{(pasma)}\\
  p\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde f(X_1-X_2) \leq b\sp *_1(X_1) + \widetilde g(X_2-X_1),\label{(pasmb)}\\
  b\sp *_1(X_1) + b\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde g(\ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2}) - \widetilde f(X_1\cap X_2) \geq \alpha,\label{(pasmalfa)}\\
  p\sp *_1(X_1) + p\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde f(\ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2}) - \widetilde g(X_1\cap X_2) \leq \beta,\label{(pasmbeta)}
\end{gather}
where, for $i = 1,2$, the functions $p\sp *_i$ and $b\sp *_i$ are defined for $X \subseteq S_{m,n}$ as
\begin{equation*}
  \begin{aligned}
    &\begin{cases}
    & \hbox{$p\sp *_i(X) := -\sigma^{\text{se}}_i(X) - \sigma^{\text{su}}_i(X)$,}\\
    & \hbox{$b\sp *_i(X) := \sigma_i(X).$}\\
    \end{cases}
  \end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
\FBOX \eT

In the special case where $\alpha = -\infty$ and $\beta = +\infty$, Theorem~\ref{PASM} states that there exists an $(f, g)$-entry-bounded PASM if and only if~\eqref{(pasma)} and~\eqref{(pasmb)} hold for every $X_1, X_2 \subseteq S_{m,n}$.
Finally, we note that both Corollaries~\ref{maxpkm} and~\ref{nofg} remain valid in the special case of PASMs.





\subsection{Weak alternating sign matrices}\label{WASM}

We say that a $(0,\pm1)$-valued $m \times n$ matrix is a {\bf weak alternating sign matrix} (WASM) if, in each row and column, the non-zero entries alternate in sign.
Brualdi and Kim~\cite{brualdi2015generalization} gave a necessary and sufficient condition for the existence of a WASM when the signs of the first and last non-zero entries in each row and column are prescribed.
We refer to these entries as the {\bf wing-entries}, and investigate the problem of finding a WASM consistent with sign-specified wing-entries.
For each row and column, there are four possible wing-entry sign patterns:  $(+,+)$, $(-,-)$, $(+,-)$, and $(-,+)$, where, for example, $(+,-)$ indicates that the first non-zero entry is $+1$ and the last one is $-1$.

This problem can be embedded into the framework of prefix-bounded matrices, which we outline below.
In fact, we provide a characterization for a more general setting in which, in addition to the wing-entry specifications, lower and upper bounds on the matrix entries are also imposed.

For a positive integer $k$ and an integer $\ell \in [k]$, define the values

\begin{equation*}
  \begin{aligned}
    &\begin{cases}
       \gamma^{++}(\ell) := +1  & \text{for } \ell \in [k-1], \\
       \phi^{++}(\ell)   :=  0  & \text{for } \ell \in [k-1], \\
       \mathrlap{\gamma^{++}(k) := \phi^{++}(k) := +1,}\hphantom{\gamma^{++}(k) := \phi^{++}(k) := +1\quad}
     \end{cases} \\
    &\begin{cases}
       \gamma^{--}(\ell) :=  0 & \text{for } \ell \in [k-1], \\
       \phi^{--}(\ell)   := -1 & \text{for } \ell \in [k-1], \\
       \mathrlap{\gamma^{--}(k) := \phi^{--}(k) := -1,}\hphantom{\gamma^{++}(k) := \phi^{++}(k) := +1\quad}
     \end{cases} \\
    &\begin{cases}
       \gamma^{+-}(\ell) := +1 & \text{for } \ell \in [k-1], \\
       \phi^{+-}(\ell)   :=  0 & \text{for } \ell \in [k-1], \\
       \mathrlap{\gamma^{+-}(k) := \phi^{+-}(k) := 0,}\hphantom{\gamma^{++}(k) := \phi^{++}(k) := +1\quad}
     \end{cases} \\
    &\begin{cases}
       \gamma^{-+}(\ell) :=  0 & \text{for } \ell \in [k-1], \\
       \phi^{-+}(\ell)   := -1 & \text{for } \ell \in [k-1], \\
       \mathrlap{\gamma^{-+}(k) := \phi^{-+}(k) := 0.}\hphantom{\gamma^{++}(k) := \phi^{++}(k) := +1\quad}
     \end{cases}
  \end{aligned}
\end{equation*}

\medskip

Let $k:=n$, and define the bounding functions for the horizontal prefixes as $\Phi\sp 1(i,j) := \phi^{s_1s_2}(j)$ and $\Gamma\sp 1(i,j) := \gamma^{s_1s_2}(j)$, where $(s_1,s_2)$ is the wing-entry sign pattern of row $i$.
Similarly, let  $k:=m$, and define the bounding functions for the vertical prefixes as $\Phi\sp 2(i,j) := \phi^{s_1s_2}(i)$ and $\Gamma\sp 2(i,j) := \gamma^{s_1s_2}(i)$, where $(s_1,s_2)$ is the wing-entry sign pattern of column $j$.

Now we derive a characterization for the existence of WASMs with specified wing-entry signs.
Recall that $\sigma^{\text{se}}_1(X)$ and $\sigma^{\text{se}}_2(X)$ denote the number of maximal proper horizontal and vertical segments in $X$, respectively, while $\sigma^{\text{su}}_1(X)$ and $\sigma^{\text{su}}_2(X)$ denote the number of maximal proper horizontal and vertical suffixes.
Similarly, we define $\sigma^{\text{pr}}_1(X)$ and $\sigma^{\text{pr}}_2(X)$ to denote the maximal proper horizontal and vertical prefixes, where the superscript ``\text{pr}'' stands for prefix.
Similarly, $\sigma^{\text{fu}}_1(X)$ and $\sigma^{\text{fu}}_2(X)$ count the total number of full rows and columns in $X$, respectively, with the superscript ``\text{fu}'' standing for full.
We define the type of a subset of a row (resp., column) as the wing-entry sign pattern of the row (column) containing it.
By applying Lemma~\ref{p*b*} and Corollary~\ref{fixedPB} in this context, we obtain the following result.
\begin{COR}
  There exists a weak alternating sign matrix with specified wing-entry signs if and only if, for every $X_1, X_2 \subseteq S_{m,n}$ such that $X_1 \cup X_2 = S_{m,n}$, the inequality
  \[
    H \leq b_1\sp *(X_1) + b_2\sp *(X_2)
  \]
  holds, where $H := b^*_1(S_{m,n}) = b^*_2(S_{m,n})$, and for $i = 1, 2$,
  \[
    b_i^*(X) := \sum\ [b_i'(X') : X' \in {\cal C}^i(X)].
  \]
  Here ${\cal C}^1(X) := \{ X \cap P\sp 1_{i,n} : i \in [m] \}$ and ${\cal C}^2(X) := \{ X \cap P\sp 2_{m,j} : j \in [n] \}$ denote the system of rows and columns of $X$, respectively, and
  \[
    b_i'(X') := \begin{cases}
        \sigma^{\text{se}}_i(X') + \sigma^{\text{pr}}_i(X') + \sigma^{\text{su}}_i(X') + \sigma^{\text{fu}}_i(X')    & \hbox{if $X'$ is of type $(+,+)$},\\
        \sigma^{\text{se}}_i(X') - \sigma^{\text{fu}}_i(X')    & \hbox{if $X'$ is of type $(-,-)$},\\
        \sigma^{\text{se}}_i(X') + \sigma^{\text{pr}}_i(X')    & \hbox{if $X'$ is of type $(+,-)$},\\
        \sigma^{\text{se}}_i(X') + \sigma^{\text{su}}_i(X')    & \hbox{if $X'$ is of type $(-,+)$}.
    \end{cases}
  \]
\FBOX\end{COR}


Corollary~\ref{fixedPB} also characterizes the existence of a WASM when certain matrix entries are fixed in advance.
More generally, Theorem~\ref{strictPBM} provides necessary and sufficient condition for the existence of a WASM with prescribed wing-entry signs subject to given lower and upper bounds on its entries.


We emphasize that in Section~\ref{aramokkal}, an alternative proof of Theorem~\ref{strictPBM} will be presented using network flow techniques.
This approach highlights that the existence problem for WASMs with entry and wing-sign constraints is algorithmically tractable and can be solved efficiently using standard network flow algorithms.


\medskip Both Behrend and Knight~\cite{behrend2007higher} and Striker~\cite{striker2007alternating, striker2009alternating} proved that the integral elements of the polytope $P$ of ASMs are in fact vertices of $P$.
In a forthcoming work, we will show the following general structural result, which immediately implies the former property of the ASM polytope.
\THEOREM
Let $P$ denote the convex hull of all $(0,\pm1)$-valued $m \times n$ matrices in which, for each row, the non-zero entries alternate in sign and the sign of either the left or the right wing-entry is specified.
Then, for any polyhedron $Q$, every integral element of $P \cap Q$ is a vertex of $P \cap Q$.
\FBOX \eT





\section{Prefix-bounded matrices and feasible circulations}\label{aramokkal}

Throughout this section, we consider PBMs defined by prefix-bounds $\Phi\sp 1$, $\Gamma\sp 1$, $\Phi\sp 2$, $\Gamma\sp 2$, and require that the entries of the PBMs in question lie between $f$ and $g$, while the total sum of its entries lies between $\alpha$ and $\beta$.
Recall that the polyhedron of these PBMs is the intersection of two laminar g-polymatroids.
Hence, it can be described by a linear inequality system given by the incidence matrix of the union of two laminar families, see Corollary~\ref{cor:boundedPBMPolytope}. This implies that this polyhedron can be obtained as the projection (restriction) of a feasible circulation polyhedron.

In what follows, we work out the details of this approach.
In particular, we show how Hoffman's classic theorem on feasible circulations~\cite{hoffman1958some,micchelli2003selected} can be used to provide an alternative proof of the non-trivial direction of Theorem~\ref{general1}.
This proof also implies that standard network flow algorithms can be used for finding the desired PBM with entry bounds and total-sum bounds, or finding a certificate that no such matrix exists as per Theorem~\ref{general1}.
Note that the minimum cost PBM problem is also tractable within this circulation framework.


\subsection{The circulation model}\label{arammodell}
In this section, we define a feasible circulation network whose solutions directly correspond to the $m \times n$ PBMs.
Let $D = (V, A)$ be a directed graph constructed as follows.
The vertex set $V$ is partitioned into two disjoint subsets, denoted by $V^1$ and $V^2$, defined as
\[
  V\sp 1 := \{v\sp 1_{i,j} : (i,j) \in S_{m,n}\} + v\sp 1_0 \ \ \ \hbox{and}\ \ \ V\sp 2:= \{v\sp 2_{i,j} : (i,j) \in S_{m,n}\} + v\sp 2_0.
\]

Now we define the arc-set $A$ by constructing four classes of arcs.
First, within the node-set $V\sp 1$, for each $i\in [m]$ and $j\in [n-1]$, include an arc $a\sp 1_{i,j} := v\sp 1_{i,j+1} v\sp 1_{i,j}$.
Additionally, for each $i\in [m]$, add an arc $a\sp 1_{i,n } := v\sp 1_0 v\sp 1_{i,n}$.
Let
\[
  A\sp 1 := \{a\sp 1_{i,j} : (i,j) \in S_{m,n}\}
\]
denote the set of all these arcs.
Next, within the node-set $V\sp 2$, for each $i\in [m-1]$ and $j\in [n]$, include an arc $a\sp 2 _{i,j}:= v\sp 2_{i,j} v\sp 2_{i+1,j}$.
For each $j\in [n]$, add an arc $a\sp 2_{m,j} := v\sp 2_{m,j}v\sp 2_0$.
These arcs form the set
\[
  A\sp 2 := \{a\sp 2_{i,j} : (i,j) \in S_{m,n}\}.
\]
To connect $V\sp 1$ to $V\sp 2$, for each $i \in [m]$ and $j \in [n]$, include an arc $a_{i,j} := v\sp 1_{i,j}v\sp 2_{i,j}$; and let
\[
  N := \{a_{i,j} : (i,j)\in S_{m,n} \}
\]
denote the set of these arcs.
Finally, insert a single arc connecting the two extra nodes
\[
  a_0 := v\sp 2_0v\sp 1_0.
\]
Figure~\ref{fig:flow} shows this construction for the case $n = m = 4$.

We will associate the arcs in $A\sp 1$ with the horizontal prefixes of the desired PBM, the arcs in $A\sp 2$ with the vertical prefixes, the arcs in $N$ with the positions of the entries, and the arc $a_0$ with the total sum of the entries.

Note that the arc-set $A\sp 1 \cup A\sp 2 + a_0 $ forms a (directed) spanning tree $T$ of $D$, whereas the arc-set $N$ forms a matching between the nodes in $V\sp 1-v\sp 1_0$ and $V\sp 2-v\sp 2_0$.
We refer to the arcs in $T$ as {\bf tree arcs} and to those in $N$ as {\bf non-tree arcs}.
In Figure~\ref{fig:flow}, the  green continuous and black dotted arrows represent the tree arcs and the non-tree arcs, respectively.


