Observe that every one-way circuit in $D$ contains the arc $a_0$, that is, $D-a_0$ is acyclic.
Furthermore, for any non-tree arc $a$, the unique circuit $C_a$ in $T+a$, called the fundamental circuit belonging to $a$, is a one-way circuit.
A basic property of circulations is that every circulation~$z~:~A \rightarrow~{\bf R}$ on the arc-set of $D$ can be written in the form $z = \sum [ z(a) \chi(C_a) : a\in N]$, where $\chi(C_a)$ denotes the characteristic vector of the one-way fundamental circuit $C_a$ (see, for example, Proposition~4.3.1 in~\cite{frank2011connections}).

The arcs $a_{i,1}, \dots, a_{i,j}$ are precisely the non-tree arcs whose fundamental circuits contain the tree-arc $a\sp 1_{i,j} \in A\sp 1$, similarly, the arcs $a_{1,j},\dots, a_{i,j}$ are precisely the non-tree arcs whose fundamental circuits contain the tree-arc $a\sp 2_{i,j}\in A\sp 2$, furthermore, the special tree-arc $a_0$, as already mentioned, is contained in every fundamental circuit.
These observations immediately yield the following.

\Claim\label{zafan} For every circulation $z$ in $D$, the $z$-values of the tree arcs can be expressed in terms of the $z$-values of the non-tree arcs as follows:
\[
  \begin{cases}
    & z(a\sp 1_{i,j}) = z(a_{i,1}) + \cdots + z(a_{i,j}),\\
    & z(a\sp 2_{i,j}) = z(a_{1,j}) + \cdots + z(a_{i,j}),\\
    & z(a_0) = \sum\ [ z(e) : e\in N].
  \end{cases}
\]
\FBOX \eCl

Note that the formulas in Claim~\ref{zafan} for the $z$-values of the tree arcs can be concisely expressed in matrix form as $z_T = M_Dz_N$, where $z_T$ and $z_N$ denote the restrictions of $z$ to the tree arcs and non-tree arcs, respectively, and $M_D$ is the network matrix associated with the (directed) spanning tree $T$ of the digraph $D$.

To encode the prefix, entry, and total-sum bounds within our circulation network, we define lower and upper bound functions $l$ and $u$ on the arc-set $A$.
These functions determine the admissible flow values on each arc in accordance with the bounds defining the desired PBM.
For each $(i,j) \in S_{m,n}$, the arc $a\sp 1_{i,j} \in A\sp 1$ corresponds to the horizontal prefix that consists of the first $j$ positions in row $i$ of the PBM. Accordingly, the bounds for each arc $a\sp 1_{i,j} \in A\sp 1$ are defined as
\begin{equation}
  l(a\sp 1_{i,j}) := \Phi\sp 1 ({i,j}) \ \ \ \hbox{and}\ \ \ u(a\sp 1_{i,j}) := \Gamma\sp 1({i,j}).\label{(a1ij)}
\end{equation}
Similarly, for each arc $a\sp 2_{i,j} \in A\sp 2$, which corresponds to the vertical prefix consisting of the first $i$ positions in column $j$, the bounds are defined by
\begin{equation}
  l(a\sp 2_{i,j}) := \Phi\sp 2 ({i,j}) \ \ \ \hbox{and}\ \ \ u(a\sp 2_{i,j}) := \Gamma\sp 2({i,j}).\label{(a2ij)}
\end{equation}
For the arcs in the set $N$, each of which corresponds to an individual entry, the lower and upper bounds are given by $f$ and $g$. That is,
\begin{equation}
  l(a_{i,j}) := f(i,j) \ \ \ \hbox{and}\ \ \ u(a_{i,j}) := g(i,j).
\end{equation}
Finally, for the special arc $a_0$, which represents the total sum of the entries, we define the bounds as
\begin{equation}
  l(a_0) := \alpha \ \ \ \hbox{and}\ \ \ u(a_0) := \beta.\label{eq:BoundsEnd}
\end{equation}


The following theorem shows that there is a one-to-one correspondence between PBMs and feasible circulations.
\THEOREM\label{PBM-aram}
Consider the circulation network defined above on the digraph $D$ with the lower and upper bounds $l$ and $u$.

{\bf (A)} \ Let $z:=(z_N,z_T)$ be an integer-valued circulation in $D$ for which $l\leq z\leq u$ (in short, $z$ is $(l,u)$-bounded).
Consider the $m \times n$ matrix $M_z$ for which $M_z(i,j) := z_N(a_{i,j})$ for $(i,j) \in S_{m,n}$.
Then $M_z$ is a matrix which is {\bf (a)} prefix-bounded with respect to horizontal-prefix bounds $\Phi\sp 1$ and $\Gamma\sp 1$ and vertical-prefix bounds $\Phi\sp 2$ and $\Gamma\sp 2$, {\bf (b)} entry-bounded by lower and upper bounds $f$ and $g$, and {\bf (c)} the total entry-sum of $M_z$ is between $\alpha $ and $\beta $; that is, the matrix $M_z$ meets the requirements in Theorem~\ref{general1}.

{\bf (B)} \ Conversely, let $M$ be a prefix-bounded matrix with entry bounds and total-sum bounds.
Define the vector $z_M$ on the arc-set of $D$ as
 \begin{equation*}
 \begin{aligned}
   &z_M(a\sp 1_{i,j}):= M(i,1) + \dots + M(i,j) & \hbox{ for every $a_{i,j}\sp 1\in A\sp 1$,}\\
   &z_M(a\sp 2_{i,j}):= M(1,j) + \dots + M(i,j) & \hbox{ for every  $a_{i,j}\sp 2\in A\sp 2$,}\\
   &z_M(a_{i,j}):= M(i,j) & \mathrlap{\hbox{ for every $ a_{i,j}\in N$,}}\hphantom{\hbox{ for every $a_{i,j}\sp 1\in A\sp 1$,}}\\   &z_M(a_0):= \sum\ [ M(i,j) : (i,j) \in S_{m,n}].\\
   \end{aligned}
 \end{equation*}
Then $z_M$ is an $(l,u)$-bounded integer-valued circulation in the digraph $D$.
\eT


\Proof (A) (a) \ To prove the prefix-boundedness of $M_z$, consider the prefix $P^1_{i,j}$ consisting of the first $j$ positions of the $i$-th row.
The sum of the entries at the positions in $P^1_{i,j}$ is
\[
  M_z(i,1) + \dots + M_z(i,j) =  z(a_{i,1}) + \dots + z (a_{i,j})=z(a\sp 1_{i,j}),
\]
which follows by the definition of $M_z$ and from Claim~\ref{zafan}.

Analogously, the sum of the entries at the positions in $P^2_{i,j}$ is
\[
  M_z(1,j) + \dots + M_z(i,j) = z(a_{1,j}) + \dots + z(a_{i,j}) =  z(a\sp 2_{i,j}).
\] 

By~\eqref{(a1ij)}~and~\eqref{(a2ij)}, the $(l,u)$-boundedness of $z$ implies that $M_z$ meets the lower and upper bounds $\Phi^1,\Gamma^1$ for horizontal and  $\Phi^2, \Gamma^2$ for vertical prefixes.

\medskip

(b) \ The constraint $l_N\leq z_N\leq u_N$ concerning non-tree arcs is equivalent to $f(i,j) \leq M_z(i,j) \leq g(i,j)$, that is, the entries of $M_z$ are indeed between $f$ and $g$.

\medskip

(c) \ The total sum of the entries of the matrix $M_z$ is given by $\widetilde z(N)$, which is equal to $z(a_0)$ by Claim~\ref{zafan}.
Since $z$ is $(l,u)$-bounded, we have $\alpha = l(a_0) \leq z(a_0) \leq u(a_0) = \beta$, and thus the total sum lies between the prescribed bounds.
\medskip

(B) \ It follows directly from the definition of $z_M$ and by the construction of $D$ that $z_M$ is a circulation.
We now verify that $z_M$ is $(l,u)$-bounded, that is, $l\leq z_M\leq u$.
By the definition of $z_M$, the horizontal-prefix bounds, the vertical-prefix bounds, the entry bounds, and the total-sum bounds for $M$ are equivalent to the $(l,u)$-boundedness of $z_M$ for the arcs in $A_1$, $A_2$, $N$, and $\{a_0\}$, respectively.
Therefore, $z_M$ is $(l,u)$-bounded, which completes the proof.
\FBOX



\subsection{ An algorithmic proof of Theorem~\ref{general1}}

Our final goal is to show that the circulation framework above makes it possible to derive the non-trivial direction of Theorem~\ref{general1} from Hoffman's classic theorem on feasible circulations.

For a digraph $D=(V,A)$ and for a subset $W\subseteq V$, let $\Delta \sp{\text{in}}(W):=\{uv \in A : u \notin W, v \in W \}$ denote the set of arcs entering $W$, and let $\Delta \sp{\text{out}}:=\{uv \in A : u \in W, v \notin W \}$ denote the set of arcs leaving $W$.
For a function $x : A \rightarrow \bf{R}$ and for a subset $W\subseteq V$, we shall use the notations $\varrho _x(W):=\widetilde x (\Delta \sp{\text{in}})$ and $\delta _x(W):=\widetilde x (\Delta \sp {\text{out}})$ to denote the sum of $x$-values of the arcs entering and leaving $W$, respectively.
Given a function $x:A \rightarrow \bf{R}$ and a subset $F \subseteq A$, we write $x \vert F$ to denote the function which is equal to $x$ on $F$ and $0$ on $A-F$.
For example, for a subset $Z \subseteq V$, the expression $\varrho_{x \vert F}(Z)$ is the sum of $x$-values on the arcs in $F$ entering $Z$.

\THEOREM [Hoffman's circulation theorem~\cite{hoffman1958some, micchelli2003selected}]\label{Hoffman-tetel}
Let $l$ and $u$ be lower and upper bound functions on the arc-set of a digraph $D=(V,A)$ for which $l\leq u$.
There is a circulation $z$ for which $l\leq z\leq u$ (in short, $z$ is $(l,u)$-bounded) if and only if $\varrho _u(W) \geq \delta _l(W)$ holds for every subset $W\subseteq V$ of nodes.
If, in addition, $l$ and $u$ are integer-valued, then the circulation $z$ may be chosen integer-valued.
\FBOX \eT

Now we are ready to derive Theorem~\ref{general1} from Hoffman's theorem.
Consider the digraph $D=(V,A)$ introduced in Section~\ref{arammodell} along with the lower and upper bounds $l$ and $u$ defined in~\eqref{(a1ij)}-\eqref{eq:BoundsEnd}.
By Part (A) of Theorem~\ref{PBM-aram}, any feasible integer-valued circulation --- when it exists --- determines an entry-bounded and prefix-bounded matrix whose total entry-sum lies between $\alpha$ and $\beta$.

Now suppose that no $(l,u)$-bounded integer-valued circulation exists in $D$.
By Hoffman's theorem, there must exist a subset $W \subseteq V$ that violates the Hoffman-condition, that is,
\begin{equation}
  \varrho _u(W) - \delta _l(W) <0.\label{(Hsertes)}
\end{equation}

Theorem~\ref{general1} characterizes the existence of the desired PBM in terms of the functions $p\sp*_1$, $b\sp*_1$, $p\sp*_2$ and $b\sp*_2$, so we need to establish the connection between these functions and our circulation network.
Consider a segment $Z \subseteq S_{m,n}$ lying in row $i$, where $i \in [m]$.
Let $(i,h)$ and $({i,k})$ denote the first and last positions of $Z$, respectively.
By applying the definition of $(p\sp *_1,b\sp *_1)$ given in~\eqref{(bi*pi*)} to the segment $Z$, we obtain from~\eqref{(a1ij)} that
\begin{equation}
  \begin{cases}
    & p\sp *_1(Z) = \Phi\sp 1({i,k}) - \Gamma\sp 1({i,h-1}) = l(a\sp 1_{i,k}) - u(a\sp 1_{i,h-1}),\\
    & b\sp *_1(Z) = \Gamma\sp 1({i,k}) - \Phi\sp 1({i,h-1}) = u(a\sp 1_{i,k}) - l(a\sp 1_{i,h-1}),
  \end{cases}\label{(b1*p1*)}
\end{equation}
with the conventions $l(a\sp 1_{i,h-1}):=0$ and $u(a\sp 1_{i,h-1}):=0$ when $h=1$.
For a subset $X\subseteq S_{m,n}$, $p \sp*_1(X)$ and $b \sp*_1(X)$ are equal to the sum of the $p\sp*_1$ and $b\sp*_1$ values of the maximal horizontal segments in $X$, respectively.

Similarly, consider a segment $Z$ in the $j$-th column of $S_{m,n}$, where $j \in [n]$.
Let $({h,j})$ and $({k,j})$ denote the first and last positions of $Z$, respectively.
By~\eqref{(a2ij)}, we have
\begin{equation}
  \begin{cases}
    & p\sp *_2(Z) = \Phi\sp 2({k,j}) - \Gamma\sp 2({h-1,j}) = l(a\sp 2_{k,j}) - u(a\sp 2_{h-1,j}),\\
    & b\sp *_2(Z) = \Gamma\sp 2({k,j}) - \Phi\sp 2({h-1,j}) = u(a\sp 2_{k,j}) - l(a\sp 2_{h-1,j}),
  \end{cases}\label{(b2*p2*)}
\end{equation}
with the conventions $l(a\sp 2_{h-1,j}):=0$ and $u(a\sp 2_{h-1,j}):=0$ when $h=1$.
For a subset $X\subseteq S_{m,n}$, $p \sp*_2(X)$ and $b \sp*_2(X)$ are equal to the sum of the $p\sp*_2$ and $b\sp*_2$ values of the maximal vertical segments in $X$, respectively.

To prove the theorem, we proceed by analyzing four distinct cases determined by the structure of the set $W$ that violates the Hoffman-condition.
These cases give rise to four types of inequalities, each corresponding to one of the necessary conditions stated in Theorem~\ref{general1}.


\medskip

{\bf Case 1.} \ $ v\sp 1_0,v\sp 2_0 \in W$.
Let $W\sp 1:= V\sp 1 -W$ and $W\sp 2:= V\sp 2-W$.
Furthermore, let
\[
     X_1 := \{ ( {i,j}) \in S_{m,n} : v\sp 1_{i,j} \in W\sp 1 \} \ \ \ \hbox{and}\ \ \  
     X_2 := \{ ({i,j}) \in S_{m,n} : v\sp 2_{i,j} \in W\sp 2 \}.
\]
Consider what~\eqref{(Hsertes)} means in terms of $X_1$ and $X_2$.
It follows from~\eqref{(b1*p1*)} and~\eqref{(b2*p2*)} that the contributions of the arcs in $A^1$ and $A^2$ to $\varrho_u(W) - \delta_l(W)$ are
\begin{align*}
    \begin{split}
      &\varrho _{u\vert A^1}(W)- \delta _{l\vert A^1}(W) = \widetilde u(A^1 \cap \Delta^{\text{out}}(W^1)) - \widetilde l(A^1 \cap \Delta^{\text{in}}(W^1)) = - p\sp *_1(X_1)\\
      &\varrho _{u\vert A^2}(W) - \delta _{l\vert A^2}(W) = \widetilde u(A^2 \cap \Delta^{\text{out}}(W^2)) - \widetilde l(A^2 \cap \Delta^{\text{in}}(W^2)) = b\sp *_2(X_2).
    \end{split}
\end{align*}
The contribution of an arc $a_{i,j} = v\sp 1_{i,j} v\sp 2_{i,j} \in N$ to $\varrho_u(W)$ and $\delta_l(W)$ is $0$ if both endpoints of $a_{i,j}$ lie in $W$ or if neither does.
If $a_{i,j}$ enters $W$, that is, if $(i,j) \in X_1 - X_2$, then it contributes $g(i,j)$ to $\varrho_u(W)$ and $0$ to $\delta_l(W)$.
If $a_{i,j}$ leaves $W$, that is, if $(i,j) \in X_2 - X_1$, then it contributes $f(i,j)$ to $\delta_l(W)$ and $0$ to $\varrho_u(W)$.
Hence,
\[
  \varrho _{u\vert N}(W) - \delta _{l\vert N}(W) = \widetilde g(X_1-X_2) - \widetilde f(X_2-X_1).
\]
The contribution of the special arc $a_0$ is $0$, since both of its endpoints are in $W$.

Summing all contributions, we obtain that
\begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
    0 &> \varrho _u(W) - \delta _l(W) = [\varrho _{u\vert A^1} (W) + \varrho _{u\vert A^2}(W) + \varrho _{u\vert N}(W)] - [\delta _{l\vert A^1}(W) + \delta_{l\vert A^2}(W) + \delta _{l\vert N}(W)]
    \\ &= [\varrho _{u\vert A^2} (W) - \delta _{l\vert A^2}(W)] + [\varrho _{u\vert A^1}(W)-\delta _{l\vert A^1}(W)] + [\varrho _{u\vert N}(W) - \delta _{l\vert N}(W)]
    \\ &= b\sp *_2(X_2) - p\sp *_1(X_1) + \widetilde g(X_1-X_2) - \widetilde f(X_2-X_1),
  \end{split}
\end{align*}
which contradicts condition~\eqref{(gen1a)}.

\medskip

{\bf Case 2.} \ $v_0^1, v_0^2 \notin W$.
Let $W\sp 1:= W\cap V\sp 1$ and $W\sp 2:=W\cap V\sp 2$, and let
\[
  X_1 := \{ ({i,j}) \in S_{m,n} : v\sp 1_{i,j} \in W\sp 1 \} \ \ \ \hbox{and}\ \ \ X_2 := \{ ( {i,j}) \in S_{m,n} : v\sp 2_{i,j} \in W\sp 2 \}.
\]
Similarly to Case 1, the contributions of $A^1$ and $A^2$ to $\varrho_u(W) - \delta_l(W)$ are
\begin{align*}
    \begin{split}
        &\varrho _{u\vert A^1}(W) - \delta _{l\vert A^1}(W) = \widetilde u(A\sp 1 \cap \Delta^{\text{in}}(W\sp 1))  - \widetilde l(A\sp 1 \cap \Delta^{\text{out}} (W\sp 1)) = b\sp *_1(X_1),\\
        &\varrho_{u\vert A^2}(W) - \delta _{l\vert A^2}(W) = \widetilde u(A\sp 2 \cap \Delta^{\text{in}}(W\sp 2)) - \widetilde l(A\sp 2 \cap \Delta^{\text{out}} (W^2)) = - p^*_2(X_2) . 
    \end{split}
\end{align*}
The arcs in $N$ contribute
\[
  \varrho _{u\vert N}(W) - \delta _{l\vert N}(W) = \widetilde g(X_2-X_1) - \widetilde f(X_1-X_2),
\]
and the special arc $a_0$ contributes $0$ as neither of its endpoints lies in $W$.

Summing all contributions, we get that
\begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
    0 &> \varrho _u(W) - \delta _l(W) = [\varrho _{u\vert A^1} (W) + \varrho_{u\vert A^2}(W) + \varrho _{u\vert N}(W)] - [\delta _{l\vert A^1}(W) + \delta _{l\vert A^2}(W) + \delta _{l\vert N}(W)]
    \\&= [\varrho _{u\vert A^1}(W) - \delta _{l\vert A^1}(W)] + [\varrho _{u\vert A^2}(W) - \delta_{l\vert A^2}(W) ] + [\varrho _{u\vert N}(W) - \delta _{l\vert N}(W)]
    \\&= b\sp *_1(X_1) - p\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde g(X_2-X_1) - \widetilde f(X_1-X_2),
  \end{split}
\end{align*}
contradicting~\eqref{(gen1b)}.




\medskip


{\bf Case 3.} \ $v\sp 1_0 \notin W, v\sp 2_0 \in W$. 
Let $W\sp 1:=V\sp
1\cap W$ and $W_2:=V\sp 2-W$, and let
\[
  X_1 := \{ ( {i,j}) \in S_{m,n} : v\sp 1_{i,j} \in W\sp 1 \} \ \ \ \hbox{and}\ \ \ X_2 := \{ ( {i,j}) \in S_{m,n} : v\sp 2_{i,j} \in W\sp 2 \}.
\]
The contribution of the arcs in $A^1$ and $A^2$ to $\varrho_u(W) - \delta_l(W)$ are
\begin{align*}
    \begin{split}
        &\varrho _{u\vert A\sp 1}(W) - \delta _{l\vert A \sp 1}(W) = \widetilde u(A^1 \cap \Delta^{\text{in}}(W^1)) - \widetilde l(A^1 \cap \Delta^{\text{out}}(W^1)) = b\sp *_1(X_1),\\
        &\varrho _{u\vert A^2}(W) - \delta _{l\vert A^2}(W) = \widetilde u(A^2 \cap \Delta^{\text{out}}(W^2)) - \widetilde l(A^2 \cap \Delta^{\text{in}}(W^2)) = b\sp *_2(X_2).
    \end{split}
\end{align*}
The arcs in $N$ contribute 
\[
  \varrho _{u\vert N}(W) - \delta _{l\vert N}(W) = \widetilde g(\ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2}) - \widetilde f(X_1\cap X_2).
\]
In this case, the special arc $a_0$ leaves $W$, thus its contribution to $\varrho_u(W)-\delta_l(W)$ is $-\alpha$.

Summing all contributions, we get that
\begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  0 &> \varrho _u(W) - \delta _l(W) = -\alpha + [\varrho _{u\vert A\sp 1}(W) - \delta _{l\vert A\sp 1}(W)] + [\varrho _{u\vert A\sp 2}(W) - \delta _{l\vert A\sp 2}(W)]\\
  &\quad+ [\varrho _{u\vert N}(W) - \delta _{l\vert N}(W)] = -\alpha + b\sp *_1(X_1) + b\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde g(\ol{X_1}\cap \ol{X_2}) - \widetilde f(X_1\cap X_2),
  \end{split}
\end{align*}
contradicting~\eqref{(gen1alfa)}.


\medskip


{\bf Case 4.} \ $ v\sp 1_0 \in W, v\sp 2_0 \notin W$.
Let $W\sp 1:=V\sp 1-W$ and $W_2:=V\sp 2\cap W$, and let
\[
  X_1 := \{ ( {i,j}) \in S_{m,n} : v\sp 1_{i,j} \in W\sp 1 \} \ \ \ \hbox{and}\ \ \ X_2 := \{ ( {i,j}) \in S_{m,n} : v\sp 2_{i,j} \in W\sp 2 \}.
\]
The contribution of the arcs in $A^1$ and $A^2$ to $\varrho_u(W) - \delta_l(W)$ are
\begin{align*}
    \begin{split}
        &\varrho _{u\vert A\sp 1}(W) - \delta _{l\vert A \sp 1}(W) = \widetilde u(A^1 \cap \Delta^{\text{out}}(W^1)) - \widetilde l(A^1 \cap \Delta^{\text{in}}(W^1)) = - p\sp *_1(X_1),\\
        &\varrho _{u\vert A\sp 2}(W) - \delta _{l\vert A\sp 2}(W) = \widetilde u(A^2 \cap \Delta^{\text{in}}(W^2)) - \widetilde l(A^2 \cap \Delta^{\text{out}}(W^2)) = -p\sp *_2(X_2).
    \end{split}
\end{align*}
The arcs in $N$ contribute 
\[
  \varrho _{u\vert N}(W) - \delta _{l\vert N}(W) = \widetilde g(X_1\cap X_2) - \widetilde f(\ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2}).
\]
In this case, the special arc $a_0$ enters $W$, thus its contribution to $\varrho_u(W)-\delta_l(W)$ is $\beta$.

Summing all contributions, we get that
\begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  0 &> \varrho _u(W) - \delta _l(W) = \beta + [\varrho _{u\vert A\sp 1}(W) - \delta _{l\vert A\sp 1}(W)] + [\varrho _{u\vert A\sp 2}(W) - \delta _{l\vert A\sp 2}(W)]\\
  &\quad+ [\varrho _{u\vert N}(W) - \delta _{l\vert N}(W)] = \beta - p\sp *_1(X_1) - p\sp *_2(X_2) + \widetilde g(X_1\cap X_2) - \widetilde f(\ol{X_1} \cap \ol{X_2}),
  \end{split}
\end{align*}
contradicting~\eqref{(gen1beta)}, which completes the proof of Theorem~\ref{general1}.
\FBOX

\medskip

We conclude by noting that a prefix-bounded matrix satisfying the prescribed entry- and total-sum bounds can be efficiently computed using standard network flow algorithms, as detailed in Section~\ref{arammodell}.
Furthermore, in the absence of such a matrix, the alternative proof of Theorem~\ref{general1} --- based on Hoffman's circulation theorem --- provides a method for identifying the subsets $X_1,X_2 \subseteq S_{m,n}$ that certify infeasibility, in accordance with Theorem~\ref{general1}.

