\section{Experiment Settings}
\label{appendix:setting}

For all baselines, we run their original implementations on our data for consistency. We report the average result of five runs. In our method, we adopt Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct \footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct}} in Dialogue Augmentation to conduct rephrasing and summarization. For dialogue encoding, we use GritLM-7B \cite{muennighoff2024generative} to generate dialogue embeddings because of its superior ability in retrieval tasks. Following previous works \cite{chen2019towards,zhou2020improving,wang2022towards}, we use a single-layer RGCN to encode the KG. The dialogue-guided attention network contains one layer of multi-head attention and feed-forward network. During dialogue augmentation, we randomly apply one of the techniques (including No Augmentation) in each stage with equal probability. During training, we first extract mentioned entities in each utterance. If an utterance contains at least one entity, it is regarded as a training sample. Its previous dialogue context as well as the previous mentioned entities are used as the input to our model, while the entities in this utterance are treated as the ground-truth targets. During testing, we only use system utterances that contain recommended items as test samples. The targets are strictly items instead of entities. We use batch size 128, AdamW optimizer with learning rate 0.001 and weight decay 0.01, trained with 50 epochs. We set the batch size as 128.
% This is an appendix.


\section{RQ3: Hyperparameter Study}
\label{appendix:hyperparameter}
\subsection{Loss Weight $\alpha$}
We show how $\alpha$ in \autoref{eq:final loss} influences the performance of DACRS in \autoref{fig:param}. As $\alpha$ increases from 0 to 0.5, the Recall@10 keeps increasing. After that, it remains relatively steady and slightly drops when $\alpha>1.5$. Therefore, $\alpha\in[0.5,1.5]$ is generally a good choice to achieve a satisfying performance.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{figs/param.pdf}
\vspace{-1em}
\caption{Parameter study of $\alpha$.}
\label{fig:param}
% \vspace{-1.5em}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Entity Substitution Rate}
In the Knowledge-Guided Entity Modeling module, we substitute mentioned entities with their 1-hop neighbors in the knowledge graph. We show how the substitution rate affects the model's performance in \autoref{fig:sub}. The best substitution rate lies between 0.1 and 0.4. We could observe a performance drop as the rate increases, suggesting that a large substitution rate could introduce noise to the modeling of user preference. 

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{figs/entity_sub_rate.pdf}
\vspace{-1em}
\caption{Parameter study of entity substitution rate.}
\label{fig:sub}
% \vspace{-1.5em}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Dialogue Augmentation Rate}
We have a set of augmentation techniques in Dialogue Augmentation stage 2. They share one dialogue augmentation rate that determines the proportion of words/utterances that would be augmented. We show how the augmentation rate affects the performance of DACRS in \autoref{fig:aug}. A proper augmentation rate between 0.1 and 0.4 could bring benefit to the performance of the model. However, if the augmentation rate further increases, the performance would drop. This is because a heavy augmentation would result in the loss of information, e.g., deleting too many words or utterances. Therefore, keeping the augmentation rate at below 0.4 is generally a good choice.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{figs/dialogue_aug_rate.pdf}
