\section{Conclusion}
Existing video TTA methods predominantly rely on visual information, neglecting the potential contribution of audio, which could offer valuable insights for enhancing TTA performance. In this work, we have introduced a novel audio-assisted video TTA framework that incorporates audio signals from videos to improve the generalization ability of video models. Specifically, we have proposed an LLM-based pseudo-label mapping method to address the challenge of aligning audio categories with the video label space, thereby facilitating audio-guided supervision. Additionally, we have introduced an adaptive strategy for determining the number of adaptation steps, which adjusts dynamically based on the data and the specific types of corruptions, optimizing the use of audio-assisted labels. Experimental results across four benchmark datasets have validated the effectiveness of our approach. Our findings provide a promising solution for integrating audio information in TTA contexts, enhancing the robustness of video models under diverse conditions.



