Recent technological advances have led to advanced parallel computing hardware and complex I/O workloads driven by artificial intelligence, data science, and high-resolution simulations. These advances have made the existing parallel I/O stack complex and challenging to tune, which if not optimized properly, can lead to massive overheads and performance degradation. With these ever-increasing complexities of the I/O stack deployed on large-scale systems, understanding how to effectively characterize, analyze, and optimize the I/O behavior of HPC applications is more critical than ever. However, with the vast array of tools and techniques available to the HPC community for this purpose, navigating the best approach can be a challenging task. By harnessing the knowledge within the I/O community, this paper surveys more than $130$ papers from the ACM Digital Library, IEEE Xplore, and other reputable journals to provide a comprehensive analysis, synthesized in the form of a taxonomy, of the current landscape of parallel I/O characterization and optimization of large-scale HPC systems. We anticipate that this taxonomy will serve as a valuable resource to the end users in enhancing the I/O performance of their HPC applications. 
