This subsection explains the fundamentals of generic receiver modeling needed for understanding the later presented channel models for analyzing the exhaled breath- 
For the generic receiver modeling, we focus on a well-known three-dimensional system model following~\cref{fig:model}, i.e., a point transmitter and a spherical receiver. 
%For a more detailed overview, the reader is referred to~\cite{jamaliChannelModelingDiffusive2019}.

\subsubsection{Passive Receiver}

For a point transmitter and a passive, i.e., observing, receiver in a three-dimensional medium, the mean fraction of received \acp{IC} until a time $t$, $F(t)$, follows~\cite{parcerisa2009molecular}:
\begin{equation}
    F(t) = V_s \frac{1}{4 \pi D (r_r + d)} \text{erfc} \Big[ \frac{r_r + d}{\sqrt{4Dt}} \Big],
\end{equation}
whereby $V_s$, $D$, $r_r$, and $d$ denote the spherical receiver volume, the diffusion coefficient, the radius of the spherical receiver, and the distance from the transmitter to the surface of the receiver, respectively. 

\subsubsection{Active Receiver}

For a point transmitter and an active, i.e., perfectly absorbing, receiver in a three-dimensional medium, $F(t)$ follows~\cite{yilmazChannelCharacteristicsAbsorbingReciver}:
\begin{equation}
    F(t) = \frac{r_r}{r_r + d} \text{erfc} \Big[ \frac{d}{\sqrt{4Dt}} \Big].
\end{equation}

