Here, too, it was found that temperature could be used as a marker for asthma. 
Further studies~\cite{paredi2003exhaled,klokstad2010effect,lazar2014exhaled} have demonstrated that \ac{COPD} patients exhibit lower \ac{EBT} compared to healthy subjects, potentially due to structural airway damage and impaired blood flow~\cite{klokstad2010effect}, with increased \ac{EBT} observed during acute exacerbations~\cite{lazar2014exhaled}.
Studies by Carpagnano \emph{et al.}~\cite{carpagnano2014exhaled,carpagnano2015exhaled} demonstrated significantly higher \ac{EBT} in patients with non-small-cell lung cancer compared to control subjects, correlating with pack-years and cancer stage, indicating \ac{EBT} as a metric for lung cancer detection.
Popov, Kralimarkovea, and DuBuske~\cite{popov2016relationship} investigated triggering exacerbations in chronic lung diseases due to viral and bacterial infections and their impact on the behavior of the \ac{EBT}. 
The study~\cite{popov2016relationship} involving six healthy subjects found that \ac{EBT} increased significantly before ear temperature during viral infections, such as rhinovirus and influenza, with \ac{EBT} rising earlier than ear temperature by up to several hours. 
The study~\cite{popov2016relationship} suggests that \ac{EBT} could serve as an early indicator of infections, potentially offering a window for prompt intervention, especially in patients at risk of exacerbating obstructive airway diseases.+
\end{comment}

\textbf{Humidity:} Breath humidity reflects respiratory tract hydration, mucus production, and fluid balance~\cite{turcios2020cystic}, aiding in detecting conditions like cystic fibrosis, chronic bronchitis, and heart failure. 
However, it plays a minor role in disease diagnosis. Instead, humidity can affect detection reliability by acting as a cross-reactive parameter~\cite{dixit2021exhaled,lagopati2023role}, potentially interfering with \acp{VOC} analysis in diabetes monitoring~\cite{dixit2021exhaled}.

\paragraph{Spatial Origins}
The spatial origin of exhaled breath (nasal or oral) impacts both oral and systemic health. 
%Nasal breathing filters and warms air, offering pathogen protection, while oral breathing is linked to dental issues and infections.\sub{Are there any references to these claims?} \ph{There are references for the function of the nose/mouth in general. However, I think it is hard to find specific references here. I will exclude (comment) this sentence.} \sub{Sure, you can take a printout of this version to hand it over for proofreading. Meanwhile, I will check if I find any references to these points. We can add them later}
Differentiating between the spatial origin, i.e., nasal or oral breathing, involves analyzing \acp{VOC}, temperature, breathing patterns, and acoustic signals. 
\Ac{VOC} concentration and composition varies based on the exhalation route~\cite{sukul2017oral}.
Utilizing thermography can differentiate between nasal and oral breathing by monitoring the temperature changes in the two facial regions~\cite{huang2021nose}. 
Apart from the surveyed sensors in~\cref{subsec:detection_technology}, acoustic sensors can also help distinguish between nasal and mouth breathing by analyzing breath sounds.~\cite{curran2012using} demonstrated that the spatial origins can be distinguished using acoustic sensors placed on specific body locations, followed by signal processing.

\paragraph{Flow Rate}

A wide range of sensors can detect disease based on the dynamics of breath flow, specifically breath rate, such as temperature, pressure, strain, acoustic, or optical sensors~\cite{yu2017wrinkled}.
%Analyzing the breath rate can be used for health monitoring as it indicates diseases such as sleep apnea, asthma, cardiac arrest, or lung cancer if the breathing behavior changes abruptly~\cite{tai2020evolution,yu2017wrinkled,khan2016monitoring}.
Distinct patterns in the respiratory rate and rhythm in the exhaled breath can indicate specific respiratory conditions, for example, associated with disorders such as Biot's respiration, tachypnea, bradypnea, and Cheyne-Stokes respiration~\cite{whited2023abnormal,buaruk2023respiratory}. 
Analyzing breath patterns may also help diagnose neurological diseases, such as Parkinson's disease through nocturnal patterns~\cite{yang2022artificial} or Alzheimer's disease through changes in breath rate~\cite{bjerkan2024neurovascular}.

