\begin{abstract} \label{sec:abstract}
\vspace{-5pt}
% Speech Foundation Models (SFMs) face challenges when deployed in real-world environments with acoustic domain shifts, such as background noise or speaker accents. Test-Time Adaptation (TTA) has emerged as an effective solution. However, existing TTA techniques, especially backpropagation-based (BP) methods, are computationally expensive and require substantial memory, limiting their practical use in efficient speech applications or on resource-constrained environments. While backpropagation-free (BP-free) methods offer a more efficient alternative, they often sacrifice adaptation accuracy. Furthermore, these methods are primarily designed for Vision Foundation Models (VFMs), which differ in task characteristics, noise patterns, and model structures compared to SFMs, presenting unique challenges for adaption.
%\ag{Can we remove the author stub, please? Do we need it for the formatting? It'll save some space.}\jd{I am not sure, since this "anonymous authors" is in the .sty file. It might be a standard format? I will double check with Hong.}
Speech Foundation Models %(SFMs)
encounter significant performance degradation when deployed in real-world scenarios involving acoustic domain shifts, such as background noise and speaker accents. Test-time adaptation (TTA) has recently emerged as a viable strategy to address such domain shifts at inference time without requiring access to source data or labels. However, existing TTA approaches, particularly those relying on backpropagation, are memory-intensive, limiting their applicability in speech tasks and resource-constrained settings. Although backpropagation-free methods offer improved efficiency, existing ones exhibit poor accuracy. This is because they are predominantly developed for vision tasks, which fundamentally differ from speech task formulations, noise characteristics, and model architecture, posing unique transferability challenges.
In this paper, we introduce \sysname{}, the \emph{first} \underline{E}fficient \underline{BA}ckpropagation-free \underline{T}TA framework designed explicitly for speech foundation models. \sysname{} achieves a balance between adaptation effectiveness and memory efficiency through three key components: (i) lightweight prompt adaptation for a forward-pass-based feature alignment, (ii) a multi-scale loss to capture both global (utterance-level) and local distribution shifts (token-level) and (iii) a test-time exponential moving average %(T-EMA) 
mechanism for stable adaptation across utterances. Experiments conducted on four noisy speech datasets spanning sixteen acoustic conditions demonstrate consistent improvements, with $4.1\%$--$13.5\%$ accuracy gains over backpropagation-free baselines and 2.0$\times$–6.4$\times$ GPU memory savings compared to backpropagation-based methods. By enabling scalable and robust adaptation under acoustic variability, this work paves the way for developing more efficient adaptation approaches for practical speech processing systems in real-world environments.
