The definitions of PP and PW are rather straightforward in a single-threaded executor as there is only one thread updating ReadySet. However, in a multi-threaded executor (implemented in the \texttt{rclcpp} package of ROS 2 Galactic and newer versions to date), the ReadySet is shared among all threads.
Hence, one or more threads might become idle (i.e., ReadySet is empty or has no eligible callback to execute) and update ReadySet, while other threads are still executing their callbacks from the previous version of ReadySet. Those who were executing callbacks when ReadySet was updated cannot even notice such an update.   
Therefore, we need to revise the definitions of PP and PW for multi-threaded executors:
%However, in a multi-threaded executor, since all threads share the same \textit{ReadySet}, one or more threads might become idle (i.e., \textit{ReadySet} is empty or has no eligible callback to execute) while other threads are still executing their callbacks. This means, while one or more threads intend to start a new PW, there might exist some threads that are still in their previous PWs. Therefore, we cannot use the previous definitions of the polling point and processing window and we need to redefine them.

%By reviewing the \textit{rclcpp} libraries on ROS 2 (used in Eloquent and newer versions), a ROS 2 multi-threaded executor shares the \textit{ReadySet} among all threads. Each thread gets the next ready executable from the \textit{ReadySet} and executes it. Once a thread sees there is no ready executable on \textit{ReadySet}, it goes into \textit{wait-for-work} mode, and it fetches new executables from underlying layers to the \textit{ReadySet}. Note that there might be multiple idle threads that go into the \textit{wait-for-work} mode simultaneously, but they are treated sequentially in regard to accessing the underlying layers. So, each of them fetches new executables to the \textit{ReadySet} if there exist any. It happens while other thread(s) might be executing their previous executable and when they’re done, the ReadySet\textit{} might be already filled, and they don’t even notice an empty \textit{ReadySet}. Therefore, we redefine the polling point (updating the \textit{ReadySet}) as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Polling Point (PP)}: A time point when \textit{at least one thread} becomes idle.
    \item \textbf{Processing Window (PW)}: The time interval between two consecutive PPs, \textit{regardless of which thread triggered the new PP}. In the $n^{th}$ PW, $pw_n$, there might be some threads that are still executing callbacks whose start times were in previous PWs, $pw_{n-p}$. These callbacks are considered {\it carry-in} callbacks for $pw_n$. Also, some callbacks that started  in $pw_n$ may continue to execute in $pw_{n+q}$. Such callbacks are considered as {\it carry-out} callbacks for $pw_{n}$.%\hyoseung{Add the example figure in our slides if space allows}
