According to the NP-FP global task scheduling analysis~\cite{lee17improved}, to obtain the worst-case response time of a given non-preemptive task $\tau_i$, we need to find the latest time that a $\tau_i$'s job starts its first unit of execution. Assuming this first unit finishes after a time interval $\Delta$ from the release of the job, the response time of $\tau_i$ is $\Delta +$ $\overline{sbf}_k(E_i -1$) where $E_i$ is the WCET of $\tau_i$ and $\overline{sbf}_k(E_i -1)$ is the minimal time interval required by any processor (worker thread $r_k$) to execute $E_i -1$. To find $\Delta$, there should be enough resource supply for the system's demand. 
%The following lemma shows the relation between the \textit{demand-bound function} ($dbf(\Delta)$) and the \textit{supply-bound function} ($sbf(\Delta)$) in the time interval $\Delta$.
For at least one unit of workload of $\tau_i$ to execute in $\Delta$, the following inequality has to hold: 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:dbf_sbf}
dbf(\Delta) < sbf_\Pi(\Delta)    
\end{equation}
where $dbf(\Delta)$ and $sbf_\Pi(\Delta)$ stand for the demand-bound function and the supply-bound function, respectively. Note that $sbf_\Pi(\Delta)=m\cdot\Delta$ is the maximum resource supply in a $m$-processor system. 
