\begin{proof}
Eq.~\eqref{eq:PWA_CD} captures all possible workloads in $[t,t+\Delta)$,  where $t$ is the release time of an instance of $\Gamma_\mathcal{C}$, until the last callback of $\Gamma_\mathcal{C}$ can start its first unit of execution. 
By Lemma~\ref{lm:block}, we know that the maximum workload caused by precedence dependencies is $m \cdot (E_\mathcal{C}- E_{\langle \mathcal{C}, \|\Gamma_\mathcal{C}\| \rangle})$. In addition, the maximum workload from the instances of other chains is upper-bounded by the sum of $W_x$ given by Lemma~\ref{lm:chain_workload}. As there is no other source of workloads in $\Delta$, the last callback of $\Gamma_\mathcal{C}$ can start at least one unit of its execution in $\Delta$ if $dbf(\Delta)<sbf_\Pi(\Delta)$ holds, and the response time of $\Gamma_\mathcal{C}$ is bounded by $\Delta+$ $\overline{sbf}_k(E_{\langle \mathcal{C}, \|\Gamma_\mathcal{C}\| \rangle}-1)$.
% Since callbacks are non-preemptive, when a callback starts its execution, it will be executed non-stop. So if we find the first unit that a callback can be scheduled, we can find its response time.
% For a chain $\Gamma_\mathcal{C}$, if we can find a time interval $\Delta$ in which all callbacks of the chain except the last one are executed and we can find at least one unit in time interval $\Delta$ to start the execution of the last callback, we can find the response-time of the chain by $\Delta+E_{\langle \mathcal{C}, \|\Gamma_\mathcal{C}\| \rangle}-1$. To find at least one unit in time interval $\Delta$ to start the execution of a callback, Eq. ~\ref{eq:dbf_sbf} should hold.
%
% To find the $dbf(\Delta)$, we need to find (i) the interference from the chain and its instances plus (ii) the interference from other chains' instances. For (i), since we have constrained-deadline chains, there is no self-interference from other instances of the given chain. However, since our callbacks in a chain have precedence dependencies, according to the ~\ref{lm:block}, we need to make sure that there is enough resource to execute them sequentially. So, to execute the whole chain excluding the remaining part of the last callback (which can be executed after the time interval $\Delta$), we need to reserve $m \cdot (E_\mathcal{C}- (E_{\langle \mathcal{C}, \|\Gamma_\mathcal{C}\| \rangle}-1))$ units.
%
% For (ii), we need to upper-bound the interference from other chains' instances. We find the workload of all interfering periodic chains with the workload function in Lemma.~\ref{lm:chain_workload} where the maximum carry-in workload happens when $\alpha_x = D_x-E_x$ for all $\Gamma_x \in \Gamma$. $D_x$ and $E_x$ are the deadline and the execution time of the chain $\Gamma_x$, respectively.
% % In Lemma.~\ref{lm:LeSh}, as described before, $\alpha_x = R_x-C_x$ is used to capture the carry-in which can be re-written as $\alpha_x = (D_x-slack_x)-C_x$ where $slack_x$ is the slack time of the chain $\Gamma_x$. In the worst-case scenario, the slack time is $slack_x =0$. Therefore, the maximum carry-in happens when $\alpha_x = D_x-C_x$.
\end{proof}

