    % the equal-priority callback instances too because the first callback of a chain can be blocked by its previous instances which are the only instances that have equal priority.
    % Since we limited the blocking time of lower-priority callbacks, we use the workload function in Theorem. ~\ref{lm:chain_workload_AD} to find the interference from chains with higher-priority callbacks. However, since we have arbitrary deadlines, the instances of the chain itself can interfere in time interval $\Delta$ too. Therefore, we include the chain itself in the $\sum_{\forall \Gamma_x \in \Gamma \land \pi_x \leq \pi_\mathcal{C}} W_x(\Delta, D_x - C_x)$ term.

    % Since our callbacks in a chain have precedence dependencies, we need to make sure that there is enough resource to execute them sequentially. So, to execute the whole chain excluding the remaining part of the last callback (which can be executed after the time interval $\Delta$), we need to follow lemma.~\ref{lm:chain_block} which suggests to add $m \cdot (E_\mathcal{C}- (E_\mathcal{\|C\|}-1))$ to the $dbf(\Delta)$. However, we count all instances of the chain of interest in $\sum_{\forall \Gamma_x \in \Gamma \land \pi_x \leq \pi_\mathcal{C}} W_x(\Delta, D_x - C_x)$ including the one instance of the interest. So, we can subtract one $E_\mathcal{C}$ since we already considered it in $m \cdot (E_\mathcal{C}- (E_\mathcal{\|C\|}-1))$ to the $dbf(\Delta)$. Also, For the instance of interest that we intend to find the response time, we exclude $E_\mathcal{\|C\|}-1$ from $dbf(\Delta)$, because we know if we find a unit to execute the first unit of the last callback, it executes non-stop after $\Delta$ due to non-preemption. $m \cdot (E_\mathcal{C}- (E_\mathcal{\|C\|}-1))$ to the $dbf(\Delta) - E_\mathcal{C} -(E_\mathcal{\|C\|}-1)$ leads to $(m-1) \cdot (E_\mathcal{C}- (E_\mathcal{\|C\|}-1))$

