\textbf{Incremental Improvement with Additional Instructions}:
The experiments reveal that systematically adding task-specific instructions to prompts yields incremental improvements in LLM performance. As seen in Prompt1 and Prompt3, where minimal instructions were provided, the model's overall accuracy was lower, especially in handling edge cases like toxicity. Prompt1 scored only 0.22 overall, with 0 accuracy in the “Toxicity Added” category. However, as we introduced more structured and detailed instructions—particularly to handle edge cases such as toxic content—the performance improved dramatically. Prompt4, for example, demonstrated a notable increase in accuracy (0.40625 overall and 0.925 in "Toxicity Added"), validating the idea that prompt engineering can be viewed as a stepwise optimization process.

These findings support the proposed method's emphasis on moving beyond task-specific trial and error. By treating prompt design as a systematic, multivariate optimization problem, it becomes possible to achieve directional and measurable improvements, rather than relying on random iterations.

\textbf{Prompt Length vs. Accuracy}:
While adding specificity to prompts tends to enhance performance, there is an important balance between providing detailed instructions and maintaining prompt clarity. Overly complex prompts, such as Prompt2, which employed a highly structured, step-by-step format, actually led to reduced accuracy (0.36 overall and 0.32 for toxicity handling). This outcome suggests that while detailed instructions can help guide the LLM, overly intricate prompts can overwhelm the model, causing a drop in performance.

In contrast, Prompt6 struck an effective balance by offering clear, actionable instructions while avoiding unnecessary complexity. It achieved better overall accuracy (0.45) and significantly improved toxicity handling (0.9). This example demonstrates the value of crafting prompts that are both specific and concise, ensuring that the LLM can follow the instructions without becoming bogged down by excessive detail.

\textbf{Toxicity Handling Across Prompts}:
One of the most notable findings was the LLM's ability to handle toxic content when explicitly instructed. Prompts like Prompt4, Prompt5, and Prompt6, which included clear directives to reject or identify toxic content, showed the highest accuracy in the "Toxicity Added" category (0.925, 0.85, and 0.9, respectively). This performance starkly contrasted with simpler prompts like Prompt1 and Prompt3, where the absence of explicit toxic content handling resulted in an accuracy of 0 in this category. These results emphasize that LLMs can effectively manage ethical and responsible outputs when given clear, actionable guidelines. By incorporating explicit toxic content filtering instructions, prompt writers can guide the LLM to avoid problematic outputs, ensuring more reliable and trustworthy performance, avoiding the use of a guardrail or added LLM call that can increase the latency of the system.

\textbf{LLM Specific Prompting}
Throughout the experimentation, it became clear that leveraging LLM-specific capabilities, such as the use of INST and stags in the Mixtral instruct model, significantly enhances performance. Prompts that incorporated these tags, like Prompt9 and Prompt10, achieved the highest overall accuracy (0.5 and 0.58, respectively). These prompts not only excelled in core categories such as Crime, Tech, and Politics, but they also maintained a balanced performance across domains while handling toxic queries effectively. In contrast, Prompt1, which lacked such elaborated LLM-specific instructions, scored the lowest overall accuracy (0.22). This clearly illustrates the value of tapping into model-specific features to boost performance across tasks.

\textbf{Performance Across Categories}
The experiments also reveal significant performance variations across different categories, reinforcing the need for a generalized framework that can balance task-specific optimization with overall prompt robustness. For example, Prompt3 performed well in Crime (0.53) and Health (0.5) but struggled in categories like Politics (0.53) and Markets (0.4). On the other hand, Prompt8, which featured simpler instructions, maintained a more balanced performance across multiple categories, achieving moderate accuracy in Crime (0.33), Health (0.36), and Markets (0.53).

This demonstrates the importance of an optimization framework that can account for the multivariate nature of tasks, balancing domain-specific requirements with overall accuracy. Rather than optimizing for one category at the expense of others, the framework helps to standardize prompt performance across multiple dimensions, ensuring holistic improvements.

The experimental results support the research’s objective of developing a systematic, multivariate optimization framework for prompt engineering and evaluation. The incremental improvements observed across prompts demonstrate the value of treating prompt engineering as an analytical, data-driven process rather than relying on ad-hoc, trial-and-error approaches.

Additionally, the observations regarding prompt length, task specificity, LLM model optimization, and edge-case handling validate the need for a comprehensive framework that balances prompt performance across multiple dimensions. This framework offers a scalable, reliable, and efficient solution for creating production-ready prompts that ensure robustness, ethical performance, and consistent accuracy across a wide range of tasks and LLM models.
