\subsection{Score Advantage Comparison with GBS}
The score advantage is defined as the ratio of the maximum Hafnian/density value acquired by random search enhanced by the Glauber dynamics to that acquired by the original random search at the end of the stage with 100 iterations. We remark that too many iterations may result in collapse of score advantage to 1 since even the original approach can acquire the same optimal solution as the variants by the Glauber dynamics. We plot our experimental results enhanced by \algo{glauber} in \fig{2}.

Ref.~\cite{PhysRevLett.130.190601} used a 144-vertex complete graph with random complex weights, whereas our numerical experiment is conducted on the aforementioned 256-vertex Erd\"{o}s-Rényi graph $G_3$ with $p=0.4$. Since all weights of the edges in the graph are real, we do not need the square of modulus. In addition, the output probability of a Gaussian Boson sampler is proportional to the square of the Hafnian while our approach has a linear dependence in Hafnian. These may explain the difference between our score advantage and~\cite{PhysRevLett.130.190601}. As shown in the left side of \fig{2}, our Hafnian score advantage up to 4x is lower than the GBS experiments~\cite{PhysRevLett.130.190601}, whereas in the right side of \fig{2} our density score advantage is comparable to theirs.

