\subsection{Task Performance Comparison}
\label{subsec:task_performance}
Figs. \ref{fig:performance_overview} and \ref{fig:score_trends} (details in Appendix \ref{app:detailed_scores}, Table \ref{tab:detailed_scores}) show significant performance variability by LLM and task. No single LLM consistently outperformed others across all tasks, highlighting that the challenges of decentralized coordination are highly task-dependent. \texttt{Flocking} was generally highest-scoring, while \texttt{Synchronization} showed more divergence. For instance, \texttt{gemini-2.0-flash} and \texttt{o4-mini} were strong in spatial tasks (\texttt{Pursuit}, \texttt{Foraging}), while \texttt{claude-3.7-sonnet} excelled in \texttt{Synchronization}. This underscores that the effectiveness of LLM coordination under swarm-like constraints is highly task-dependent and relies on the successful emergence of appropriate strategies from purely local information.




\subsection{Analysis of Emergent Group Dynamics and Communication Correlates}
\label{subsec:group_dynamics_comm_results}
Analysis of emergent group dynamics (metrics in Appendix~\ref{app:group_dynamics_metrics}, task-specific visualizations in Appendix~\ref{app:task_specific_dynamics_viz}) revealed that physical group dynamics (e.g., behavioral variability, movement efficiency, joint actions) strongly indicated task success. In contrast, features derived directly from the content of explicit communication (e.g., coordinate sharing, message homogeneity) showed weaker, though occasionally statistically significant, correlations with task success. This suggests that, under SwarmBench's constraints, effective coordination primarily emerges implicitly from agents observing each other's actions and the shared environment, rather than relying heavily on the explicit content of their limited broadcasts. Section~\ref{subsec:action_attribution} further explores the influence of observations vs. messages on decisions.

\subsection{Analysis of Failure Modes}
\label{subsec:failure_modes_analysis}
Common failure modes, identified via qualitative observation and correlation analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:task_specific_dynamics_viz}, Appendix~\ref{app:detailed_scores}), included repetitive, suboptimal behaviors (e.g., low Avg. Directional Entropy), insufficient coordinated actions (e.g., low Avg. Agent Push Events in \texttt{Pursuit}, \texttt{Transport}), or inefficient exploration. Figure~\ref{fig:failed_mode} visually exemplifies several such patterns observed in SwarmBench.

For instance, {``Movement Bias''} (Fig.~\ref{fig:failed_mode}a), observed with the \texttt{claude-3.5-haiku} model, showcases agents exhibiting a strong, potentially suboptimal, directional preference (indicated by the blue dashed arrow towards the bottom-right of its panel). This can lead them to neglect global task objectives, such as reaching entity \texttt{P} located elsewhere.
Another prevalent issue is the formation of an {``Information Silo''} (Fig.~\ref{fig:failed_mode}b). Here, a subgroup of agents, exemplified by \texttt{gemini-2.0-flash} (highlighted within the dashed blue box), may become locally clustered and interactive but fails to effectively coordinate or share vital information with the broader swarm, hindering overall group performance.
{``Traffic Jams''} (Fig.~\ref{fig:failed_mode}c) were also common, particularly with models like \texttt{claude-3.7-sonnet}. In this scenario, agents tend to over-aggregate in confined areas (demarcated by the dashed blue circle), leading to severe congestion. This not only obstructs individual movement but also impedes access to critical resources like \texttt{F}ood sources or \texttt{N}est locations.
Finally, a phenomenon aptly termed {``Memory of a goldfish''} was observed in some instances (Fig.~\ref{fig:failed_mode}d), exemplified here with agents operating under the \texttt{gemini-2.0-flash} model. In such cases, agents appeared to exhibit challenges with long-term spatial recall. This could be linked to the system's constrained memory buffer, which defaults to the last 5 frames of observation. When agents remain confined to a specific map area for an extended period, crucial information about distant locations, like the nest cluster, might fall outside this limited memory window. Consequently, this can lead to situations where previously known positions, such as that of the nest, seem to be ``forgotten,'' necessitating subsequent ``re-exploration'' or ``rediscovery'' phases. Such occurrences can hinder consistent progress towards strategically important goals that have not been recently observed.

These failure modes highlight challenges in robust planning and decision-making under the uncertainty imposed by limited local views, which often hampered the translation of potentially useful communicated information into effective collective action.



