\subsection{Action Attribution Analysis}
\label{subsec:action_attribution}
To quantify the influence of different information sources, a Random Forest classifier \citep{breiman2001random} was trained to predict an agent's action. Input features were Sentence-BERT \citep{reimers2019sentencebertsentenceembeddingsusing} embeddings derived from the agent's local observation (its $k \times k$ view) and the messages it received from other agents in the preceding round. We then used permutation importance (F1 score drop) to assess the relative influence of these two feature categories (observation vs. message).
The results, aggregated by task and visualized in Fig.~\ref{fig:action_attribution_wrapped} (with detailed scores in Appendix~\ref{app:action_attribution}), revealed that across several tasks, received messages often exhibited higher permutation importance than direct visual observations for predicting agent actions.
This suggests that communicated information, even when its source is anonymous, significantly influences immediate agent choices.
However, it is important to note that while messages demonstrably influence local, individual decision-making, this does not inherently translate to optimal global task outcomes in these zero-shot settings. As discussed further in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}, the strategic utility of this communication for achieving effective collective coordination appears more nuanced. The strong local influence of messages contrasts with the findings in Section~\ref{subsec:group_dynamics_comm_results}, where explicit communication features (derived from message content) showed weaker correlations with overall task success compared to physical dynamic metrics. This potentially highlights a crucial distinction: while communication has an immediate tactical impact on individual actions, its broader strategic value for achieving emergent group-level performance appears limited under SwarmBench's strict decentralized constraints in these zero-shot settings.



\subsection{Impact of Agent Density and Perception Range}
\label{subsec:sensitivity_main_text}


\begin{figure}[t!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1.0\linewidth]{figs/figS5_sensitivity_analysis_plot.pdf} % Or a new, more focused figure
    \caption{\textbf{Selected Parameter Sensitivity Analysis Results.} Score variation with agent number ($N$) and field of view ($k \times k$) for key tasks. This figure is a condensed representation of findings detailed in Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity_analysis}. Transport task use the case where the obstacle is a solid square (rather than the more difficult irregular obstacle).}
    \label{fig:sensitivity_analysis}
\end{figure}


To understand the robustness of LLM-driven swarm coordination, we investigated the impact of two fundamental swarm parameters: agent density ($N$, the number of agents) and local perception range ($k \times k$ view). Detailed methodologies and full results are presented in Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity_analysis}. 

Our findings reveal that these parameters significantly influence task performance, often in a task-dependent manner. For instance, increasing the perception range from $k=3$ to $k=5$ generally improved outcomes across tasks such as \textcolor{colorPursuit}{\texttt{Pursuit}}, \textcolor{colorSynchronization}{\texttt{Synchronization}}, \textcolor{colorForaging}{\texttt{Foraging}}, and \textcolor{colorFlocking}{\texttt{Flocking}}, suggesting that enhanced local awareness is beneficial for coordination. However, further expansion to $k=7$ offered diminishing returns and, in some cases like the \textcolor{colorTransport}{\texttt{Transport}} task, led to a slight performance decrease compared to $k=5$. This suggests a potential trade-off: while more information can theoretically be beneficial, an overly broad view (e.g., $k=7$) might increase the complexity of the LLM's reasoning task. Processing a larger input prompt could make it more challenging for the LLM to discern critical, immediate local cues amidst a greater volume of potentially less relevant or noisy information. Consequently, this could dilute the agent's focus on environmental features or neighboring agent states most pertinent for tightly coupled maneuvers, such as the precise alignment required in the \textcolor{colorTransport}{\texttt{Transport}} task. This phenomenon, where increased perceptual input does not monotonically improve, or even degrades, performance in these zero-shot settings, is further discussed in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}.





The influence of agent numbers ($N$) also varied. The \textcolor{colorTransport}{\texttt{Transport}} task, which inherently relies on cumulative force, benefited from a larger group ($N=16$ outperformed $N=8$). Conversely, \textcolor{colorForaging}{\texttt{Foraging}} performance degraded with increased $N$, likely due to congestion and interference. Intriguingly, \textcolor{colorPursuit}{\texttt{Pursuit}} showed peak performance at an intermediate group size ($N=12$), while the \textcolor{colorSynchronization}{\texttt{Synchronization}} task shows the opposite pattern. These diverse scaling behaviors underscore the challenge of maintaining effective decentralized coordination as group size and information availability change, highlighting the need for adaptive strategies in LLM-based swarms. Figure~\ref{fig:sensitivity_analysis} provides a visual summary of these trends for selected tasks.








\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
SwarmBench evaluations reveal that the success of LLM-driven swarms under strict decentralization hinges primarily on emergent physical coordination. Metrics capturing physical group dynamics—such as behavioral variability (Avg. Directional Entropy), movement efficiency (Avg. Moving Distance), and joint actions (Avg. Agent Push Events; see Appendices \ref{app:group_dynamics_metrics}, \ref{app:task_specific_dynamics_viz})—consistently showed stronger correlations with task outcomes. In contrast, features derived from the explicit semantic content of inter-agent messages (e.g., Avg. Proportion of Digit Characters, Avg. Info Homogeneity) exhibited weaker correlations. This suggests that, under SwarmBench's constraints, LLMs predominantly achieve implicit coordination by observing the environment and the actions of others, a mechanism reminiscent of natural swarms \citep{bonabeau1999swarm_Swarm_intelligence_From_natural_to_artificial_systems}.

While action attribution analysis (Sec. \ref{subsec:action_attribution}) indicated that received messages significantly influence an agent's immediate local decision, this local impact did not readily translate into superior global task success in these zero-shot settings. Metrics reflecting explicit message content (Sec. \ref{subsec:group_dynamics_comm_results}) were weaker predictors of overall performance. This highlights a nuanced challenge: while LLM-generated messages are locally influential on an agent's immediate decision, they appear to lack the strategic depth or shared understanding required to consistently formulate and execute effective group-level strategies under severe decentralization in these zero-shot settings. Effective coordination seems more robustly determined by emergent physical interaction patterns, possibly subtly influenced by basic signaling or agent presence, but whose impact is more directly captured by physical dynamic metrics. Furthermore, the observed sensitivity to agent density ($N$) and perception range ($k$) (Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity_analysis}) underscores that achieving robust LLM swarm intelligence will necessitate strategies that can adapt to varying group dynamics and information availability.

Although SwarmBench operates within an abstracted 2D grid, its unique value lies in unifying LLM-driven agency with \textit{local} spatial perception, language-based and action-based interactions, and emergent collective abilities. This integrated perspective positions SwarmBench as a valuable theoretical simulation tool. It can serve as a platform to explore and anticipate complex emergent phenomena that might arise in future scenarios involving widespread deployment of embodied, intelligent agents, thereby potentially aiding in the anticipation and responsible navigation of implications arising from future advanced decentralized AI systems.


\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
We introduced SwarmBench, a novel benchmark specifically designed for assessing emergent decentralized coordination in Large Language Models when operating under classical swarm intelligence constraints. Our comprehensive evaluations demonstrate that while contemporary LLMs exhibit nascent capabilities for coordination, they generally struggle to achieve the sophisticated and robust collective behaviors characteristic of natural swarms, particularly when faced with strict local perception and communication limits. This highlights a significant gap and a critical avenue for future research in multi-agent AI.

SwarmBench provides a systematic platform to measure progress towards developing LLMs capable of more adaptive and genuinely collective behavior, which is vital for their application in complex, real-world decentralized systems. While the current 2D grid world, built upon a customizable physical engine (Appendix~\ref{app:physics_details}) and aligned with foundational AI benchmarks \citep{chollet2025arcprize, snake_bench_2025, ruoss2025lmact_google_deepmind}, necessarily abstracts from 3D complexities, and performance can vary with parameters like agent count ($N$) and view range ($k$) (Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity_analysis}), these design choices establish SwarmBench as a valuable foundational tool for dissecting the core mechanisms of emergent coordination in LLM-based agent systems.

Future work will target several key directions. Enabling agent adaptation within SwarmBench, potentially via integration with Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) pipelines \citep{deepseekai2025deepseekr1incentivizingreasoningcapability, xin2024deepseekproverv15harnessingproofassistant, jin2025searchr1trainingllmsreason}, is a primary goal. We also plan to extend the benchmark itself, exploring 3D environments, richer physics models, and a wider array of coordination tasks. Deeper investigations into communication strategies (e.g., diverse protocols, learned messaging, broader NLP analysis), alongside the development of novel agent architectures and prompting techniques (see Appendix~\ref{app:prompt_design}), are crucial. Furthermore, SwarmBench invites exploration of fundamental, biologically-inspired questions: Can LLM swarms exhibit phenomena like locust consensus \citep{science2025_governing_swarming_locusts}, adaptively form multi-scale structures from local interactions \citep{Nature_khona2025global}, or achieve spontaneous functional modularity? Addressing these will advance our understanding of and ability to engineer collective intelligence in artificial systems.


