%This problem requires the vertices of the convex polygon from the input point sets. The trivial solution for $k=3$ is $\binom{n}{3}$, when the planar point set contains $n$ points. Katchalski and Meir~\cite{katchalski1988empty} studied the problem for empty triangles proving the existence of point sets admitting at most $\mathcal{O}(n^2)$ empty triangles. Aichholzer et al.~\cite{AichholzerMFHHU09} studied the problem of the bounds on the minimum number of empty monochromatic triangles in a bichromatic planar point set with $n$ points and proved the bound is $\Omega(n^{\frac{5}{4}})$. Later Pach and T{\'o}th~\cite{PachT13} improved the bound to $\Omega(n^{\frac{4}{3}})$. Further, Cravioto-Lagos~\cite{cravioto2019almost} addressed the bound on the number of \emph{almost} empty monochromatic triangles and convex quadrilaterals for multi-colored point sets. Finally, Aichholzer et al.~\cite{aichholzer2010mono4gons} could show that every sufficiently large bichromatic point set contains an empty monochromatic quadrilateral, however it is not necessarily convex. The existence of a monochromatic 4-hole, that is empty convex 4-gon in large bichromatic point sets is an open problem.

